window.CFII_SCRIPTS = {"cfii-i-a-the-learning-process":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"4-1":[{"f":"e41f0b40d09b0e9a.webp","w":471,"h":294,"cap":"Levels of Learning"},{"f":"c6479f7688ff9665.webp","w":471,"h":294,"cap":"Levels of Learning"}],"4-4":[{"f":"326c89057d7dd25f.webp","w":374,"h":223,"cap":"Levels of Learning"}],"6-1":[{"f":"fe50ab97560d0b63.webp","w":382,"h":208,"cap":"Memory & Forgetting"}]},"pts":{"task":"I.A LEARNING PROCESS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least TASK E, F, and G and one other task.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of the learning process by describing:","items":[{"t":"Learning theories.","sub":[]},{"t":"Characteristics of learning.","sub":[]},{"t":"Principles of learning.","sub":[]},{"t":"Levels of learning.","sub":[]},{"t":"Learning physical skills.","sub":[]},{"t":"Memory.","sub":[]},{"t":"Transfer of learning.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Develop knowledge of the learning process (Instructor ACS) and begin integrating it into instruction.","key":["HOTS / SBT","Laws of Learning","Domains & Levels of Learning","Characteristics of Learning"],"cards":[{"h":"Learning Theory","obj":"Explain the two major learning theories and how instructors combine them.","pts":["**Learning theory**: principles explaining how people acquire skills, knowledge, and attitudes; 2 major concepts: Behaviorism and Cognitive Theory.","**Behaviorism**: encourage progress with rewards (positive reinforcement rather than none or punishment); popularity has waned.","**Cognitive theory**: learning changes how a learner thinks, understands, feels. Branches: Information Processing Theory and Constructivism.","**Information processing**: brain selects, processes, stores, retrieves info and uses feedback. **Constructivism**: match new info to existing info, build connections.","Behavioral + Cognitive: use the best of each; measure behavioral outcomes while promoting cognitive learning."],"ce":""},{"h":"HOTS and SBT","obj":"Explain why higher order thinking skills matter and how SBT teaches them.","pts":["**HOTS** (also called ADM): training based on problems or scenarios.","A common thread in aviation accidents is the absence of HOTS.","Teach HOTS with problem-based instruction, authentic real-world problems, active/cooperative learning, and customized instruction.","**SBT** is the heart of HOTS: real-world scenarios address flight-training objectives.","Other than the first flight or two, the scenario should be planned/led by the learner."],"ce":""},{"h":"Characteristics of Learning (PRMA)","obj":"Describe the four characteristics of learning.","pts":["**Purposeful**: learners learn from any activity that furthers their goals.","**Result of experience**: learner can learn only from personal experiences; make them meaningful, varied, appropriate.","**Multifaceted**: verbal, conceptual, perceptual, emotional, and problem-solving elements all at once.","**Active process**: learners must react/respond outwardly, inwardly, emotionally, or intellectually; constantly engage them."],"ce":""},{"h":"Principles of Learning (REEPIR)","obj":"Explain each principle and how it shapes instruction.","pts":["**Readiness**: basic needs met; clear reason to learn. Communicate clear objectives; introduce topics in logical order.","**Exercise**: connections strengthen with practice, weaken without; best in real-world context.","**Effect**: satisfying outcomes are repeated; include elements that make the learner feel satisfaction.","**Primacy**: what is learned first creates a strong, almost unshakable impression.","**Intensity**: vivid, dramatic, exciting experiences teach more than routine ones.","**Recency**: things most recently learned are best remembered."],"ce":""},{"h":"Domains and Levels of Learning","obj":"Distinguish the three domains and the four levels of learning.","pts":["**Cognitive** (knowledge): ground school, textbooks; 6 levels, simplest to most complex.","**Affective** (attitude): feelings, values, motivations; not easy to measure; framework of 5 levels.","**Psychomotor** (physical skill): e.g. flying a precision approach, programming a GPS.","Levels: **Rote** (repeat without understanding), **Understanding** (insight), **Application** (apply what was learned), **Correlation**.","**Correlation** is the primary objective in aviation instruction, e.g. relating turn entries to lazy eights and chandelles."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"e41f0b40d09b0e9a.webp","w":471,"h":294,"cap":"Levels of Learning"},{"f":"c6479f7688ff9665.webp","w":471,"h":294,"cap":"Levels of Learning"},{"f":"326c89057d7dd25f.webp","w":374,"h":223,"cap":"Levels of Learning"}]},{"h":"Acquiring Skill Knowledge","obj":"Explain skill stages, plateaus, and types of practice.","pts":["Stages: **Cognitive** (memorize steps), **Associative** (practice stores skill; learner self-adjusts), **Automatic response**.","**Knowledge of results**: inform the learner of progress, good and bad.","**Learning plateaus** are normal and temporary; over-practice can bring one on.","Practice types: **Deliberate** (specific areas plus feedback), **Blocked** (same drill; short-term gains), **Random** (mixed skills; better retention)."],"ce":""},{"h":"Memory","obj":"Describe sensory, short-term, and long-term memory.","pts":["**Sensory**: scans stimuli by personal importance; dramatic or multi-sense input more likely to register; passed on within seconds.","**Short-term**: like a control tower; holds info about 30 seconds; repetition and chunking help.","**Recoding** (relating new info to existing knowledge) is when actual learning begins.","Short-term operations: **iconic** (visual), **acoustic** (sound), **working** memory.","**Long-term**: relatively permanent, unlimited; the more effective the coding, the easier the recall."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"fe50ab97560d0b63.webp","w":382,"h":208,"cap":"Memory & Forgetting"}]},{"h":"Forgetting and Retention","obj":"Explain why learners forget and how to promote retention.","pts":["Retrieval depends mainly on how often and how recently knowledge was used.","Forgetting: **Fading**, **Interference** (worst for poorly learned material), **Retrieval failure**, **Repression/Suppression**.","Retention: praise, association, favorable attitudes, all senses, meaningful repetition (3-4 repetitions = maximum effect), mnemonics.","Encourage short regularly spaced study; explain the disadvantages of cramming.","After training, continued practice is the only means to retain what was learned."],"ce":""},{"h":"Transfer of Learning","obj":"Explain positive vs negative transfer and how to achieve positive transfer.","pts":["Transfer: applying what was learned in one context to new contexts; primary objective is **positive transfer**.","**Positive**: skill A helps skill B (slow flight and short-field landings). **Negative**: A hinders B (airplane vs helicopter landing).","Plan for transfer, show it applies elsewhere, keep high-order standards, avoid unnecessary rote learning.","**Habit formation**: insist on correct techniques. Follow the **building block** concept."],"ce":""}],"recall":[["What are the two major branches of cognitive theory?","Information Processing Theory\nConstructivism"],["Name the principles of learning (REEPIR).","Readiness, Exercise, Effect, Primacy, Intensity, Recency"],["Which principle explains why the learner must do it right the first time?","Primacy – what is learned first creates a strong, almost unshakable impression."],["What are the four levels of learning, and which is the primary objective?","Rote, Understanding, Application, Correlation\nCorrelation is the primary objective in aviation instruction."],["Which type of practice leads to better retention?","Random practice – mixes up the skills throughout the session."],["How long does short-term memory hold information, and how is that limit overcome?","About 30 seconds\nRepetition and sorting into chunks"],["Name the theories of forgetting.","Fading\nInterference\nRetrieval failure\nRepression or suppression"],["Give an example of positive and negative transfer from the eBook.","Positive: slow flight helps short-field landings\nNegative: landing an airplane vs a helicopter"]]},"cfii-i-b-behavior-communication":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-2":[{"f":"542a8fb0c6046a0d.webp","w":256,"h":250,"cap":"Human Behavior"}]},"pts":{"task":"I.B HUMAN BEHAVIOR AND EFFECTIVE COMMUNICATION","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least TASK E, F, and G and one other task.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of human behavior and effective communication as it applies to the teaching/learning process by describing:","items":[{"t":"Human behavior—","sub":["control of human behavior.","human needs.","defense mechanisms.","the flight instructor as a practical psychologist."]},{"t":"Effective communication","sub":["basic elements of communication.","barriers of effective communication.","developing communication skills."]}]},"intro":"Understand human behavior and its relation to teaching, the 3 basic elements of communication, barriers to communication, and how to develop communication skills.","key":["Hierarchy of Human Needs","Defense Mechanisms","Effective Communication: Source, Symbols, Receiver"],"cards":[{"h":"Hierarchy of Human Needs","obj":"Explain the needs a learner must satisfy before focusing on learning.","pts":["Until a need is satisfied, one can't focus fully on learning; once satisfied, it no longer motivates.","**Physiological**: food, rest, protection from the elements. **Security**: protection against danger, threats, deprivation.","**Belonging**: associate, give and receive friendship. **Esteem**: internal (confidence, competence) and external (status, recognition).","**Cognitive & Aesthetic** (added later): need to know and understand; emotional needs.","**Self-actualization**: realizing one's potential, attained only when all other needs are satisfied."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"542a8fb0c6046a0d.webp","w":256,"h":250,"cap":"Human Behavior"}]},{"h":"Defense Mechanisms","obj":"Recognize subconscious ego-protecting reactions in learners.","pts":["Subconscious, almost automatic reactions that soften failure, relieve guilt, protect personal worth.","**Repression** (thoughts pushed to the unconscious), **Denial** (refuse threatening reality), **Compensation** (emphasize a stronger quality).","**Projection** (blame others), **Rationalization** (sincerely believed excuses for unacceptable actions).","**Reaction formation** (develop opposite attitudes), **Fantasy** (daydream instead of act), **Displacement** (shift emotion to a less threatening substitute)."],"ce":""},{"h":"Instructor as Practical Psychologist","obj":"Handle learner emotional reactions that block learning.","pts":["**Anxiety** is the most significant psychological factor in flight instruction; counter by reinforcing enjoyment and teaching to cope with fear.","**Impatience**: present training one step at a time with clear goals; tailor to learner's pace.","**Worry/lack of interest**: divert attention to the task; show exactly how they've progressed.","**Fatigue** impairs judgment; acute (everyday) vs chronic (physiological/psychological). Dehydration reduces alertness.","**Apathy due to inadequate instruction**: give well-planned, accurate, meaningful instruction at the learner's level."],"ce":""},{"h":"Stress and Abnormal Reactions","obj":"Recognize abnormal stress reactions and the instructor's actions.","pts":["**Normal**: respond rapidly and exactly, within limits of experience and training.","**Abnormal**: random, illogical, absent, or inadequate response.","Signs: over-cooperation, extreme self-control, inappropriate laughter/singing, rapid emotion changes.","Seriously abnormal learner: stop instructing, ensure they don't continue or certificate; arrange another instructor's evaluation flight, then confer."],"ce":""},{"h":"Basic Elements of Communication","obj":"Explain source, symbols, and receiver.","pts":["Effectiveness = similarity between idea transmitted and idea received; goal is a change in behavior.","**Source**: ability to use language, reveal positive attitude, accurate/up-to-date/stimulating material.","**Symbols**: words, signs, oral/visual/tactile codes; choose best medium; monitor feedback and modify.","Negative feedback in private only.","**Receiver**: reacts with understanding and changes behavior; use multiple approaches."],"ce":""},{"h":"RM: Barriers to Effective Communication","obj":"Identify and overcome communication barriers.","pts":["**Lack of common experience**: greatest single barrier; speak the learner's language.","**Confusion between symbol and symbolized object**: unclear meaning or context (e.g. sarcasm).","**Overuse of abstractions**: general words may not evoke the same experience.","**External factors**: physiological, environmental (noise), psychological interference.","**Interference**: message disrupted or truncated; ensure it's understood."],"ce":""},{"h":"Developing Communication Skills","obj":"Build instructional communication skills.","pts":["**Role playing**: practice instructing.","**Instructional communication**: know the topic; use past experience; evaluate understanding.","**Listening**: listen to understand rather than refute.","**Questioning**: open-ended and focused questions; paraphrasing and perception checking confirm understanding.","**Instructional enhancement**: deeper knowledge, better conveyance."],"ce":""}],"recall":[["List the hierarchy of human needs in order.","Physiological, Security, Belonging, Esteem, Cognitive & Aesthetic, Self-Actualization"],["A learner blames the crosswind and the CFI for every bad landing. Which defense mechanism?","Projection – relegating blame to others for one's own shortcomings."],["What should a CFI do with a seriously abnormal learner?","Refrain from instructing; ensure they don't continue or become certificated\nArrange an evaluation flight with another instructor, then confer on further action"],["What is the greatest single barrier to effective communication?","Lack of common experience"],["Name the three types of external-factor interference.","Physiological\nEnvironmental\nPsychological"]]},"cfii-i-c-teaching-process":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-3-1":[{"f":"6f1de918b1f69000.webp","w":221,"h":193,"cap":"Completion Standards"}]},"pts":{"task":"I.C TEACHING PROCESS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least TASK E, F, and G and one other task.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of the teaching process by describing:","items":[{"t":"Preparation of a lesson for a ground or flight instructional period.","sub":[]},{"t":"Presentation methods.","sub":[]},{"t":"Application, by the student, of the material or procedure that was presented.","sub":[]},{"t":"Review and evaluation of student performance.","sub":[]},{"t":"Problem-based learning.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Understand how to prepare a lesson, presentation methods, how a learner applies knowledge, and the use of review and evaluation.","key":["Performance Based Objectives","Problem Based Instruction / SBT","Preparation, Presentation, Application, Review/Evaluation"],"cards":[{"h":"Preparation: Objectives","obj":"Write performance- and decision-based objectives.","pts":["Four parts of teaching: **Preparation, Presentation, Application, Review & Evaluation**.","**Performance-based objectives**: clear, measurable, repeatable. Elements: **Description, Conditions, Criteria**.","Criteria: no question whether the objective is met.","As training progresses, shift to **decision-based objectives** (SBT for judgment/ADM).","ACS/PTS supplies performance objectives; use its maneuver-based approach but present as SBT."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"6f1de918b1f69000.webp","w":221,"h":193,"cap":"Completion Standards"}]},{"h":"Problem-Based Learning and SBT","obj":"Build effective problems and scenarios.","pts":["Starts with a carefully constructed problem with no single solution; deepens understanding and recall.","Effective problems: real-world, open-ended, connect old and new knowledge, reflect lesson objectives.","Teaching HOTS: set up problem, determine outcomes, solve, reflect, consider more solutions (guided discovery), reevaluate, reflect on best.","Good scenario: not a test, no single/obvious answer, doesn't promote errors, promotes SA and decisions.","Also: **collaborative problem solving** and **case study** method."],"ce":""},{"h":"Organization, Presentation, Application, Review","obj":"Structure a lesson and carry it through the teaching process.","pts":["**Intro**: Attention, Motivation, Overview. **Development**: past-present, simple-complex, known-unknown, most-least used. **Conclusion** retraces to the objective.","Presentation: know many methods; demonstration-performance has 5 phases. RM: choose the best method for the situation.","**Application**: learner explains or performs; must learn it right the first few times (Primacy).","**Review & evaluation**: compare performance to completion standards before moving on; fix unrelated deficiencies too."],"ce":""}],"recall":[["What are the three elements of a performance-based objective?","Description\nConditions\nCriteria"],["What makes a good scenario?","Not a test\nNo one right or obvious answer\nDoesn't promote errors\nPromotes situational awareness and decision-making"],["What are the three elements of a lesson introduction?","Attention\nMotivation\nOverview"],["What are the four parts of the teaching process?","Preparation, Presentation, Application, Review & Evaluation"]]},"cfii-i-d-teaching-methods":{"req":false,"ofigs":{},"pts":{"task":"I.D TEACHING METHODS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least TASK E, F, and G and one other task.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of teaching methods by describing:","items":[{"t":"Material organization.","sub":[]},{"t":"The lecture method.","sub":[]},{"t":"The cooperative or group learning method.","sub":[]},{"t":"The guided discussion method.","sub":[]},{"t":"The demonstration-performance method.","sub":[]},{"t":"Computer-based training method.","sub":[]},{"t":"Scenario-based training method.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Organize material, then select and properly use a teaching method appropriate to the lesson.","key":["Performance Based Objectives","Problem Based Instruction / SBT","Integrated Flight Instruction"],"cards":[{"h":"Lecture Method","obj":"Choose and deliver the right type of lecture.","pts":["Used to introduce new subjects, summarize, relate theory to practice, reemphasize main points.","Types: **illustrated talk**, **briefing**, **formal**, **informal**, **teaching lecture** (favored: allows participation).","Advantages: large groups, economical, common understanding, supplements other methods.","Disadvantages: passiveness, poor for motor skills, hard to gauge understanding, retention drops after 10/15 min.","Best delivery: **extemporaneous from an outline**. Simple, specific words; vary tone and pace."],"ce":""},{"h":"Cooperative / Group Learning","obj":"Set the conditions for effective group learning.","pts":["Small groups of **3-6**, mixed heterogeneously; tends to raise test scores, self-esteem, social skills, comprehension.","Conditions: clear complete instructions; learners own the objectives; opportunity for success; access to info.","Sufficient time; **individual accountability**; recognition/reward for group success; time to reflect/debrief."],"ce":""},{"h":"Guided Discussion","obj":"Plan and lead a guided discussion.","pts":["Instructor is a facilitator; goal is to draw out what learners know rather than tell them.","Pick a topic learners know; state the objective at the **understanding** level.","Plan at least one **lead-off question** per learning outcome (how/why); give it at end of intro.","Be patient; use how/why follow-ups; conclusion summarizes and re-clarifies.","Question types: lead-off, follow-up, overhead, rhetorical, direct, reverse, relay."],"ce":""},{"h":"Demonstration-Performance","obj":"Teach a skill using the five phases.","pts":["Based on learn-by-doing; best for mental or physical skills that require practice.","**Explanation**: precise actions and end result; encourage questions.","**Demonstration**: show it; acknowledge and explain any deviation from the explanation immediately.","**Learner performance & instructor supervision**, then **evaluation** with actionable feedback."],"ce":""},{"h":"Computer-Based Training and SBT","obj":"Use CBT and scenarios appropriately.","pts":["CBT: reduces manpower, self-paced, responses can vary presentation, focus on areas of need.","CBT limits: avoid excessive use; not practical for a whole program; it can't tell if a learner struggles, so stay involved.","SBT: no canned scenarios fit all learners; devise them from what the learner needs and local features."],"ce":""}],"recall":[["What is the best type of lecture delivery and why?","Speaking extemporaneously from an outline\nMore personalized, flexible, holds attention better"],["What's the difference between a reverse and a relay question?","Reverse: redirected to another learner\nRelay: redirected to the whole group"],["At what level of learning should a guided discussion objective be stated?","Understanding level"],["Name the five phases of demonstration-performance.","Explanation, Demonstration, Learner Performance, Instructor Supervision, Evaluation"],["Why is CBT not a substitute for the instructor?","It doesn't know if a learner is having difficulty; the instructor must stay involved and intervene.\nNot practical for an entire training program."]]},"cfii-i-e-critique-evaluation":{"req":true,"ofigs":{},"pts":{"task":"I.E CRITIQUE AND EVALUATION","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least TASK E, F, and G and one other task.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of critique and evaluation by explaining:","items":[{"t":"Critique—","sub":["purpose and characteristics of an effective critique.","methods and ground rules for a critique."]},{"t":"Evaluation—","sub":["characteristics of effective oral questions and what types to avoid.","responses to student questions.","characteristics and development of effective written test.","characteristics and uses of performance tests, specifically, the FAA PTS.","collaborative assessment (or LCG)."]}]},"intro":"Effectively critique and evaluate student performance using the methods and characteristics described.","key":["Assessment Characteristics","Critique Ground Rules","Effective Questions"],"cards":[{"h":"Characteristics of a Critique","obj":"Give a critique that helps the learner improve.","pts":["Instructor-to-learner assessment of good and bad, parts, relationships, and overall performance.","**Objective** (performance, not opinion), **Flexible** (in context), **Acceptable** (learner must accept the instructor).","**Comprehensive** (strengths and weaknesses), **Constructive** (no negative without a solution), **Organized**.","**Thoughtful** (no ridicule), **Specific** (why it wasn't good and how to improve)."],"ce":""},{"h":"Critique Methods and Ground Rules","obj":"Select a critique method and follow the ground rules.","pts":["Methods: instructor/learner, learner-led, small group, by another learner, self-critique, written.","Written: more thought, learner keeps a record; other class members don't benefit.","Keep to **10-15 min**; hit main points (**4-5** to correct at most).","Avoid absolute statements and controversies; never be maneuvered into defending criticism.","Keep written and oral consistent; end with a summary."],"ce":""},{"h":"Oral Questions","obj":"Ask effective oral questions and handle learner questions.","pts":["Effective: apply to subject, brief/clear, adapted to learner, one idea, a challenge, proper English.","Avoid: puzzle, oversize, toss-up, bewilderment, trick, irrelevant questions.","\"Do you understand?\" and \"Any questions?\" have no place in effective quizzing.","If you can't answer, admit it and promise to get the answer or reintroduce it later."],"ce":""},{"h":"Characteristics of a Good Written Test","obj":"Build reliable, valid written tests.","pts":["**Reliability**: consistent results with repeated measurement.","**Validity**: measures what it's supposed to; the most important consideration.","**Usability** (functional), **Objectivity** (grader bias not reflected), **Comprehensiveness** (samples all objectives).","**Discrimination**: wide score range, all difficulty levels, separates high and low achievers."],"ce":""},{"h":"Collaborative Assessment (Learner-Centered Grading)","obj":"Guide self-assessment with the 4 Rs and rubrics.","pts":["Four Rs: **Replay**, **Reconstruct**, **Reflect**, **Redirect**; then compare with instructor's assessment.","Maneuver grades: **Describe, Explain, Practice, Perform**, Not Observed.","Risk management grades: **Explain, Practice, Manage-Decide**.","Grades aren't self-esteem related; a learner can't flunk a lesson."],"ce":""},{"h":"Performance Tests","obj":"Explain criterion-referenced testing and the PTS/ACS.","pts":["**Criterion referenced**: performance evaluated against a written, measurable standard.","PTS/ACS delineates the standards examiners use; Areas of Operation and Tasks.","Evaluation only against the PTS standards, though train to the highest level possible; removes bias."],"ce":""}],"recall":[["What are the ground rules on critique length and number of points?","No more than 10-15 min\n4-5 things to correct at most"],["Which written test characteristic is most important?","Validity – the test measures what it is supposed to measure."],["What are the four steps of a collaborative critique?","Replay, Reconstruct, Reflect, Redirect"],["Name the maneuver grades and the risk-management grades.","Maneuver: Describe, Explain, Practice, Perform (Not Observed)\nRM: Explain, Practice, Manage-Decide"],["Why is a practical test criterion referenced?","The objective is for all applicants to meet a written, measurable standard (the FAR/PTS standards)."]]},"cfii-i-f-characteristics-responsibilities":{"req":true,"ofigs":{"3-1-3":[{"f":"8b0d1bba28dc7a79.webp","w":310,"h":275,"cap":"Sincerity"}],"3-1-5":[{"f":"fdf938aab7b060f6.webp","w":310,"h":186,"cap":"Personal Appearance and Habits"}]},"pts":{"task":"I.F FLIGHT INSTRUCTOR CHARACTERISTICS AND RESPONSIBILITIES","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least TASK E, F, and G and one other task.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of instructor responsibilities and professionalism by describing:","items":[{"t":"Aviation instructor responsibilities in—","sub":["providing adequate instruction.","establishing standards of performance.","emphasizing the positive."]},{"t":"Flight instructor responsibilities in—","sub":["providing student pilot evaluation and supervision.","preparing practical test recommendations and endorsements.","determining requirements for conducting additional training     and   endorsement requirements."]},{"t":"Professionalism as an instructor by—","sub":["explaining important personal characteristics.","describing methods to minimize student frustration."]}]},"intro":"Understand instructing responsibilities and the characteristics of a professional.","key":["Emphasize the positive","Be consistent","Accept the learners"],"cards":[{"h":"Aviation Instructor Responsibilities","obj":"Provide adequate instruction and set standards.","pts":["Analyze each learner's personality, thinking, ability; same instruction isn't equally effective for all.","Slow learners: easier sub-goals, increase difficulty as confidence grows. Fast learners: constantly raise the standard.","Don't invent deficiencies; don't allow substandard performance.","**Emphasize the positive**: positive instruction results in positive learning."],"ce":""},{"h":"Flight Instructor Responsibilities","obj":"Evaluate, supervise, and endorse responsibly.","pts":["Evaluate against established standards adapted to experience; keep a record; trace errors to their origin.","Often let learners make and fix a mistake (safety permitting).","**Pilot supervision** of solo ops is the most important responsibility.","Practical test recommendation: never less than the complete PTS procedure; instructor is accountable.","Endorsements per **AC 61-65**; keep a record of all endorsements."],"ce":""},{"h":"Additional Training (AC 61-98)","obj":"Conduct flight reviews, IPCs, and checkouts.","pts":["**Flight review**: an instructional service, not a test/checkride; agree on objectives and standards first.","**IPC**: use the Instrument PTS as primary reference for maneuvers and tolerances.","**Checkouts**: to PTS/ACS standards; never in an airplane you aren't current in; log the exact extent."],"ce":""},{"h":"Professionalism and Minimizing Frustration","obj":"Project a professional image and keep learners motivated.","pts":["Professional traits: qualifications, sincerity, **acceptance of the learner**, appearance and habits, demeanor, proper language.","Minimize frustration: motivate, keep informed, treat as individuals, give credit, criticize constructively.","**Be consistent**, admit errors, be well prepared."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"8b0d1bba28dc7a79.webp","w":310,"h":275,"cap":"Sincerity"},{"f":"fdf938aab7b060f6.webp","w":310,"h":186,"cap":"Personal Appearance and Habits"}]}],"recall":[["What is the flight instructor's most important responsibility?","Pilot supervision – only the instructor can determine a learner is ready for solo operations."],["What is the primary reference for an IPC?","The Instrument PTS (maneuvers and tolerances)"],["List ways to minimize learner frustration.","Motivate, keep informed, approach as individuals, give credit\nCriticize constructively, be consistent, admit errors, be well prepared"]]},"cfii-i-g-planning-instruction":{"req":true,"ofigs":{"1-1-1":[{"f":"6f1de918b1f69000.webp","w":238,"h":208,"cap":"Developing Objectives & Standards"}],"5-3":[{"f":"48d09bd9ad4b35b7.webp","w":303,"h":395,"cap":"Scenario Based Training (SBT)"}]},"pts":{"task":"I.G PLANNING INSTRUCTIONAL ACTIVITY","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least TASK E, F, and G and one other task.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of planning instructional activity by describing:","items":[{"t":"Developing objectives and standards for a course of training.","sub":[]},{"t":"Theory of building blocks of learning.","sub":[]},{"t":"Requirements for developing a training syllabus.","sub":[]},{"t":"Purpose and characteristics of a lesson plan.","sub":[]},{"t":"How a scenario-based lesson is developed.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Plan instruction: objectives and standards, building blocks, training syllabus, and lesson plan purpose and characteristics.","key":["Performance Based Objectives","Problem Based Instruction / SBT","Integrated Flight Instruction"],"cards":[{"h":"Objectives and Standards","obj":"Develop objectives and standards before instruction begins.","pts":["Performance-based: description, conditions, criteria. Decision-based: critical thinking, RM, ADM via SBT.","Decision-based doesn't preclude maneuver training; integrate learned maneuvers into realistic situations.","Objectives include desired level of learning and domain(s).","Standards: knowledge/behavior/skill in specific terms with conditions and criteria.","Teach not only how, but also why and when."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"6f1de918b1f69000.webp","w":238,"h":208,"cap":"Developing Objectives & Standards"}]},{"h":"Building Blocks and Syllabus","obj":"Break training into blocks and build a syllabus.","pts":["Master blocks individually, then combine progressively toward the overall objective.","Blocks must be measurable and integral; extraneous blocks are expensive frills.","Syllabus: a road map; abstract of the course with objectives/standards for course, each stage, flight and ground segments.","Ground lessons: cognitive (plus affective). Flight lessons: psychomotor (plus affective); include RM and special emphasis areas."],"ce":""},{"h":"Lesson Plans","obj":"Explain the purpose and characteristics of a lesson plan.","pts":["Purpose: best possible instruction; wise selection of material, suitable sequence, confidence, uniformity.","Characteristics: **Unity, Content, Scope, Practicality, Flexibility, Relation to a course of training, Instructional steps**.","A working document; revise as changes are needed."],"ce":""},{"h":"Scenario-Based Training","obj":"Plan an SBT lesson and act as mentor.","pts":["Reality is the ultimate learning situation; SBT gets as close as possible.","Duties: orient to SBT, build confident planner/self-evaluator, diagnose difficulties, keep records, review.","When the learner makes decisions, revert to **mentor and learning facilitator**.","Pre-scenario planning: destination(s) or task narrative, learning outcomes, desired performance, possible scenario changes."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"48d09bd9ad4b35b7.webp","w":303,"h":395,"cap":"Scenario Based Training (SBT)"}]}],"recall":[["Which domain does each lesson type emphasize?","Ground: cognitive (affective for safety/ADM)\nFlight: psychomotor (affective also important)"],["Name the characteristics of a lesson plan.","Unity, Content, Scope, Practicality, Flexibility, Relation to a course of training, Instructional steps"],["What role should the instructor take once the learner begins making decisions in SBT?","Mentor and learning facilitator"]]},"cfii-ii-a-instruments-nav-equipment":{"req":true,"ofigs":{"1-1":[{"f":"208d6797956dbb75.webp","w":408,"h":250,"cap":"Flight Instruments"}],"1-1-1":[{"f":"12b59a43ee90e759.webp","w":410,"h":200,"cap":"Operation"},{"f":"b1a523b1e68280e8.webp","w":720,"h":226,"cap":"Mechanical"},{"f":"30302adaac68f9bd.webp","w":720,"h":226,"cap":"Mechanical"},{"f":"d9b5d1246396ef32.webp","w":274,"h":228,"cap":"Vertical Speed Indicator"},{"f":"08b521a2f5aa8402.webp","w":396,"h":204,"cap":"Airspeed Indicator"}],"1-1-3":[{"f":"57635df1a477fcf7.webp","w":203,"h":154,"cap":"Blocked Pitot-Static System"},{"f":"27e07db5b7503284.webp","w":201,"h":321,"cap":"Ram Air and Drain Hole Blocked"},{"f":"337b4cdc9dc13edf.webp","w":201,"h":203,"cap":"In a Climb"},{"f":"36f76f5e53954a2d.webp","w":691,"h":375,"cap":"Alternate Static Source"}],"1-2-1":[{"f":"de0c3b6dfd1b2717.webp","w":219,"h":190,"cap":"Gyro Basics"}],"1-2-2":[{"f":"43618f9d896fa516.webp","w":256,"h":216,"cap":"Operation"}],"1-2-3":[{"f":"1f19c59adea570a5.webp","w":256,"h":217,"cap":"Heading Indicator"}],"1-2-4":[{"f":"295b7a91c022f609.webp","w":255,"h":235,"cap":"Heading"},{"f":"a52528a98711a6bf.webp","w":256,"h":272,"cap":"Indications"}],"1-2-5":[{"f":"d2d91e3a9e9ccedb.webp","w":230,"h":226,"cap":"Turning"}],"1-3-1":[{"f":"4bc093260269ff2c.webp","w":154,"h":159,"cap":"Operation"},{"f":"4641209f493e13e5.webp","w":539,"h":286,"cap":"Variation"},{"f":"aa43d624cd3e3734.webp","w":720,"h":220,"cap":"What’s Going On"},{"f":"da724e7c88749e42.webp","w":720,"h":220,"cap":"What’s Going On"}],"1-3-2":[{"f":"74d0abfadcc762a9.webp","w":188,"h":124,"cap":"RMI & HSI"},{"f":"7faa4f80dab176dc.webp","w":655,"h":246,"cap":"RMI & HSI"},{"f":"d09ecf6136244bda.webp","w":655,"h":246,"cap":"RMI & HSI"}],"2-1-3":[{"f":"7333df893c70e656.webp","w":653,"h":219,"cap":"Types of VORS"},{"f":"c8ecb8ead58281f8.webp","w":653,"h":219,"cap":"Types of VORS"}],"2-1-4":[{"f":"c4e93f49f7a7c0ae.webp","w":187,"h":181,"cap":"NEW SERVICE VOLUMES"}],"2-3-2":[{"f":"9d6b862c421da6e9.webp","w":465,"h":258,"cap":"Ground Components"},{"f":"c5dbe31089c098b0.webp","w":407,"h":268,"cap":"Ground Components"},{"f":"b51d8dc714b5f534.webp","w":332,"h":187,"cap":"Ground Components"}],"2-4-2":[{"f":"9000bc15b0718e24.webp","w":130,"h":130,"cap":"Two Antennas"},{"f":"da887ee3258ab820.webp","w":126,"h":126,"cap":"Indicator Instrument"},{"f":"de737104c1e34f00.webp","w":122,"h":123,"cap":"Indicator Instrument"}],"2-6-2":[{"f":"3063f1930c3f3cfe.webp","w":235,"h":176,"cap":"Receiver Autonomous Integrity Monitoring (RAIM)"},{"f":"2e2145ef76a5dfd7.webp","w":210,"h":512,"cap":"Capabilities"},{"f":"f60a929ae21ab8f8.webp","w":210,"h":512,"cap":"Capabilities"}],"3-1":[{"f":"bf4c0ba9407f885c.webp","w":308,"h":198,"cap":"Electronic flight displays"}],"3-2-7":[{"f":"64aeec18e2272496.webp","w":238,"h":233,"cap":"Trend Vectors"}],"6-1-4":[{"f":"c9dac6357ec57c68.webp","w":411,"h":212,"cap":"Basic Terminology"},{"f":"76ddd3384ae5f506.webp","w":411,"h":212,"cap":"Basic Terminology"}]},"pts":{"task":"II.A AIRCRAFT FLIGHT INSTRUMENTS AND NAVIGATION EQUIPMENT","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASKS A and D and at least one other TASK.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of aircraft:","items":[{"t":"Flight instrument systems and their operating characteristics to include—","sub":["pitot-static system.","attitude indicator.","heading indicator/horizontal situation indicator/radio magnetic indicator.","magnetic compass.","turn-and-slip indicator/turn coordinator.","electrical system.","vacuum system.","electronic engine instrument display.","primary flight display, if installed."]},{"t":"Navigation equipment and their operating characteristics to include—","sub":["VHF omnirange (VOR).","DME.","ILS","marker beacon receiver/indicators.","ADF.","transponder/altitude encoding.","electronic flight instrument display.","GPS.","automatic pilot.","FMS.","multifunction display, if installed."]},{"t":"Anti-ice/deicing and weather detection equipment and their operating characteristics to include—","sub":["airframe.","propeller or rotor.","air intake.","fuel system.","pitot-static system.","radar/lightning detection system.","other inflight weather systems."]}]},"intro":"Student can describe the flight and navigation instruments, how they work, their potential errors, and apply the concepts in real-life scenarios.","key":["Pitot / Static Errors","Compass Corrections","VOR Navigation & TIM"],"cards":[{"h":"Pitot-static system","obj":"Explain how pitot and static pressure feed the ASI, altimeter and VSI.","pts":["**Static pressure** (still air) measured at a flush static port where air is undisturbed.","**Pitot pressure** (impact/ram) measured by a pitot tube pointed into the relative wind.","Pitot tube feeds only the **airspeed indicator**; static port feeds **all 3** instruments.","**ADC** (air data computer): solid-state, takes pitot/static + temperature, computes precise altitude, IAS/TAS and temp; displayed on EFIS."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"208d6797956dbb75.webp","w":408,"h":250,"cap":"Flight Instruments"},{"f":"12b59a43ee90e759.webp","w":410,"h":200,"cap":"Operation"},{"f":"b1a523b1e68280e8.webp","w":720,"h":226,"cap":"Mechanical"},{"f":"30302adaac68f9bd.webp","w":720,"h":226,"cap":"Mechanical"},{"f":"d9b5d1246396ef32.webp","w":274,"h":228,"cap":"Vertical Speed Indicator"},{"f":"08b521a2f5aa8402.webp","w":396,"h":204,"cap":"Airspeed Indicator"}]},{"h":"Altimeter","obj":"Explain altimeter operation and its errors.","pts":["Aneroid wafers in a case vented to static: pressure decreases, wafers expand, altitude indication increases.","Kollsman window sets reference pressure: **1\" Hg = 1,000 ft**; pressure alt = 29.92; indicated alt = local setting.","Mechanical: must read within **75 ft** of field elevation with local setting; more than 75 ft = maintenance.","**Cold** air: true alt < indicated. **Warm** air: true alt > indicated.","High to low pressure: altimeter reads high, true alt < indicated. \"From hot to cold, or high to low, look out below!\""],"ce":"Cold-weather risk on approaches: see VIII.A.10 / VIII.B.10."},{"h":"VSI and airspeed indicator","obj":"Explain how the VSI and ASI measure pressure.","pts":["**VSI**: rate-of-pressure-change instrument. Diaphragm and case both vented to static; case through a **calibrated leak**.","Climb: diaphragm pressure drops immediately while case lags, so VSI shows climb; equalizes on level-off.","VSI pointer lags a few seconds behind actual pressure change.","**ASI**: differential gauge of dynamic pressure (pitot minus static); diaphragm fed by pitot, case by static."],"ce":""},{"h":"Blocked pitot-static system","obj":"Predict instrument indications for each blockage.","pts":["Pitot ram air blocked, drain open: pressure vents to static, **airspeed drops to zero**.","Ram air AND drain blocked: **ASI acts like an altimeter**; climb = airspeed increases, descent = decreases.","Static blocked, pitot open: altimeter **freezes**, VSI shows **zero**; ASI reads low above the blockage altitude, high below it.","**Alternate static** (usually in flight deck, lower pressure from venturi): altimeter slightly high, airspeed high, VSI momentary climb then stabilizes."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"57635df1a477fcf7.webp","w":203,"h":154,"cap":"Blocked Pitot-Static System"},{"f":"27e07db5b7503284.webp","w":201,"h":321,"cap":"Ram Air and Drain Hole Blocked"},{"f":"337b4cdc9dc13edf.webp","w":201,"h":203,"cap":"In a Climb"},{"f":"36f76f5e53954a2d.webp","w":691,"h":375,"cap":"Alternate Static Source"}]},{"h":"Gyro principles and power","obj":"Explain rigidity, precession and gyro power sources.","pts":["**Rigidity**: spinning gyro resists tilt; aircraft rotates about it. Used by attitude and heading indicators.","**Precession**: force felt 90 deg from point of application in the direction of rotation. Used by rate instruments (turn indicator/coordinator).","Power: electrical (DC 14 or 28 V; AC for some attitude gyros/autopilots via inverter) or pneumatic (vacuum pump or venturi).","Pneumatic attitude gyros are usually paired with electric rate instruments so one failure doesn't lose all gyros.","Wet vacuum pumps (oil-lubricated vanes) for low altitude; dry pumps for higher altitude. Twin: two systems with check valves."],"ce":"Know each instrument's power source from the POH so you know what to do when one fails.","figs":[{"f":"de0c3b6dfd1b2717.webp","w":219,"h":190,"cap":"Gyro Basics"}]},{"h":"Attitude and heading indicators","obj":"Explain operation and errors of the AI and HI.","pts":["**AI**: vertical spin axis, double gimbal; aircraft pitches/rolls about the gyro; erection mechanism keeps it upright.","Older AIs **tumble** at pitch/bank limits; caging mechanism locks the gyro. Newer designs eliminate tumble.","AI errors: slight nose-up in rapid acceleration (and vice versa); small bank/pitch error after a 180 deg turn; correct within a minute.","**HI**: horizontal spin axis, double gimbal, almost always air driven; set to the compass unless slaved.","Reset HI to compass every **15 min**; earth rotates about 15 deg per hour while the gyro holds position in space."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"43618f9d896fa516.webp","w":256,"h":216,"cap":"Operation"},{"f":"1f19c59adea570a5.webp","w":256,"h":217,"cap":"Heading Indicator"}]},{"h":"Turn instruments and inclinometer","obj":"Explain turn-and-slip, turn coordinator and the ball.","pts":["Turn-and-slip: single gimbal, precession, shows rate and direction; doghouse = **standard rate (3 deg/sec)**; senses yaw only.","Turn coordinator: gimbal canted about **30 deg**, senses **roll and yaw**; shows rate of roll, then rate of turn.","Ball centered = coordinated. Ball **inside** turn = **slip** (too much bank for rate).","Ball **outside** turn = **skid** (rate too great for bank)."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"295b7a91c022f609.webp","w":255,"h":238,"cap":"Basics"},{"f":"a52528a98711a6bf.webp","w":256,"h":272,"cap":"Indications"}]},{"h":"AHRS","obj":"Explain what AHRS replaces and why it is better.","pts":["Same pitch/roll/yaw data from solid-state sensors: inertial sensors, rate gyros, magnetometers, sometimes satellite reception.","One system replaces separate attitude, heading and turn coordinator gyros.","Superior reliability and accuracy, less cost and maintenance; usually integrated with EFIS/glass cockpit."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"d2d91e3a9e9ccedb.webp","w":230,"h":226,"cap":"Turning"}]},{"h":"Magnetic compass and errors","obj":"Explain compass operation, variation, deviation and compass course.","pts":["Two magnets on a float in fluid; card read against the lubber line; pivot allows about **18 deg** of tilt.","Pilot sees the card from behind, so headings appear backward: move the desired heading to the lubber line.","**Variation**: magnetic vs geographic pole (about 1,300 mi apart); isogonic lines; agonic = zero variation.","**Deviation**: aircraft's own magnetic fields; varies by heading; shown on the compass correction card.","TC +/- variation = MC; MC +/- deviation = CC. **East is least, West is best**."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"4bc093260269ff2c.webp","w":154,"h":159,"cap":"Operation"},{"f":"4641209f493e13e5.webp","w":539,"h":286,"cap":"Variation"},{"f":"aa43d624cd3e3734.webp","w":720,"h":220,"cap":"What’s Going On"},{"f":"da724e7c88749e42.webp","w":720,"h":220,"cap":"What’s Going On"}]},{"h":"Dip, acceleration and oscillation errors","obj":"Teach UNOS and ANDS.","pts":["Dip: flux lines dip near the poles, tilting the float and card.","Turning from south: compass **leads**. Turning from north: compass **lags**.","**UNOS**: undershoot north (30 deg at N, 20 at 030/330, 10 at 060/300); overshoot south (30 at S, 20 at 150/210, 10 at 120/240).","**ANDS** (E/W headings only): accelerate = shows turn north; decelerate = turn south.","Oscillation: card swings around the heading; use the average indication."],"ce":""},{"h":"RMI and HSI","obj":"Explain flux-valve heading and bearing needles.","pts":["**RMI**: bearing needle(s) over a heading card driven by a **flux valve** (soft iron ring), not a gyro.","ADF needle: head = bearing TO station, tail = bearing FROM.","VOR needle: head = bearing TO station, tail = radial you're on/crossing.","**HSI**: flux-valve heading indicator plus nav and glideslope display; shows position relative to a selected navaid and course."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"74d0abfadcc762a9.webp","w":188,"h":124,"cap":"RMI & HSI"},{"f":"7faa4f80dab176dc.webp","w":655,"h":246,"cap":"RMI & HSI"},{"f":"d09ecf6136244bda.webp","w":655,"h":246,"cap":"RMI & HSI"}]},{"h":"VOR basics","obj":"Explain what a VOR is, service volumes and types.","pts":["VHF ground station projecting radials referenced to **magnetic north**; course alignment accuracy **+/- 1 deg**.","Frequencies **108.0-117.95 MHz**; line-of-sight, range increases with altitude.","Classes: T 12,000 ft and below 25 NM; L below 18,000 ft 40 NM; H up to 130 NM at 18,000 ft-FL450.","Types: VOR, VOR/DME, VORTAC (TACAN + VOR; DME always integral).","**VOR MON**: VORs reduced from 896 to 590 by 2030 so GPS-loss aircraft can revert; new low volume 70 NM from 5,000-18,000 ft."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"7333df893c70e656.webp","w":653,"h":219,"cap":"Types of VORS"},{"f":"c8ecb8ead58281f8.webp","w":653,"h":219,"cap":"Types of VORS"},{"f":"c4e93f49f7a7c0ae.webp","w":187,"h":181,"cap":"NEW SERVICE VOLUMES"}]},{"h":"VOR instrument and TIM","obj":"Use OBS, CDI and TO/FROM correctly.","pts":["**TIM**: Tune, Identify (Morse or voice ID), Monitor. No ID = out of service, don't use.","CDI shows position relative to the selected course; each dot = **2 deg**. OFF flag = unusable signal.","TO/FROM shows whether the selected course takes you to or from the station, not where you're heading.","Center needle: TO = course to fly to the station; FROM = radial you're on. Two VORs fix position."],"ce":"Indications are the same regardless of aircraft heading - the VOR is heading-independent."},{"h":"VOR checks (91.171)","obj":"Know the IFR VOR check requirement and tolerances.","pts":["No IFR use of VOR unless operationally checked within the preceding **30 days** (FAR 91.171).","Check types (listed in the Chart Supplement): VOT, certified airborne checkpoint, certified ground checkpoint, dual VOR check.","IFR tolerances: **+/- 4 deg** ground checks, **+/- 6 deg** airborne checks."],"ce":""},{"h":"DME","obj":"Explain how DME works and slant-range error.","pts":["Aircraft interrogates ground station; elapsed reply time converted to **slant-range NM**; some units give GS and time to station.","UHF **962-1213 MHz**; line-of-sight. DME ident plays once per 3-4 VOR idents.","Slant-range error greatest **over the station** (shows altitude above it); smallest low and far.","Negligible if **1 NM or more away per 1,000 ft** above facility elevation."],"ce":""},{"h":"ILS - localizer and glideslope","obj":"Explain ILS categories and ground components.","pts":["CAT I not less than **200 ft** HAT; CAT II not less than **100 ft**; CAT III no DH; II/III need special certification.","Localizer: normally **5 deg** wide (full scale 2.5 deg each side); usable 18 NM, up to 4,500 ft above antenna; 108.1-111.95 MHz, odd tenths.","Glideslope: usually 3 deg (2.5-3.5), **1.4 deg** thick, front course only; auto-tuned with the localizer.","Glideslope crosses MM at about 200 ft and OM about 1,400 ft above runway elevation."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"9d6b862c421da6e9.webp","w":465,"h":258,"cap":"Ground Components"},{"f":"c5dbe31089c098b0.webp","w":407,"h":268,"cap":"Ground Components"},{"f":"b51d8dc714b5f534.webp","w":332,"h":187,"cap":"Ground Components"}]},{"h":"ILS - markers, lights, errors","obj":"Identify marker beacons and ILS errors.","pts":["**OM**: 4-7 mi out, glidepath intercept; low tone, 2 dashes/sec, blue/purple.","**MM**: about 3,500 ft from threshold, GS about 200 ft; dots/dashes 95/min, amber. **IM**: CAT II DH; 6 dots/sec, white.","BCM: two dots at 72-75/min, white. Use marker **Low** sensitivity on approach. Compass locators = low-power NDBs at OM/MM.","Errors: **reflection** from vehicles/aircraft below 5,000 ft AGL; **false glideslopes** at higher angles, lowest about 9-12 deg.","Counter false courses by flying the charted altitudes."],"ce":""},{"h":"Flying the localizer and glideslope","obj":"Teach reverse sensing and glideslope technique.","pts":["OBS has no effect on the localizer needle, but set the inbound course anyway.","Front course inbound / back course outbound: normal sensing. Front course outbound / back course inbound: **fly away from the needle** (unless reverse-sensing HSI).","Use back course signals only with a published back course approach.","Glideslope full up to full down = 1.4 deg; set pitch/power at intercept, then **1/2 to 1 deg** pitch changes.","Establish drift correction early; corrections get smaller as the course narrows."],"ce":""},{"h":"ADF and NDB","obj":"Explain NDB/ADF operation and bearing math.","pts":["NDB transmits all directions, **190-535 kHz**; ADF needle points TO the station.","**MH + RB = MB** (\"Mary Had + Roast Beef\"). RB measured clockwise from the nose.","Sense antenna (non-directional) + loop antenna (bidirectional) resolve ambiguity.","Fixed card: always 0 at top, pilot computes MB. Movable card: set MH at top, head = MB to. RMI: auto heading.","Near the station, needle gets sensitive; abeam when needle is 90 deg off track; passage time varies a few seconds to 3 min with altitude."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"9000bc15b0718e24.webp","w":130,"h":130,"cap":"Two Antennas"},{"f":"da887ee3258ab820.webp","w":126,"h":126,"cap":"Indicator Instrument"},{"f":"de737104c1e34f00.webp","w":122,"h":123,"cap":"Indicator Instrument"}]},{"h":"Transponder","obj":"Explain codes, modes and ident.","pts":["Four digits 0-7 = **4,096** codes.","VFR **1200**, hijack **7500**, lost comm **7600**, emergency **7700**.","Mode A: code/position. Mode C: + altitude. Mode S: ICAO address, heading, speed, more; integral to TCAS and ADS-B.","Ident makes the target flash on the controller's screen."],"ce":""},{"h":"GPS segments and position","obj":"Explain how GPS determines position.","pts":["Space: **31 satellites** about 11,000 NM up, ~12 hr orbits, atomic clocks, UHF signals, line-of-sight.","Control: master station, 5 monitor stations, 3 ground antennas. User: the receivers.","**4 satellites** for a 3D fix (lat, long, altitude); about 5 in view at any time.","Pseudo-range: distance from signal travel time, not a direct measurement.","GPS course deviation is **linear** - no sensitivity increase near waypoints like VOR/LOC."],"ce":""},{"h":"RAIM and IFR GPS","obj":"Explain RAIM and IFR GPS equipment rules.","pts":["RAIM: receiver verifies satellite signal integrity; without it, no assurance of position accuracy.","Detection needs **5 satellites**, or **4 + baro-aiding**. Exclusion of a bad signal needs **6** (5 with baro-aiding).","RAIM messages: not enough satellites, or error exceeding limits for the phase of flight.","IFR GPS: TSO-C129, C196, C145 or C146, installed per AC 20-138. Un-augmented (C129/C196) needs an alternate means of nav for the route.","Alternate equipment need not be monitored if RAIM is available; if RAIM loss is predicted, use other equipment, delay or cancel."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"3063f1930c3f3cfe.webp","w":235,"h":176,"cap":"Receiver Autonomous Integrity Monitoring (RAIM)"}]},{"h":"GPS substitution","obj":"List where IFR GPS may substitute for ADF and DME.","pts":["IFR en route/terminal-certified GPS may substitute for ADF and DME to:","Determine position over a DME fix; fly a DME arc.","Navigate TO/FROM an NDB/compass locator; determine position over one.","Fix defined by an NDB bearing crossing a VOR/LOC course; hold over an NDB/compass locator."],"ce":""},{"h":"WAAS and LAAS (GBAS)","obj":"Explain augmentation and what it enables.","pts":["WAAS: ground stations measure signal errors, master station sends corrections via geostationary satellite.","Integrity: undetected error no more than equivalent of **3 seconds** of bad data per year.","Required accuracy 25 ft or less 95% of the time; typically better than 1 m lateral, 1.5 m vertical.","Electronic glidepath independent of ground equipment and baro-aiding; removes cold-temp/altimeter-setting errors. APV as low as **200 ft DA, 1/2 mile**.","**LAAS/GBAS**: airport-local differential corrections over VHF data link, ILS-style display, CAT I and above."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"2e2145ef76a5dfd7.webp","w":210,"h":512,"cap":"Capabilities"},{"f":"f60a929ae21ab8f8.webp","w":210,"h":512,"cap":"Capabilities"}]},{"h":"Electronic flight displays and FMS","obj":"Describe PFD/MFD layout and features.","pts":["PFD (left): flight/nav instruments. MFD (right): engine instruments, moving map. **Reversionary mode** if one screen fails.","Airspeed tape left (TAS at bottom); altitude tape right (20 ft increments) with VSI beside it; HSI below attitude.","Slip/skid bar below the roll pointer; turn-rate bar atop the HSI shows standard/half-standard rate.","**Trend vectors** (magenta) show airspeed/altitude **6 seconds** ahead.","FMS: route/nav brain; integrates autopilot and flight director; monitors ETA, fuel, deviations."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"bf4c0ba9407f885c.webp","w":308,"h":198,"cap":"Electronic flight displays"},{"f":"64aeec18e2272496.webp","w":238,"h":233,"cap":"Trend Vectors"}]},{"h":"Autopilot and automation","obj":"Teach managing and monitoring automation.","pts":["Keeps the aircraft level or on course; capability varies - reference the POH/operator's manual.","Reduces physical and mental workload.","Managing: automation is only as good as its inputs; proficiency is required or SA drops.","Monitoring: follow along to ensure it does what you intend, when you intend it."],"ce":""},{"h":"Anti-ice / deice","obj":"Describe ice protection systems.","pts":["Airframe/prop: weeping wing (fluid through pores), heated surfaces (bleed air), inflatable boots, prop heat or fluid.","Intake: carb heat; alternate air downstream of the filter if the primary ices.","Fuel: no water in tanks; approved additives (e.g. PRIST in Jet-A prevents water crystallizing). 100LL freezes about -58 C; Jet-A -40 C.","Pitot heat is essential in potential icing; windshield defrost can help in light icing.","Bottom line: avoid icing; if in it, leave as soon as possible."],"ce":""},{"h":"Weather detection","obj":"Explain onboard radar, lightning detection and datalink limits.","pts":["Radar: reflects off precipitation; green light, yellow moderate, red severe. **Attenuation**: heavy rain hides weather behind it.","Lightning detection: 360 deg view; **radial spread** (strong strikes look closer) and **ionospheric skip** (false strikes from storms up to 1,000 NM).","**FIS-B** over ADS-B UAT: NEXRAD, METAR/TAF, PIREPs, AIRMET/SIGMET, NOTAMs; can be **15-20 min old**, needs line of sight to a ground station.","AC 00-63: datalink is for **strategic** avoidance only, not close-in maneuvering; augment, don't replace, briefings."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"c9dac6357ec57c68.webp","w":411,"h":212,"cap":"Basic Terminology"},{"f":"76ddd3384ae5f506.webp","w":411,"h":212,"cap":"Basic Terminology"}]}],"recall":[["Which instruments are connected to the pitot tube, and which to the static port?","Pitot: airspeed indicator only.\nStatic: airspeed indicator, altimeter and VSI."],["Flying from high to low pressure without resetting the altimeter: what happens?","Altimeter reads it as a climb, pilot descends to hold altitude.\nTrue altitude < indicated. Look out below."],["Altimeter tolerance on the ground with the local setting?","Within 75 ft of field elevation. More than 75 ft error requires maintenance/calibration."],["Pitot tube and drain hole both iced over. Airspeed in a climb?","Increases with altitude (trapped ram pressure vs. decreasing static). ASI acts like an altimeter; speed changes don't show."],["Static port blocks during a climb. Altimeter, VSI, ASI?","Altimeter frozen at the blockage altitude.\nVSI continuous zero.\nASI reads lower than actual above that altitude (higher below)."],["What do the instruments show on the alternate static source?","Altimeter slightly higher than actual.\nAirspeed greater than actual.\nVSI momentary climb, then stabilizes."],["Which gyro principle do attitude/heading indicators use, and which do turn instruments use?","Attitude and heading: rigidity in space.\nTurn indicator / turn coordinator: precession."],["Ball is to the inside of the turn. What is happening?","Slipping turn: bank too steep for the rate of turn (gravity > inertia)."],["Why reset the heading indicator every 15 minutes?","Gyro holds its position in space while the earth rotates about 15 deg per hour (plus precession), so it drifts from the compass."],["Rolling out on north using the compass from a left turn: lead or undershoot by how much?","Undershoot north by about 30 deg (UNOS). Roll out before the compass reaches N."],["Heading 090, you accelerate. What does the compass show?","An apparent turn to the north (ANDS: Accelerate North, Decelerate South)."],["How do you get compass course from true course?","TC +/- variation = MC (East is least, West is best).\nMC +/- deviation (correction card) = CC."],["VOR check: how often for IFR, and what are the tolerances?","Within the preceding 30 days.\nGround checks (VOT/ground checkpoint) +/- 4 deg; airborne +/- 6 deg."],["What does TIM stand for, and what does a missing ident mean?","Tune, Identify, Monitor.\nNo ident = station out of service; do not use for navigation."],["VOR frequency band and course accuracy?","108.0-117.95 MHz; radial alignment accuracy +/- 1 deg."],["When is DME slant-range error negligible?","When 1 NM or more from the facility for each 1,000 ft above facility elevation. Greatest directly over the station."],["ILS CAT I, II, III minimums?","CAT I: not less than 200 ft HAT.\nCAT II: not less than 100 ft.\nCAT III: no DH; II and III need special certification."],["Localizer width and glideslope thickness?","Localizer normally 5 deg (full scale 2.5 deg each side).\nGlideslope 1.4 deg thick, normally 2.5-3.5 deg angle (usually 3)."],["What is a false glideslope and how do you avoid it?","Extra courses at higher angles (lowest about 9-12 deg) causing very high descent rates. Fly the charted altitudes to intercept."],["Outer marker vs middle marker identification?","OM: low tone, continuous dashes 2/sec, blue/purple light.\nMM: intermediate tone, alternating dot-dash 95/min, amber light."],["Heading 090, ADF relative bearing 270. Magnetic bearing to the station?","MH + RB = MB: 090 + 270 = 360 (subtract 360 when over). Station is due north."],["Standard transponder codes?","VFR 1200, Hijack 7500, Lost comm 7600, Emergency 7700."],["How many satellites for RAIM fault detection? For fault exclusion?","Detection: 5, or 4 with baro-aiding.\nExclusion: 6, or 5 with baro-aiding."],["Un-augmented TSO-C129 GPS under IFR: what else do you need?","An approved, operational alternate means of navigation appropriate to the route. Monitoring not required if RAIM is available."],["Name the GPS substitutions for ADF/DME allowed by AIM 1-2-3c.","Position over a DME fix; fly a DME arc.\nTO/FROM an NDB/compass locator; position over one.\nNDB bearing x VOR/LOC fix; hold over an NDB/compass locator."],["Can you use FIS-B NEXRAD to pick your way between storm cells?","No. It shows where precipitation was (can be 15-20 min old). Per AC 00-63, use it for strategic planning only."],["What do PFD trend vectors show?","Where airspeed and/or altitude will be in 6 seconds."]]},"cfii-ii-b-aeromedical-factors":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-1":[{"f":"b96d3458d7878008.webp","w":72,"h":72,"cap":"Hypoxia"}],"1-2":[{"f":"cef76a719b7d64b1.webp","w":72,"h":72,"cap":"Hypoxia"}],"1-3":[{"f":"328545bb9d7267ba.webp","w":75,"h":75,"cap":"Hypoxia"}],"1-4":[{"f":"2f054ee68301d5a4.webp","w":80,"h":80,"cap":"Hypoxia"}],"3-1-1":[{"f":"895bddac1b9cf741.webp","w":273,"h":205,"cap":"Middle Ear & Sinus Problems"}],"4-2":[{"f":"2c97915faf6421d2.webp","w":351,"h":178,"cap":"Spatial Disorientation"}],"4-2-2":[{"f":"d2e608f55ee09c37.webp","w":217,"h":281,"cap":"Spatial Disorientation"}],"4-2-3":[{"f":"235e447378f8188a.webp","w":219,"h":122,"cap":"Spatial Disorientation"}]},"pts":{"task":"II.B AEROMEDICAL FACTORS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASKS A and D and at least one other TASK.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements related to aeromedical factors by describing the effects, corrective action, and safety considerations of:","items":[{"t":"Hypoxia.","sub":[]},{"t":"Hyperventilation.","sub":[]},{"t":"Middle ear and sinus problems.","sub":[]},{"t":"Spatial disorientation.","sub":[]},{"t":"Motion sickness.","sub":[]},{"t":"Alcohol and drugs.","sub":[]},{"t":"Carbon monoxide poisoning.","sub":[]},{"t":"Evolved gases from scuba diving.","sub":[]},{"t":"Stress and fatigue.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Student can explain the aeromedical factors required by the PTS, why they matter to flying, and their possible effects in flight.","key":["IMSAFE","Trust the Instruments","CO is 200x more likely to bond with blood than oxygen"],"cards":[{"h":"Hypoxia - 4 types","obj":"Define each type of hypoxia with a cause.","pts":["**Hypoxic**: not enough O2 to the lungs - for pilots, reduced O2 partial pressure at altitude.","**Hypemic**: blood can't carry enough O2 - low blood volume, blood disease, **CO poisoning**.","**Stagnant**: blood not moving to tissues - G-forces, shock, poor heart pumping, constricted artery.","**Histotoxic**: cells can't use the O2 delivered - alcohol, narcotics, poison."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"b96d3458d7878008.webp","w":72,"h":72,"cap":"Hypoxia"},{"f":"cef76a719b7d64b1.webp","w":72,"h":72,"cap":"Hypoxia"},{"f":"328545bb9d7267ba.webp","w":75,"h":75,"cap":"Hypoxia"},{"f":"2f054ee68301d5a4.webp","w":80,"h":80,"cap":"Hypoxia"}]},{"h":"Hypoxia symptoms, TUC, treatment","obj":"Recognize hypoxia and know time of useful consciousness.","pts":["Symptoms: cyanosis, headache, delayed reactions, impaired judgment, euphoria, visual impairment, drowsiness, dizziness, tingling, numbness.","**TUC** = max time to make and carry out rational decisions without supplemental O2.","TUC: 20,000 ft 30+ min; 25,000 ft 3-5 min; 30,000 ft 1-2 min; 35,000 ft 30-60 sec; 40,000 ft 15-20 sec.","Treatment: **descend and use supplemental oxygen**.","FAA physiology training: one-day course in OK with altitude chamber and vertigo demos."],"ce":""},{"h":"Hyperventilation","obj":"Distinguish hyperventilation from hypoxia and treat it.","pts":["Stress, fright or pain increase breathing rate/depth; excessive loss of **CO2**; can cause unconsciousness.","Symptoms like hypoxia: visual impairment, dizziness, tingling, hot/cold sensations, muscle spasms.","Treatment: breathe normally (best prevention and cure); paper bag or talk aloud.","**If unsure, treat for hypoxia** - the more dangerous condition."],"ce":""},{"h":"Middle ear and sinus problems","obj":"Explain pressure-related ear and sinus problems.","pts":["Cause: pressure difference between outside air and middle ear/sinuses. Pain is the primary symptom; hearing may drop.","Climb: eardrum bulges **outward**; descent: bulges **inward**; excess can rupture the eardrum.","Ear: chew gum, stretch jaw, **Valsalva**, approved meds/sprays.","Sinus: pain, upper teeth ache, bloody mucus; slower descents help. **Do not fly with sinus problems**."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"895bddac1b9cf741.webp","w":273,"h":205,"cap":"Middle Ear & Sinus Problems"}]},{"h":"Spatial disorientation","obj":"Explain the three orientation systems and how flight fools them.","pts":["Visual (largest source), somatosensory (nerves, \"seat of the pants\"), vestibular (inner ear).","VMC: eyes override false sensations. IMC: they can't.","**Semicircular canals** sense angular acceleration; after about **20 seconds** a steady turn feels like none.","**Otoliths** sense linear acceleration/gravity: acceleration feels like a climb, deceleration like a descent.","Fix: recognize, disregard false sensations, **rely on the instruments**."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"2c97915faf6421d2.webp","w":351,"h":178,"cap":"Spatial Disorientation"},{"f":"d2e608f55ee09c37.webp","w":217,"h":281,"cap":"Spatial Disorientation"},{"f":"235e447378f8188a.webp","w":219,"h":122,"cap":"Spatial Disorientation"}]},{"h":"Vestibular illusions","obj":"Describe cause and result of each vestibular illusion.","pts":["**Leans**: abrupt roll-out from a slowly entered bank feels like banking the other way; pilot rolls back into the turn.","**Coriolis**: abrupt head movement in a prolonged turn; rotation in another axis; dangerous attitude.","**Graveyard spin**: after recovery, feels like spinning the other way; pilot re-enters the original spin.","**Graveyard spiral**: prolonged turn feels like wings-level descent; pulling back tightens the spiral."],"ce":""},{"h":"Vestibular and visual illusions (cont.)","obj":"Describe somatogravic, inversion, elevator and visual illusions.","pts":["**Somatogravic**: rapid acceleration feels nose-up, pilot dives (deceleration = nose-up/stall).","**Inversion**: abrupt climb to level feels like tumbling backward; pilot pushes nose low.","**Elevator**: updraft feels like a climb, pilot pushes nose low (downdraft = nose up).","**False horizon**: sloping clouds, obscured horizon, light patterns. **Autokinesis**: stared-at light in darkness appears to move.","Prevention: reference flight instruments or reliable fixed ground points."],"ce":""},{"h":"Motion sickness and carbon monoxide","obj":"Recognize and treat motion sickness and CO poisoning.","pts":["Motion sickness: conflicting messages, anxiety/stress. Fix: fresh air, look outside, limit head movement, take controls and fly straight and level.","CO: colorless, odorless engine exhaust entering through an exhaust leak; binds to hemoglobin (**200x** more readily than oxygen).","CO effects: hypemic hypoxia, headache, blurred vision, dizziness, drowsiness, loss of muscle power.","Strong exhaust odor = assume CO. **Heater off, fresh air/windows open, oxygen if available, land**."],"ce":""},{"h":"Fatigue and stress","obj":"Distinguish acute and chronic fatigue and stress.","pts":["**Acute fatigue**: short term, normal. Skill fatigue: timing disruption, attention narrowed to center of vision. Prevent with diet and rest.","**Chronic fatigue**: long term, psychological roots or disease; usually needs a physician.","Stressors: physical (noise, vibration), physiological (fatigue), psychological (work/personal).","**Acute stress**: immediate threat, fight or flight; ongoing acute stress can become chronic.","**Chronic stress** exceeds ability to cope, performance falls sharply - not safe to fly; see a physician."],"ce":""},{"h":"Dehydration and nutrition","obj":"Recognize dehydration and poor nutrition effects.","pts":["Dehydration causes: hot flight decks, wind, humidity, diuretics (coffee, tea, alcohol, soda).","Effects: fatigue, poor concentration, headache, cramps, sleepiness, dizziness. Prevent with water, ventilation, sun protection.","Poor nutrition (missed meals): low energy/blood sugar, distraction, short attention span.","Insufficient **vitamin A** can impair night vision."],"ce":""},{"h":"Alcohol, drugs and scuba diving","obj":"Know the regulations and wait times.","pts":["Alcohol causes histotoxic hypoxia; altitude multiplies its effects.","**FAR 91.17**: 8 hrs bottle to throttle (8 hrs and not feeling effects is better); no drugs affecting faculties contrary to safety.","**FAR 61.53**: no flying with meds/treatment that would prevent obtaining a medical. Fly on meds only if FAA approved.","Scuba: excess nitrogen can cause **DCS**. Wait **12 hrs** before flight up to 8,000 ft MSL, **24 hrs** above 8,000 ft.","After a dive requiring a controlled ascent: wait 24 hrs before flight up to 8,000 ft MSL."],"ce":""},{"h":"IMSAFE","obj":"Teach the personal fitness preflight.","pts":["Always perform your own preflight - on yourself.","**I**llness - symptoms? **M**edication - taking any?","**S**tress - family, money, relationships, work.","**A**lcohol - been drinking? **F**atigue - well rested? **E**motion - upset?"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Name the four types of hypoxia and one cause of each.","Hypoxic - high altitude\nHypemic - CO poisoning\nStagnant - G-forces/shock\nHistotoxic - alcohol/drugs"],["TUC at 25,000 ft? At 30,000 ft?","25,000 ft: 3-5 min.\n30,000 ft: 1-2 min."],["Which type of hypoxia does alcohol cause? CO?","Alcohol: histotoxic.\nCO: hypemic."],["Student is tingly and lightheaded at 8,000 ft and you can't tell hypoxia from hyperventilation. What do you do?","Treat for hypoxia (more dangerous): descend / supplemental oxygen, then slow the breathing rate."],["What are the three systems the body uses for orientation?","Visual (eyes), somatosensory (nerves), vestibular (inner ear: semicircular canals and otoliths)."],["How long until a constant-rate turn stops being sensed by the semicircular canals?","About 20 seconds."],["Missed approach at night, rapid acceleration, you feel a strong nose-up. Name the illusion and the danger.","Somatogravic illusion. Pilot may push into a nose-low dive. Trust the attitude indicator."],["Graveyard spiral: what causes it and what does the pilot do wrong?","Altitude loss in a prolonged turn that no longer stimulates the inner ear feels like a wings-level descent; pilot pulls back, tightening the spiral."],["What is the Coriolis illusion?","Abrupt head movement during a prolonged turn creates the illusion of rotation in a different axis; pilot may maneuver into a dangerous attitude."],["Suspected CO poisoning in flight. Actions?","Heater off; open fresh air vents and windows; supplemental oxygen if available; land."],["Scuba wait times before flying?","12 hrs for flight up to 8,000 ft MSL; 24 hrs above 8,000 ft.\n24 hrs after a dive requiring a controlled ascent (up to 8,000 ft)."],["Which regs cover alcohol and medication?","91.17: 8 hrs bottle to throttle, no drugs contrary to safety.\n61.53: no flying with a condition/meds that would prevent a medical."],["What does IMSAFE stand for?","Illness, Medication, Stress, Alcohol, Fatigue, Emotion."]]},"cfii-ii-c-ifr-regs-pubs":{"req":false,"ofigs":{},"pts":{"task":"II.C REGULATIONS AND PUBLICATIONS RELATED TO IFR OPERATIONS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASKS A and D and at least one other TASK.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements related to regulations and publications, (related to instrument flight and instrument flight instruction) their purpose, general content, availability, and method of revision by describing:","items":[{"t":"14 CFR parts 61, 71, 91, 95, and 97.","sub":[]},{"t":"FAA-H-8083-15, Instrument Flying Handbook.","sub":[]},{"t":"Aeronautical Information Manual.","sub":[]},{"t":"Practical Test Standards.","sub":[]},{"t":"Chart Supplement.","sub":[]},{"t":"Standard Instrument Departures/Terminal Arrivals.","sub":[]},{"t":"En Route Charts.","sub":[]},{"t":"Standard Instrument Approach Procedure Charts.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"The student knows the FARs and publications related to IFR certification and flight: IFR rules and requirements, plus the publications available to assist in flight and in learning.","key":["Instrument Rating Requirements","Part 91 IFR Regs","Useful Publications"],"cards":[{"h":"Part 61 overview","obj":"Know what Part 61 covers and where the IFR-relevant sections live.","pts":["Part 61 = requirements for pilot, flight instructor and ground instructor certificates and ratings","Also: when those certificates are necessary, and their privileges and limitations","IFR pieces: **61.57** recent flight experience (PIC), **61.65** instrument rating requirements","CFII pieces: 61.183(c)(2)(iv) eligibility, 61.187(b)(7) areas of operation, 61.191 additional flight instructor ratings"],"ce":""},{"h":"61.65(a) Instrument rating eligibility","obj":"List what an applicant must do to get the instrument rating.","pts":["Hold at least a current PPL with an airplane rating suitable to the rating sought","Read, speak, write and understand English","Logged ground training from an authorized instructor OR a home-study course on the 61.65(b) knowledge areas","Endorsement for the knowledge test, then pass it","Logged flight training on the 61.65(c) areas of operation (aircraft, FFS or FTD)","Endorsement for the practical test, then pass it"],"ce":""},{"h":"61.65(d) Aeronautical experience","obj":"Recite the hour requirements for the instrument-airplane rating.","pts":["**50 hr** cross-country PIC, at least **10 hr** in airplanes","**40 hr** actual or simulated instrument time on the areas of operation","Of that: at least **15 hr** instrument training from an authorized instructor in the category sought","At least **3 hr** instrument training for the practical test within **2 calendar months** before the test","One IFR cross-country: **250 NM** along airways or ATC routing, an approach at each airport, **three different kinds** of approaches"],"ce":"FFS/FTD credit is in 61.65(h); ATD credit is in 61.65(i)."},{"h":"61.57(c) IFR currency","obj":"State what keeps a pilot instrument-current as PIC.","pts":["Within the last **6 months**, actual or simulated conditions, performed and logged:","At least **6 instrument approaches**","**Holding** procedures","**Intercepting and tracking** courses using navigation systems"],"ce":""},{"h":"Part 91 structure","obj":"Know where IFR rules sit in Part 91.","pts":["Part 91 = rules governing the operation of aircraft","Subpart A General; B Flight Rules: 91.101 general, 91.103 preflight action, 91.109 flight instruction / simulated instrument flight, 91.151 visual, **91.167 onward instrument**","Subpart C equipment/instrument/certificate requirements: **91.205(d)** IFR equipment","Subpart E maintenance: **91.411** altimeter/static inspections"],"ce":""},{"h":"Can the airplane fly IFR? 91.205(d) GRABCARD","obj":"List the IFR instruments and equipment beyond VFR day (TOMATOFFLAAMES) and night (FLAPS).","pts":["**G**enerator/alternator of adequate capacity","**R**adio (two-way) and nav equipment appropriate to the route","**A**ltimeter (sensitive), **B**all (of turn coordinator)","**C**lock with second hand, **A**ttitude indicator","**R**ate-of-turn indicator, **D**irectional gyro"],"ce":""},{"h":"91.411 and 91.171 equipment checks","obj":"Know the altimeter/static and VOR check intervals and tolerances.","pts":["**91.411**: within the preceding **24 months**, each static pressure system, each altimeter, each automatic pressure altitude reporting system","**91.171**: VOR operationally checked within the preceding **30 days**","**VOT**: +/-4 deg; reads 180 TO or 360 FROM","Designated ground checkpoint +/-4 deg; airborne checkpoint +/-6 deg","No check point: fly over a prominent ground point on an airway radial; must be within +/-6 deg","Dual VOR: within 4 deg of each other"],"ce":""},{"h":"IFR preflight: 91.103, 91.169, 91.167","obj":"Explain IFR preflight duties, alternate rules and fuel.","pts":["91.103 IFR-specific: weather reports/forecasts, fuel, alternatives if the flight can't be completed, known ATC traffic delays","91.169 no alternate needed if from **1 hr before to 1 hr after ETA**: ceiling **>= 2,000 ft** AND vis **>= 3 SM**","Alternate minimums at ETA: precision **600 ft / 2 SM**; non-precision **800 ft / 2 SM**","No approach at the alternate: must allow descent from MEA, approach and landing under VFR","91.167 fuel: to destination, then to alternate (if required), then **45 min** at normal cruise"],"ce":""},{"h":"IFR departure: 91.173, 91.175","obj":"Know what is required to depart IFR.","pts":["91.173: in controlled airspace under IFR you need a **filed IFR flight plan** and an **ATC clearance**","91.175: **0/0 takeoffs are legal** under Part 91","Recommended: use published takeoff minimums, or approach minimums if none, as a guideline"],"ce":""},{"h":"IFR en route: 91.177, 91.179, 91.181","obj":"Explain minimum altitudes, cruising altitudes and course to be flown.","pts":["91.177: no lower than Part 95/97 altitudes; if none, **2,000 ft** (mountainous) or **1,000 ft** (non-mountainous) above highest obstacle within **4 NM** of course","Below MEA but not below MOCA when within **22 NM** of the VOR","Climb to a higher MIA immediately after the point it applies; cross at or above the MCA","91.179: controlled airspace = altitude ATC assigns (VFR-on-top: 91.159); uncontrolled below 18,000 MSL: 0-179 deg odd thousands, 180-359 even","91.181: airway centerline, or direct course between navaids/fixes; maneuvering to clear traffic allowed"],"ce":""},{"h":"91.183 reports and 91.187 malfunction reports","obj":"List required IFR reports to ATC.","pts":["91.183, as soon as possible: time/altitude passing designated reporting points (under radar: only those ATC requests)","Any unforecast weather encountered; any other information relating to safety of flight","91.187, controlled airspace: report malfunctions of **nav, approach or comm** equipment","Report contents: aircraft ID, equipment affected, degree IFR capability is impaired, nature/extent of assistance desired"],"ce":""},{"h":"91.185 Lost communications","obj":"Teach the lost-comm route, altitude and clearance-limit rules.","pts":["VFR conditions: continue VFR and land as soon as practicable","Route **AVEF** in order: Assigned, Vectored (direct from point of failure), Expected, Filed","Altitude **MEA** = highest of: Minimum IFR altitude, Expected, Assigned","Limit is an approach fix: descend as close as possible to EFC, or if none, to the filed ETA","Limit not an approach fix: leave at EFC; if none, leave on arrival, proceed to an approach fix, descend near the filed ETA"],"ce":""},{"h":"IFR approaches: 91.175(a)(c)","obj":"State when you may descend below DA/DH/MDA.","pts":["Must use a Part 97 approach for that airport when an approach is necessary","Continuously in position for a normal descent to the intended runway with normal maneuvers","Flight visibility not less than the approach prescribes","At least one visual reference distinctly visible (approach lights, threshold, REIL, VASI, TDZ, runway and their lights/markings)","Approach lights alone: not below **100 ft above TDZE** unless red terminating bars or red side row bars are visible"],"ce":""},{"h":"91.175 (d), (e), (j), (k)","obj":"Know landing, missed approach, PT limits and OM substitutes.","pts":["(d) May not land if flight visibility is below that prescribed","(e) Miss immediately if (c) not met below MDA, or at the MAP / DA, and any time after until touchdown","(e) Also miss if an identifiable part of the airport is lost while circling at or above MDA","(j) No PT (unless cleared) with radar vectors to final, a timed approach from a holding fix, or 'No PT'","(k) OM substitutes: compass locator, PAR/ASR, DME/VOR/NDB fixes in the SIAP, suitable RNAV with a procedure fix"],"ce":""},{"h":"Parts 71, 95, 97","obj":"Know what the other IFR-related parts prescribe.","pts":["**Part 71**: airspace and routes: reporting points, ATS routes, Class A through E","**Part 95**: IFR altitudes on ATS routes or direct routes with a designated MEA; designates mountainous areas and changeover points","**Part 97**: ODPs, instrument approach procedures, takeoff/landing minimums; symbols/terminology (categories, segments), TERPS"],"ce":""},{"h":"Publications: IFH, AIM, PTS/ACS","obj":"Know which publications support IFR learning and flight.","pts":["**IFH**: for instrument instructors and pilots preparing for the rating; all IMC knowledge/skills; revised occasionally by the FAA","**AIM**: basic flight info and ATC procedures; **Chapters 4, 5 and 6** carry most IFR operations info","AIM Ch 1 nav aids/PBN/RNAV; 3 airspace; 4 ATC; 5 ATC procedures; 6 emergencies (lost comm); 7 meteorology, altimeter errors, wake; 9 charts","PTS still used for Sport, Recreational and Flight Instructor; ACS for Private, Instrument, Commercial, ATP/type rating"],"ce":""},{"h":"Chart Supplement and en route low charts","obj":"Know what these contain and how often they change.","pts":["**Chart Supplement**: directory of public airports, seaplane bases, heliports, comm data, navaids, procedures, special notices","Published every **8 weeks** by NACO; **7 districts**: NW, SW, NC, SC, EC, NE, SE","**En route low**: IFR navigation below 18,000 MSL; IFR equivalent of the sectional","Charted altitudes: **MEA, MOCA, MRA, MCA, MAA**; navaids, airways, intersections, RCOs","Revised every **56 days**"],"ce":""},{"h":"Terminal Procedures Publication (TPP)","obj":"Find nonstandard minimums, DPs, STARs and IAPs in the TPP.","pts":["Published by NACO: approaches, arrivals, DPs, airport diagrams by region","**Trouble T** (black triangle with T): nonstandard takeoff mins, plus ODPs and diverse vector areas","**Black triangle with A**: nonstandard alternate minimums","Radar minimums: PAR and ASR approaches; controller gives course/altitude guidance; used in emergency or distress"],"ce":""},{"h":"DPs and STARs","obj":"Explain the purpose and assumptions of departure and arrival procedures.","pts":["DPs: preplanned IFR departure/obstacle avoidance, textual (TPP section C) or graphic (after the airport's IAPs)","Purpose: obstacle protection while reducing communications and departure delays","Protection assumes crossing the runway end at **35 ft AGL**, climbing to **400 ft** above airport before turning, and **200 ft/NM** (unless higher gradient published)","STARs: transition from en route structure to a terminal fix where an approach can be made (TPP section P)","Both revised every **56 days**"],"ce":""},{"h":"Instrument approach chart layout","obj":"Name the main sections of an IAP chart.","pts":["Purpose: method to descend and land safely in low visibility","**Margin identification**: top/bottom info, airport location, procedure ID","**Plan view**: overhead view; **Profile view**: side view with altitudes, headings, distances, fixes","**Landing minimums** (and notes): lowest altitude and visibility","**Airport diagram**: surface features, configuration, frequencies","Revised every 56 days"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Instrument-airplane rating: what does the required IFR cross-country flight need?","At least 250 NM along airways or ATC-directed routing\nAn instrument approach at each airport\nThree different kinds of approaches using navigation systems"],["How much instrument training must be within 2 calendar months of the instrument practical test?","At least 3 hours, in preparation for the practical test (61.65(d))."],["What are the 61.57(c) instrument recency requirements?","Within the preceding 6 months:\n6 instrument approaches\nHolding procedures\nIntercepting and tracking courses with nav systems"],["What does GRABCARD stand for?","Generator/alternator, Radio & nav equipment, Altimeter (sensitive), Ball, Clock (second hand), Attitude indicator, Rate-of-turn indicator, Directional gyro"],["VOR check tolerances for IFR?","Within 30 days\nVOT: +/-4 (180 TO / 360 FROM)\nGround checkpoint: +/-4\nAirborne checkpoint or airway ground point: +/-6\nDual VOR: 4 deg of each other"],["What must be inspected every 24 months under 91.411?","Each static pressure system, each altimeter instrument, and each automatic pressure altitude reporting system."],["When is an IFR alternate NOT required?","From 1 hour before to 1 hour after ETA, forecast ceiling at least 2,000 ft AND visibility at least 3 SM (1-2-3 rule)."],["Standard alternate minimums and IFR fuel reserve?","Precision approach: 600 ft and 2 SM\nNon-precision: 800 ft and 2 SM\nFuel: destination + alternate + 45 min at normal cruise"],["No published minimum IFR altitude: how low may you fly?","Mountainous: 2,000 ft above highest obstacle within 4 NM of course\nNon-mountainous: 1,000 ft above highest obstacle within 4 NM"],["When may you fly below the MEA?","When an MOCA is prescribed: down to the MOCA, but only within 22 NM of the VOR concerned."],["Lost comm in IMC: what route and altitude?","Route AVEF: Assigned, Vectored, Expected, Filed\nAltitude: highest of Minimum IFR altitude, Expected, Assigned (MEA)"],["Lost comm, clearance limit is not an approach fix and no EFC was received. What do you do?","Leave the limit on arrival over it, proceed to a fix from which an approach begins, and start descent as close as possible to the filed ETA."],["When may you NOT fly a procedure turn (unless cleared)?","Radar vectors to the final approach course or fix\nTimed approach from a holding fix\nProcedure marked No PT"],["Using only the approach light system, how low may you descend?","Not below 100 ft above TDZE unless the red terminating bars or red side row bars are distinctly visible."],["Which CFR part designates mountainous areas and changeover points?","Part 95 (IFR Altitudes)."],["Name the five charted IFR altitudes on an en route low chart.","MEA - Minimum En Route Altitude\nMOCA - Minimum Obstacle Clearance Altitude\nMRA - Minimum Reception Altitude\nMCA - Minimum Crossing Altitude\nMAA - Maximum Authorized Altitude"],["What does a DP assume for obstacle clearance?","Cross the departure end at least 35 ft AGL\nClimb to 400 ft above airport elevation before turning\nClimb at least 200 ft per NM unless a higher gradient is specified"],["What does a black triangle with a T or an A in the TPP mean?","T: nonstandard takeoff minimums (and/or ODP / diverse vector area)\nA: nonstandard alternate minimums"]]},"cfii-ii-d-instrument-logbook-entries":{"req":true,"ofigs":{},"pts":{"task":"II.D LOGBOOK ENTRIES RELATED TO INSTRUMENT INSTRUCTION","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASKS A and D and at least one other TASK.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of logbook entries related to instrument instruction by describing:","items":[{"t":"Logbook entries or training records for instrument flight/instrument flight instruction or ground instruction given.","sub":[]},{"t":"Preparation of a recommendation for an instrument rating practical test, including appropriate logbook entry.","sub":[]},{"t":"Required endorsement of a pilot logbook for satisfactory completion of an instrument proficiency check.","sub":[]},{"t":"Required flight instructor records.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"For the instructor: correctly log instrument time and flight instruction and give the endorsements required for IFR operations.","key":["Logging Time","Endorsements"],"cards":[{"h":"Logging instrument time: 61.51(g)","obj":"Know who may log instrument time and when sim time counts.","pts":["Log only time flown **solely by reference to instruments**, actual or simulated","Instructor may log instrument time when instructing in **actual** IFR conditions","FFS/FTD/ATD time counts toward a certificate/rating only with an authorized instructor present who signs to verify time and content","Recency in an FFS/ATD needs **no instructor**; log the device, time and content (61.51(g)(5))","Any combination of aircraft and ATD can meet recency (61.57(c)(2))"],"ce":""},{"h":"Logbook contents: 61.57(c), 61.51(b), 61.51(h)","obj":"List what must be recorded for currency and training.","pts":["Currency (61.57(c)): location and type of each approach; safety pilot name, if required","61.51(b): date, total flight/lesson time, departure and arrival (sim: lesson location), type and ID of aircraft/sim/FTD, type of experience/training, conditions of flight","Ground training on the 61.65(b) knowledge areas must be received and logged","61.51(h) training time: legibly endorsed; description of training, lesson length, instructor signature, certificate number and expiration date"],"ce":""},{"h":"Practical test recommendation and IPC","obj":"Know the prerequisites and endorsements you sign.","pts":["Applicant: at least a private certificate; English proficient","Ground training or home study on 61.65(b); knowledge test endorsement and pass","Training on 61.65(c) areas of operation; practical test endorsement","AC 61-65 endorsements: **A.42** knowledge test, **A.43** flight proficiency/practical test, **A.44** prerequisites for instrument practical test","IPC: **AC 61-65 A.71** certifies completion of the IPC of 14 CFR 61.57(d) in a make/model on a date"],"ce":""},{"h":"61.189 Flight instructor records","obj":"State what records a CFI must keep and for how long.","pts":["Sign the logbook of each person given flight or ground training","Record the name of each person endorsed for a knowledge or practical test","Record the kind of test, the date and the results","Keep records at least **3 years**"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Does an instructor need to be present for instrument currency done in an ATD?","No. Keep a logbook/training record of the device, time and content. An instructor must be present only when the time is used toward a certificate or rating."],["What must an instructor include when endorsing logged training time (61.51(h))?","Description of the training given\nLength of the lesson\nInstructor's signature, certificate number and expiration date"],["What extra items must be logged for instrument currency under 61.57(c)?","Location and type of each instrument approach, and the name of the safety pilot if required."],["Which AC 61-65 endorsement is the IPC, and which regulation does it cite?","A.71; certifies satisfactory completion of the instrument proficiency check of 14 CFR 61.57(d)."],["What records must a CFI keep and for how long?","Name of each person endorsed for a knowledge or practical test, kind of test, date and result; at least 3 years (61.189)."]]},"cfii-iii-a-weather-info":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"3-3-4":[{"f":"560f3c5bb438436b.webp","w":293,"h":188,"cap":"Description"}],"5-3":[{"f":"c34f8add87d1a139.webp","w":597,"h":254,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"505c475e58b0afff.webp","w":597,"h":254,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"8c79a1375bb2d8ea.webp","w":597,"h":254,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"}],"5-4":[{"f":"67bc4ef28ed1c9eb.webp","w":543,"h":231,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"a2f6e4f07cdb019e.webp","w":543,"h":231,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"}],"5-5":[{"f":"c58f29ee42ea979d.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"f15c84b9a1f66d7f.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"4bdd91cc19933798.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"b9a3cdb1cd5f322b.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"53549d0d39bd5dc6.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"}],"6-1":[{"f":"198ffd1593079345.webp","w":329,"h":216,"cap":"Radar Summary Chart"}],"7-1":[{"f":"365fa77b1ad41ccf.webp","w":436,"h":92,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"}],"7-2":[{"f":"f41e8616a5059ae1.webp","w":242,"h":188,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"}],"7-2-1":[{"f":"c3909b5d88c5d843.webp","w":357,"h":78,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"},{"f":"73967a50b31fff9b.webp","w":672,"h":223,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"}],"7-3":[{"f":"7e936065f2606713.webp","w":246,"h":107,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"},{"f":"464c8f34dbd6ac68.webp","w":670,"h":325,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"}],"7-4":[{"f":"0274c2fa04e21db0.webp","w":268,"h":115,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"}],"8-1":[{"f":"609f07117417dd41.webp","w":344,"h":101,"cap":"Winds & Temperatures Aloft Chart (FD)"}],"9-1":[{"f":"e4b8a38a3bdad715.webp","w":291,"h":268,"cap":"Freezing Level Charts"}],"10-1":[{"f":"521c19fee6881ca9.webp","w":349,"h":91,"cap":"Stability Charts"}],"10-2":[{"f":"8c17091add88bf6d.webp","w":352,"h":297,"cap":"Stability Charts"}],"11-2-2":[{"f":"88a8a564523dc8e9.webp","w":290,"h":227,"cap":"Marginal (MRGL) – Dark Green"}],"11-2-6":[{"f":"c6d89ffb5f56dbf0.webp","w":294,"h":267,"cap":"High (HIGH) – Magenta"}],"11-3":[{"f":"465fd1d25a9cfdaf.webp","w":297,"h":182,"cap":"Day 4-8 Chart Depictions"}],"14-2-3":[{"f":"7398f8cd383c734a.webp","w":405,"h":181,"cap":"Flying through a Microburst"}]},"pts":{"task":"III.A WEATHER INFORMATION","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one TASK.","note":"NOTE: If the current weather reports, forecasts, or other pertinent information is not available, or if the current weather is not appropriate for the practical test scenario, then weather reports, forecasts, and other pertinent information shall be simulated by the evaluator in a manner to adequately measure the applicant’s competence.","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge related to IFR weather information.","items":[{"t":"Sources of weather—","sub":["AWOS, ASOS, and ATIS reports.","Sources of weather data (e.g., National Weather Service, Flight Service) for flight planning purposes."]},{"t":"Weather reports and charts—","sub":["METAR, TAF, and Graphical Forecasts for Aviation (GFA).","inflight weather advisories.","surface analysis, weather depiction, and radar summary charts.","significant weather prognostic charts.","winds and temperatures aloft charts.","PIREPS.","freezing level charts.","severe weather outlook charts.","SIGMETs and AIRMETs."]}]},"intro":"The learner interprets several weather sources and makes a competent Go/No Go decision based on that information.","key":["Weather Sources","FSS: 122.2","Go/No Go Decision"],"cards":[{"h":"Briefing and weather sources","obj":"Explain why a thorough briefing matters and where to get it.","pts":["First step in deciding if the flight is safe and where/when problems may occur; **91.103** requires PIC familiarity with weather","Big picture in the days before: internet, apps, TV","Detailed: **FSS** (1-800-WX-BRIEF / 1800wxbrief.com), primary source; NOTAMs, weather, flight plan filing","NWS aviationweather.gov: weather only; ForeFlight briefings are timestamped and stored for 91.103","AIM 7-1-2-C: regulatory-compliant self-briefing possible; see **AC 91-92** Pilot's Guide to a Preflight Briefing","In flight: FSS (published freq or **122.2**), apps, satellite weather, **FIS-B** (part of ADS-B)"],"ce":""},{"h":"METAR decode, part 1","obj":"Read the front half of a METAR.","pts":["Routine hourly; **SPECI** = special report when weather changes, mishaps, etc.","Station: 4 letters (K = US); Alaska PA, Hawaii PH. Date/time: DDHHMMZ (UTC)","Modifiers: AUTO (automated), AO1/AO2 (precip sensor type), COR (corrected)","Wind: 3 digits direction (tens of degrees) + speed in knots; G = peak gust; V group if varying >60 deg at >6 kt","Visibility in SM; RVR as R + runway / range in feet","Weather: qualifiers (-, +, VC, SH, TS, FZ) + phenomena (DZ, RA, HZ, SS, DS, SN)"],"ce":""},{"h":"METAR decode, part 2","obj":"Read sky, temperature, altimeter and remarks.","pts":["Sky: amount, height, type; eighths of sky","SKC/CLR <1/8, FEW 1/8-2/8, SCT 3/8-4/8, BKN 5/8-7/8, OVC 8/8","Heights in hundreds of feet AGL; clouds above **12,000 ft** not detected; TCU/CB reported with height","Temp/dewpoint in Celsius; M = below zero","Altimeter: A + 4 digits inHg; PRESRR / PRESFR = pressure rising / falling","RMK: wind data, variable vis, begin/end times, pressure info"],"ce":""},{"h":"TAF","obj":"Decode a terminal aerodrome forecast.","pts":["Covers a **5 SM** radius around the airport; valid **24 or 30 hr**; issued 4x daily (00, 06, 12, 18Z)","Order: type (TAF / TAF AMD), station, issue time, valid period, wind, vis (P6SM = >6), weather (NSW = none), sky (only **CB** clouds forecast)","**FM**: rapid change, usually within an hour","**BECMG**: gradual change over no more than **2 hr**","**TEMPO**: fluctuations lasting less than an hour; **PROB**: % chance of TS/precip"],"ce":""},{"h":"GFA and MOS","obj":"Know what the GFA and MOS provide and their limits.","pts":["**GFA**: en route forecast and for locations without a TAF; replaces the area forecast; CONUS, Gulf, Caribbean, part of Atlantic","GFA: 14 hr past to 15 hr future, hourly; clouds, flight category, precip, icing, turbulence, wind","GFA limits: not every phenomenon, no amendments, mostly below FL180, automated, clutter, overlays can be turned off","**MOS** (not ACS-required): automated statistics on models; a TAF-like forecast for airports without one","MOS is **not a legal weather source**; can't forecast multiple layers, VC showers/fog, precip intensity, non-convective LLWS, variable winds; US only"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"560f3c5bb438436b.webp","w":293,"h":188,"cap":"Description"}]},{"h":"Surface analysis chart","obj":"Read the current surface analysis and station model.","pts":["Current surface weather: pressure, fronts, temps, dewpoints, wind, weather, obstructions; every **3 hr**","Round station = official observer, square = automated","SLP 3 digits in tenths of mb: prefix 10 if >=1000 mb, 9 if <1000; tendency over past 3 hr below it","Temp and dewpoint in **Fahrenheit**; precip over last 6 hr","Wind from true direction; short barb 5 kt, long barb 10 kt, pennant 50 kt"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"c34f8add87d1a139.webp","w":597,"h":254,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"505c475e58b0afff.webp","w":597,"h":254,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"8c79a1375bb2d8ea.webp","w":597,"h":254,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"67bc4ef28ed1c9eb.webp","w":543,"h":231,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"a2f6e4f07cdb019e.webp","w":543,"h":231,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"c58f29ee42ea979d.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"f15c84b9a1f66d7f.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"4bdd91cc19933798.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"b9a3cdb1cd5f322b.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"},{"f":"53549d0d39bd5dc6.webp","w":719,"h":163,"cap":"Surface Analysis Chart"}]},{"h":"Radar summary chart","obj":"Know what radar summary shows and doesn't.","pts":["Collection of radar reports: precipitation areas and characteristics; NA = not reported, NE = no echoes","Intensity: 6 levels shown by 3 contours; tops in hundreds of feet **MSL**; cell movement arrow with knots (LM = little movement)","Echoes as areas, cells or lines; weather watches = heavy dashed boxes","Limits: precipitation only; no clouds/fog without precip; no cloud tops/bases","Use with METARs and forecasts; published hourly at **35 past**"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"198ffd1593079345.webp","w":329,"h":216,"cap":"Radar Summary Chart"}]},{"h":"Significant weather prog charts","obj":"Distinguish surface, low, mid and high level progs.","pts":["Snapshot of forecast weather at the valid time","**Surface**: next 2.5 days, periods 12/18/24/48/60 hr; pressure, fronts, precip, squall lines","**Low level** (FL240 and below): 12 and 24 hr; IFR/MVFR/VFR, turbulence, freezing level (MSL); 4x daily","**Mid** (10,000 MSL-FL450): single 24 hr; TS/CB, mod/severe turb and icing, jets, tropopause; 4x daily","**High** (FL250-FL630): international en route; same as mid minus non-convective clouds with icing/turb"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"365fa77b1ad41ccf.webp","w":436,"h":92,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"},{"f":"f41e8616a5059ae1.webp","w":242,"h":188,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"},{"f":"c3909b5d88c5d843.webp","w":357,"h":78,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"},{"f":"73967a50b31fff9b.webp","w":672,"h":223,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"},{"f":"7e936065f2606713.webp","w":246,"h":107,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"},{"f":"464c8f34dbd6ac68.webp","w":670,"h":325,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"},{"f":"0274c2fa04e21db0.webp","w":268,"h":115,"cap":"Significant Weather Prognostic Charts"}]},{"h":"Winds and temperatures aloft (FD)","obj":"Decode FD wind and temperature groups.","pts":["4-digit group: direction (tens, **true**) + speed (knots); issued 4x daily","No wind within **1,500 ft** of station elevation; no temp within **2,500 ft**","100-199 kt: direction +50, speed -100. Ex 7319 = 230 deg at 119 kt","200 kt or more: coded 99. Ex 7799 = 270 at 199+ kt","**9900** = light and variable; temps in Celsius, negative above 24,000 MSL"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"609f07117417dd41.webp","w":344,"h":101,"cap":"Winds & Temperatures Aloft Chart (FD)"}]},{"h":"Freezing level, stability, convective outlook","obj":"Use these charts to judge icing and convective risk.","pts":["**Freezing level**: colors show the lowest freezing level; used to avoid clear, rime and mixed ice","**Lifted Index** above station: positive = stable, negative = unstable","**K Index** below station: higher = greater thunderstorm likelihood","**Convective outlook**: severe and general convection over 8 days (Day 1, 2, 3, 4-8); severe = tornado, gusts >=50 kt or hail >=1 in","Day 1-3 risk: TSTM (>=10%), MRGL dark green, SLGT yellow, ENH orange, MDT red, HIGH magenta","Day 4-8: 15% and 30% thresholds; else 'predictability too low' or 'potential too low'"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"e4b8a38a3bdad715.webp","w":291,"h":268,"cap":"Freezing Level Charts"},{"f":"521c19fee6881ca9.webp","w":349,"h":91,"cap":"Stability Charts"},{"f":"8c17091add88bf6d.webp","w":352,"h":297,"cap":"Stability Charts"},{"f":"88a8a564523dc8e9.webp","w":290,"h":227,"cap":"Marginal (MRGL) – Dark Green"},{"f":"c6d89ffb5f56dbf0.webp","w":294,"h":267,"cap":"High (HIGH) – Magenta"},{"f":"465fd1d25a9cfdaf.webp","w":297,"h":182,"cap":"Day 4-8 Chart Depictions"}]},{"h":"In-flight advisories","obj":"Distinguish AIRMET, SIGMET, convective SIGMET and PIREPs.","pts":["**AIRMET (WA)**: hazards mainly to light aircraft; every **6 hr**. Sierra = IFR/mountain obscuration; Tango = turbulence, strong surface winds, LLWS; Zulu = icing/freezing levels","**SIGMET (WS)**: non-convective, all aircraft: severe icing, extreme turbulence/CAT not from TS, dust/sand <3 mi vis, volcanic ash; valid **4 hr** (hurricane 6)","**Convective SIGMET (WST)**: severe TS with surface winds >50 kt, hail >=3/4 in, tornadoes; embedded TS; line of TS","Also TS with heavy precip covering >=40% of a 3,000 sq mi or larger area","**PIREPs**: real-time tops, icing, winds, temps, turbulence; fill gaps between stations"],"ce":""},{"h":"Surface observations: ASOS","obj":"Explain manual, automated and augmented observations and ASOS.","pts":["Manual (FAA-certified observer), automated (no human, shows AUTO), augmented (human input, no AUTO)","**ASOS**: primary network; minute-by-minute, hourly METARs and SPECIs (e.g. vis <3 SM)","One-minute obs are averaged (clouds 30 min, vis 10 min); report deterioration faster than improvement","Reports clouds to **12,000 ft**, vis to at least 10 SM, TS/VCTS, FG/BR/HZ, pressure, temp/dew, wind, precip","Limits: can't tell stratus from CB; limited vis restriction ID; no prevailing/sector/tower vis","Service levels A (major, near Class B), B (observers 24 hr), C (part-time tower), D (unattended, always AUTO)"],"ce":""},{"h":"AWOS and ATIS","obj":"Compare AWOS with ASOS and know ATIS.","pts":["**AWOS**: 3 reports per hour at fixed intervals; **cannot issue specials**; one-minute obs by phone/radio","AWOS-A altimeter only; AV adds vis; 1 wind/temp/dew; 2 adds vis; 3 adds precip accumulation and clouds (about ASOS)","AWOS-3P precip type/intensity; 3PT thunderstorm/lightning; 4 adds freezing rain","**ATIS**: continuous recorded non-control info in busier terminals: weather, active runways/approaches, NOTAMs","Updated on significant change with a new letter; some airports have separate arrival/departure ATIS"],"ce":""},{"h":"Wind shear and microbursts","obj":"Teach recognition and avoidance of wind shear.","pts":["Sudden drastic change in wind speed/direction over a small area; LLWS from fronts, TS, inversions, obstructions, upper winds >25 kt","Microburst: 1-2 mi wide, 1,000 ft deep, 5-15 min life, downdrafts to **6,000 fpm**, headwind losses **30-90 kt**","Sequence: headwind (performance up), downdraft, then strong tailwind, risking terrain impact or landing short","Alerting: LLWAS, TDWR, ASR-9 WSP at major airports; many small airports have none","Visual: rain shaft at surface with virga at base, ring of blowing dust","Avoid: no pattern work near active TS, no takeoff with wind shear in the area; use PIREPs and LLWAS"],"ce":"Scenario: Departing into a microburst, airspeed jumps on climb-out. What comes next and why is that a trap?","figs":[{"f":"7398f8cd383c734a.webp","w":405,"h":181,"cap":"Flying through a Microburst"}]},{"h":"Alternates and Go/No Go","obj":"Apply 91.169 and the PAVE/IMSAFE framework to the decision.","pts":["**1-2-3 rule**: 1 hr before/after ETA, 2,000 ft ceiling, 3 SM; otherwise an alternate is needed","Alternate mins: precision 600-2, non-precision 800-2, no approach: VFR descent from MEA","**PAVE**: Pilot, Airplane, enVironment, External pressures; **IMSAFE** for the pilot","Consider overall and recent experience; don't exceed your or the aircraft's abilities","Set personal limits (e.g. TS/squall lines, icing, mod+ turbulence, fog = no go) that meet or exceed FARs and never bend them","Decision making continues all flight; divert or discontinue if needed"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Can a pilot get a legal preflight briefing without calling Flight Service?","Yes. AIM 7-1-2 says pilots can receive a regulatory-compliant briefing without contacting FSS; AC 91-92 guides self-briefing."],["Sky cover amounts for FEW, SCT, BKN, OVC?","FEW 1/8-2/8\nSCT 3/8-4/8\nBKN 5/8-7/8\nOVC 8/8"],["FM vs BECMG vs TEMPO?","FM: rapid significant change, usually within an hour\nBECMG: gradual change over no more than 2 hours\nTEMPO: temporary fluctuations lasting less than an hour"],["Is MOS a legal weather source?","No. It is automated guidance (more or less a TAF for airports without one) and cannot be used as a legal source."],["What will a radar summary chart NOT show?","Clouds and fog without appreciable precipitation, and the heights of cloud tops and bases."],["Low-level sig wx prog: altitude range, forecast times, contents?","Surface to FL240; 12 and 24 hour progs\nFlight categories (IFR/MVFR/VFR), turbulence and altitudes, freezing level in hundreds of feet MSL"],["Decode the winds aloft group 7319.","Direction 73-50 = 23 (230 deg true), speed 19+100 = 119 kt."],["What does a negative Lifted Index mean? A high K Index?","Negative LI = unstable air\nHigher K Index = greater likelihood of thunderstorms"],["Name the three AIRMETs and what each covers.","Sierra: IFR and mountain obscuration\nTango: turbulence, strong surface winds, LLWS\nZulu: icing and freezing levels"],["What triggers a convective SIGMET?","Severe TS (surface winds >50 kt, hail >=3/4 in, tornadoes), embedded TS, a line of TS, or TS with heavy precip over >=40% of a 3,000 sq mi or larger area."],["Key difference between ASOS and AWOS?","ASOS identifies and reports significant changes (issues SPECIs); AWOS sends 3 reports per hour at fixed intervals and cannot issue a special report."],["Describe a typical microburst and the encounter sequence.","1-2 mi diameter, 1,000 ft deep, 5-15 min, downdrafts to 6,000 fpm, 30-90 kt headwind loss\nHeadwind (gain), then downdraft, then tailwind (loss) near the ground"],["What does PAVE stand for?","Pilot, Airplane, enVironment, External pressures."]]},"cfii-iii-a-weather-theory":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"2-1-2":[{"f":"436765d59d9dfc79.webp","w":240,"h":252,"cap":"Pressure Gradient Force (PGF)"}],"2-1-3":[{"f":"8c360d7fb8fbd107.webp","w":720,"h":102,"cap":"Friction"},{"f":"a75dd316399c785c.webp","w":720,"h":102,"cap":"Friction"}],"2-2-2":[{"f":"efbafa377ac97918.webp","w":232,"h":145,"cap":"What is it?"},{"f":"b8d7586e2311aa4b.webp","w":232,"h":177,"cap":"Visual Indicators"}],"5-2-1":[{"f":"9f3253caa58fc5a5.webp","w":296,"h":189,"cap":"A boundary or transition zone between two air masses"},{"f":"511347914a96681d.webp","w":291,"h":185,"cap":"A boundary or transition zone between two air masses"},{"f":"70b18dc60aef1481.webp","w":288,"h":190,"cap":"A boundary or transition zone between two air masses"},{"f":"f77085792634ea38.webp","w":296,"h":188,"cap":"A boundary or transition zone between two air masses"}],"6-1":[{"f":"5658cd5ac12a7f6c.webp","w":404,"h":306,"cap":"Clouds"}],"6-3-1":[{"f":"f213f9123aa55f0e.webp","w":123,"h":85,"cap":"Cloud Forms"},{"f":"6d2bc998a5f29a39.webp","w":109,"h":84,"cap":"Cirri-form"},{"f":"1188ce4b61a82079.webp","w":109,"h":107,"cap":"Cumuli-form"},{"f":"2fd9eee05bb4cf34.webp","w":110,"h":95,"cap":"Cumuli-form"}],"6-3-2":[{"f":"78a1dfbe558ec2df.webp","w":629,"h":153,"cap":"Cloud Levels"}],"6-3-3":[{"f":"f976cd885fb33db0.webp","w":192,"h":166,"cap":"Cirrostratus"},{"f":"39c9acac0cc90b40.webp","w":189,"h":164,"cap":"Cirrostratus"},{"f":"08597ee4cf133e8e.webp","w":188,"h":160,"cap":"Cirrostratus"},{"f":"af87d2c82181efa8.webp","w":174,"h":150,"cap":"Altostratus"},{"f":"4c6f34461e8301ca.webp","w":174,"h":149,"cap":"Altostratus"},{"f":"777c797a629d85cd.webp","w":176,"h":151,"cap":"Altostratus"},{"f":"38acae3170455f1c.webp","w":184,"h":161,"cap":"Altostratus"},{"f":"f4be862841eefbe6.webp","w":176,"h":150,"cap":"Stratus"},{"f":"19803d0d87520434.webp","w":176,"h":152,"cap":"Stratus"},{"f":"f0013b9c91e4fc0c.webp","w":172,"h":148,"cap":"Stratus"},{"f":"7300cf25c7bcd11a.webp","w":180,"h":155,"cap":"Stratus"}]},"pts":null,"intro":"Develop knowledge of weather theory: the student understands how the various weather phenomena form and how they affect flight.","key":["Weather Sources","FSS: 122.2","Go/No Go Decision"],"cards":[{"h":"Atmospheric composition & layers","obj":"Describe what the atmosphere is made of and its five layers.","pts":["4 gases make up 99.998%: **nitrogen, oxygen, argon, carbon dioxide**.","**Troposphere**: surface to about 36,000 ft (varies with temperature); temp decreases with height; almost all weather. Top = **tropopause**.","**Stratosphere**: to 31 miles; temp increases with height (UV absorption); airliners cruise low in it to avoid turbulence/convection.","**Mesosphere** (to ~53 mi, cooling), **thermosphere** (to 430 mi, up to 2,000 C), **exosphere** (to 6,200 mi, satellites).","**Standard atmosphere**: 15 C / 59 F and 29.92 in Hg - an average for all latitudes, seasons, altitudes."],"ce":""},{"h":"Wind: the three forces","obj":"Explain what drives and steers the wind.","pts":["Wind = air in motion relative to the surface; it forms, dissipates and redistributes weather.","**Pressure gradient force**: high to low pressure; speed proportional to isobar/contour gradient.","**Coriolis**: deflects right in N. Hemisphere, at right angles to the wind; proportional to wind speed (double speed = double Coriolis); zero at the equator, max at the poles.","**Friction** slows surface wind (rougher terrain, stronger wind = more); insignificant above the lowest few thousand feet.","Above the friction layer only PGF and Coriolis act on horizontal motion."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"436765d59d9dfc79.webp","w":240,"h":252,"cap":"Pressure Gradient Force (PGF)"},{"f":"8c360d7fb8fbd107.webp","w":720,"h":102,"cap":"Friction"},{"f":"a75dd316399c785c.webp","w":720,"h":102,"cap":"Friction"}]},{"h":"Adverse winds: wind shear & mountain waves","obj":"Recognize wind shear and mountain-wave hazards.","pts":["**Wind shear**: sudden, drastic change in wind speed and/or direction over a very small area; any altitude.","Low-level wind shear: frontal passages, thunderstorms, temperature inversions, strong upper winds (> 25 kt).","Tailwind-to-headwind raises airspeed/performance; headwind-to-tailwind lowers it. Often undetected even when reported.","**Mountain wave**: strong wind + stable air over a ridge - smooth updraft upwind, turbulent downdraft past the crest, waves for miles.","Signs: lenticular clouds, cap clouds, CCSL, ACSL, rotor clouds - may be absent if the air is dry."],"ce":"Scenario: what does a headwind suddenly becoming a tailwind on short final do to you?","figs":[{"f":"efbafa377ac97918.webp","w":232,"h":145,"cap":"What is it?"},{"f":"b8d7586e2311aa4b.webp","w":232,"h":177,"cap":"Visual Indicators"}]},{"h":"Temperature & heat transfer","obj":"Explain how heat moves and how temperature changes with altitude.","pts":["Temperature = average kinetic energy of molecules; the sun is the heat source.","Transfer by **radiation**, **conduction** (warm to cold, in contact), **convection** (within a fluid) - convection dominates because air is a poor conductor.","Water warms and cools more slowly than land - coastal cities have smaller seasonal swings.","Average decrease 2 C per 1,000 ft; **isothermal layer** = constant temp; **inversion** = temp increases with altitude.","Surface-based inversion: clear nights, light wind. Inversion aloft: warm air overrunning cold. Inversions are marked by stability - little turbulence."],"ce":""},{"h":"Moisture & precipitation","obj":"Explain what precipitation needs and what determines its type.","pts":["Ingredients: **water vapor**, **sufficient lifting**, a **growth process**.","Growth: **collision-coalescence** (warm rain) or the **ice crystal process**.","Vertical temperature profile sets the type at the surface.","**Rain**: deep above-freezing layer at the surface. **Snow**: below freezing throughout.","**Ice pellets**: shallow warm layer aloft, deep cold layer at surface. **Freezing rain**: deep warm layer aloft, shallow cold layer at surface."],"ce":""},{"h":"Air masses","obj":"Classify air masses by source region.","pts":["Air mass = large body of air with generally uniform temperature and humidity; origin = **source region**.","Temperature: **arctic** (very deep, cold), **polar** (shallow, cool-cold), **tropical** (warm-hot).","Moisture: **continental** (dry, over land), **maritime** (moist, over water).","cA and cP cold/dry; cT hot/dry; mP cool/moist; mT warm/moist; maritime arctic seldom if ever forms.","Air masses change attributes as they move."],"ce":""},{"h":"Fronts","obj":"Describe each front type and its typical weather.","pts":["Front = boundary between air masses; most weather occurs along fronts; named by which air replaces which.","Surface signs: temperature gradient, converging winds, pressure falling before and rising after passage.","**Cold front**: steep slope, abrupt lift - narrow band of showers/thunderstorms if air is unstable.","**Warm front**: gentle slope - widespread stratiform cloud and precipitation ahead if air is stable.","**Stationary**: clouds/precip in the warm rising air. **Occluded**: faster cold front catches the warm front; weather ahead, along and behind it."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"9f3253caa58fc5a5.webp","w":296,"h":189,"cap":"A boundary or transition zone between two air masses"},{"f":"511347914a96681d.webp","w":291,"h":185,"cap":"A boundary or transition zone between two air masses"},{"f":"70b18dc60aef1481.webp","w":288,"h":190,"cap":"A boundary or transition zone between two air masses"},{"f":"f77085792634ea38.webp","w":296,"h":188,"cap":"A boundary or transition zone between two air masses"}]},{"h":"Cloud formation & cloud forms","obj":"Explain how clouds form and the four basic forms.","pts":["Rising air expands and cools until temp = dewpoint; **lifted condensation level (LCL)** = saturation level.","Lift sources: **orographic**, **frictional** (convergence into lows = clouds; divergence from highs = clearing), **frontal**, **buoyancy**.","**Cirriform**: above 20,000 ft, ice crystals, fair weather.","**Nimbo-form**: about 7,000-15,000 ft with steady precipitation; bases lower as it thickens.","**Cumuliform**: vertical motion, flat base, tops can exceed 60,000 ft. **Stratiform**: featureless low layer, often a few hundred ft AGL."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"5658cd5ac12a7f6c.webp","w":404,"h":306,"cap":"Clouds"},{"f":"f213f9123aa55f0e.webp","w":123,"h":85,"cap":"Cloud Forms"},{"f":"6d2bc998a5f29a39.webp","w":109,"h":84,"cap":"Cirri-form"},{"f":"1188ce4b61a82079.webp","w":109,"h":107,"cap":"Cumuli-form"},{"f":"2fd9eee05bb4cf34.webp","w":110,"h":95,"cap":"Cumuli-form"},{"f":"78a1dfbe558ec2df.webp","w":629,"h":153,"cap":"Cloud Levels"}]},{"h":"Cloud types & their hazards","obj":"Know which cloud types carry icing and turbulence.","pts":["High: **cirrus** and **cirrostratus** - little or no icing/turbulence; **cirrocumulus** - some turbulence and icing.","Middle: **altocumulus** - some turbulence, small icing; **altostratus** - little turbulence, light-moderate icing; **ACSL** - mountain wave sign, not an intensity gauge.","**Nimbostratus**: little turbulence but serious icing near/below freezing.","Low: **cumulus** - some turbulence; **towering cumulus** - very strong turbulence, clear icing above freezing level; **stratocumulus** - some turbulence, possible icing; **stratus** - icing if near freezing.","**Cumulonimbus**: nearly the entire spectrum of hazards, including extreme turbulence; lightning common."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"f976cd885fb33db0.webp","w":192,"h":166,"cap":"Cirrostratus"},{"f":"39c9acac0cc90b40.webp","w":189,"h":164,"cap":"Cirrostratus"},{"f":"08597ee4cf133e8e.webp","w":188,"h":160,"cap":"Cirrostratus"},{"f":"af87d2c82181efa8.webp","w":174,"h":150,"cap":"Altostratus"},{"f":"4c6f34461e8301ca.webp","w":174,"h":149,"cap":"Altostratus"},{"f":"777c797a629d85cd.webp","w":176,"h":151,"cap":"Altostratus"},{"f":"38acae3170455f1c.webp","w":184,"h":161,"cap":"Altostratus"},{"f":"f4be862841eefbe6.webp","w":176,"h":150,"cap":"Stratus"},{"f":"19803d0d87520434.webp","w":176,"h":152,"cap":"Stratus"},{"f":"f0013b9c91e4fc0c.webp","w":172,"h":148,"cap":"Stratus"},{"f":"7300cf25c7bcd11a.webp","w":180,"h":155,"cap":"Stratus"}]},{"h":"Turbulence","obj":"Name the causes and types of turbulence.","pts":["Causes: convective currents, obstructions to wind flow, wind shear.","**Convective**: rising/sinking air; billowy cumulus show it, but it can exist in dry air without clouds.","**Mechanical**: trees, buildings, mountains; mountain waves give violent downdrafts on the immediate lee side.","**Wind shear turbulence**: often across temperature inversion layers.","**Clear air turbulence**: high altitude, cloud-free, wind shear especially around the jet stream core."],"ce":""},{"h":"Thunderstorms","obj":"Explain thunderstorm ingredients, life cycle, types and hazards.","pts":["Ingredients: **sufficient water vapor**, **unstable air** (conditionally unstable), **lifting mechanism** (fronts, lows/troughs, upslope, drylines, outflow boundaries, sea/lake/valley breezes).","Life cycle: **towering cumulus** (updraft) - **mature** (precip reaches surface, downdraft beside updraft) - **dissipating** (downdraft cuts off moisture).","**Single cell** is rare; **multicell** lines can run hundreds of miles - too high to overfly, too long to circumnavigate, too dangerous to go under.","**Supercell**: single rotating updraft, up to 9,000 fpm, can last hours.","Hazards: lightning, microburst, turbulence, icing, hail, rapid altimeter changes, static electricity, tornado."],"ce":""},{"h":"Frost & structural icing","obj":"Distinguish ice types and explain why icing is dangerous.","pts":["Frost forms when cool ground drops air below the dewpoint below freezing; aircraft must be free of frost before flight.","**Rime**: rough, milky, opaque; small droplets freeze instantly; colder temps, low liquid water content.","**Clear**: glossy, large droplets freeze slowly; warmer, higher LWC; more hazardous - disrupts airflow more, hard to see and to remove.","**Mixed**: hazard similar to clear.","Ice destroys smooth airflow (more drag, less lift) and degrades engine performance; its weight is insignificant compared to the airflow disruption.","Expect icing near 0 C in visible moisture; clear icing very rapid between 0 and -15 C."],"ce":""},{"h":"Fog & mist","obj":"Identify fog types and how they form.","pts":["**Fog**: surface droplets reducing visibility below 5/8 SM. **Mist**: below 7 SM but at least 5/8 SM.","**Radiation**: clear skies, light wind, moist shallow layer; ground fog burns off soon after sunrise.","**Advection**: moist air over a colder surface; mostly coastal. **Upslope**: stable moist air up terrain, 5-15 kt winds favorable.","**Mountain/valley**: cool air sinks into valleys; densest near sunrise. **Frontal**: rain evaporating into cold air, mostly warm fronts.","**Steam fog**: very cold air over warm water - expect convective turbulence. **Freezing fog**: supercooled droplets freeze on surfaces."],"ce":""},{"h":"Obstructions to visibility","obj":"Recognize non-fog visibility hazards.","pts":["**Haze**: stable air, usually a few thousand feet thick (up to 15,000 ft); vis varies facing into/away from the sun.","**Smoke**: can reduce vis to zero, contains toxic compounds like CO; dispersed only by air movement.","**Volcanic ash**: silica rock powder, hard to see at night/IMC; pistons may keep power but severe engine and abrasive damage likely.","**Blowing snow** (6 ft or more, vis < 7 SM); **whiteout** (snow up to 50 ft, vis zero).","**Dust storms**: winds 15 kt+, 3,000-6,000 ft (up to 15,000), vis to zero in seconds; slant-range vis far worse than reported. **Haboob**: dust lifted by thunderstorm downdrafts."],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Name the five atmospheric layers in order and where weather occurs.","Troposphere, stratosphere, mesosphere, thermosphere, exosphere\nAlmost all weather is in the troposphere (surface to ~36,000 ft)"],["Standard atmosphere at sea level?","15 C / 59 F and 29.92 in Hg"],["How does Coriolis force vary with wind speed and latitude?","Directly proportional to wind speed (doubles if speed doubles)\nZero at the equator, maximum at the poles\nDeflects right in the Northern Hemisphere"],["Low-level wind shear is commonly associated with what?","Passing frontal systems\nThunderstorms\nTemperature inversions\nStrong upper-level winds (> 25 kt)"],["Surface-based inversion vs. inversion aloft?","Surface-based: base at the surface; typical over land on clear nights with light wind\nAloft: base above the surface, e.g. warm air overrunning cold surface air\nBoth are very stable"],["Ice pellets vs. freezing rain - what temperature profile causes each?","Ice pellets: shallow above-freezing layer aloft, deep below-freezing layer at the surface\nFreezing rain: deep above-freezing layer aloft, shallow below-freezing layer at the surface"],["Cold front vs. warm front weather?","Cold: steep slope, narrow band of showers/thunderstorms along or just ahead\nWarm: gentle slope, widespread layered (stratiform) clouds and precipitation ahead"],["Which cloud types are a serious icing threat?","Nimbostratus and stratus near/below freezing\nAltostratus (light-moderate in supercooled water)\nTowering cumulus (clear ice above freezing level)\nCumulonimbus"],["What is clear air turbulence?","High-altitude turbulence in cloud-free air associated with wind shear, particularly between the jet stream core and surrounding air"],["Three stages of a thunderstorm and what marks each?","Towering cumulus: strong updraft\nMature: precipitation reaches the surface\nDissipating: downdraft dominates, cutting off moisture"],["Why is clear ice more hazardous than rime?","Disrupts airflow considerably more\nClear, so harder to see and recognize\nSpreads beyond deice/anti-ice coverage, hard to remove"],["Fog vs. mist visibility?","Fog: less than 5/8 SM\nMist: less than 7 SM but at least 5/8 SM"]]},"cfii-iii-b-xc-flight-planning":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"9":[{"f":"199f577848e8c761.webp","w":319,"h":183,"cap":"Airframe Icing"}],"7-1-3":[{"f":"6a86c67599c0b907.webp","w":508,"h":381,"cap":"RNP (Required Navigation Performance)"}],"7-2-2":[{"f":"26a2f0766ab96f0d.webp","w":458,"h":197,"cap":"Standard U.S. RNP Levels"}],"7-2-3":[{"f":"83653277fd027064.webp","w":571,"h":112,"cap":"What it Means for You"}],"7-6-3":[{"f":"6a86c67599c0b907.webp","w":473,"h":355,"cap":"RNP (Required Navigation Performance)"}],"7-7-2":[{"f":"26a2f0766ab96f0d.webp","w":458,"h":197,"cap":"Standard U.S. RNP Levels"}],"7-7-3":[{"f":"83653277fd027064.webp","w":571,"h":112,"cap":"What it Means for You"}],"8-3":[{"f":"7333df893c70e656.webp","w":653,"h":219,"cap":"VOR Minimum Operating Network (VOR MON)"},{"f":"c8ecb8ead58281f8.webp","w":653,"h":219,"cap":"VOR Minimum Operating Network (VOR MON)"}]},"pts":{"task":"III.B CROSS-COUNTRY FLIGHT PLANNING","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one TASK.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of cross-country flight planning by describing the:","items":[{"t":"Regulatory requirements for instrument flight within various types of airspace.","sub":[]},{"t":"Computation of estimated time en route and total fuel requirement for an IFR cross-country flight.","sub":[]},{"t":"Selection and correct interpretation of the current and applicable en route charts, RNAV, DPs, STARs, and standard IAP.","sub":[]},{"t":"Procurement and interpretation of the applicable NOTAM information.","sub":[]},{"t":"Completes and files an IFR flight plan that accurately reflects the conditions of the proposed flight. (Does not have to be filed with ATC.)","sub":[]},{"t":"Demonstrates adequate knowledge of GPS and RAIM capability, when aircraft is so equipped.","sub":[]},{"t":"Demonstrates the ability to recognize wing contamination due to airframe icing.","sub":[]},{"t":"Demonstrates adequate knowledge of the adverse effects of airframe icing during landing phases of flight and corrective actions: pretakeoff, takeoff, and cruise.","sub":[]},{"t":"Demonstrates familiarity with any icing procedures and/or information published by the manufacturer that is specific to the aircraft used on the practical test.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"The student develops knowledge of IFR cross-country planning and can effectively develop and file an IFR flight plan based on current weather and NOTAM information.","key":["Applicable IFR Regs","Choosing a Course & Altitude","Filing a Flight Plan"],"cards":[{"h":"IFR preflight & fuel","obj":"State the preflight action and fuel rules for IFR.","pts":["**91.103** IFR-specific: weather reports/forecasts, fuel requirements, alternatives if the flight can't be completed, known ATC traffic delays.","Any flight: runway lengths, takeoff and landing distances.","**91.167** fuel: to the first airport of intended landing, then to the alternate (if required), then **45 min** at normal cruise."],"ce":""},{"h":"Flight plan & alternate requirements","obj":"Determine when an alternate is needed and what qualifies.","pts":["**91.169**: IFR flight plan includes 91.153 info plus an alternate unless not required.","No alternate needed when from **1 hr before to 1 hr after ETA**, ceiling at least **2,000 ft** and vis at least **3 SM**.","Alternate minimums at ETA: precision approach **600 ft and 2 SM**; non-precision **800 ft and 2 SM**.","No approach at the alternate: weather must allow descent from the MEA, approach and landing in VFR."],"ce":""},{"h":"IFR departure rules","obj":"Explain clearance and takeoff minimum rules.","pts":["**91.173**: no IFR in controlled airspace without a filed IFR flight plan and an appropriate ATC clearance.","**91.175**: 0/0 takeoffs are legal under Part 91.","Recommended: use published takeoff minimums (or approach minimums if none) as a guideline."],"ce":""},{"h":"IFR en route: airspace & minimum altitudes","obj":"Apply the en route equipment and minimum altitude rules.","pts":["**91.135** Class A: IFR with ATC clearance. **AIM 3-2-3** Class B: VOR or TACAN receiver or suitable RNAV.","GPS for IFR must meet TSO-C129, C196, C145 or C146, installed per AC 20-138; un-augmented (C129/C196) needs an alternate means of navigation.","**91.177**: not below Part 95 (MEA, MOCA) or Part 97 altitudes; if none, **2,000 ft** above highest obstacle within 4 NM (mountainous), **1,000 ft** (non-mountainous).","May fly below MEA but not below MOCA when within **22 NM** of the VOR.","Climb to a higher minimum immediately after the point it applies; cross MCA at or above."],"ce":""},{"h":"Cruising altitude, course & reports","obj":"State the altitude, course and reporting rules.","pts":["**91.179**: controlled airspace - assigned altitude (VFR-on-top uses 91.159 VFR altitudes).","Uncontrolled below 18,000 MSL: magnetic course 0-179 **odd** thousands; 180-359 **even** thousands.","**91.181**: fly airway centerline, or the direct course between fixes; maneuvering clear of traffic is allowed.","**91.183** report ASAP: time/altitude at reporting points (only requested points under radar), unforecast weather, other safety info.","Restricted areas: ATC routes you around if active and not released; MOAs - cleared through if IFR separation is available."],"ce":""},{"h":"Route, ETE & fuel planning","obj":"Build the nav log from departure to alternate.","pts":["Record DP, en route fixes (preferred IFR, TEC, airways, direct), STAR/IAP/IAF, courses and distances.","Altitude from: minimum IFR altitude, performance/equipment, winds, temperature, freezing level, icing, tops, turbulence, duration, oxygen.","Use POH Section 5 for climb, cruise TAS/fuel flow; add start/taxi/takeoff fuel; plan a descent point and reduced-power fuel.","ETE plus takeoff time = **ETA**: the time to begin the approach after radio failure with no EFC (91.185); also drives alternate weather/fuel.","Add alternate leg (if required) plus 45 min to get 91.167 minimum fuel."],"ce":""},{"h":"Charts & NOTAMs","obj":"Verify charts and read the NOTAM types.","pts":["Confirm current en route charts and TPPs (DPs, STARs, IAPs) and app effective dates; check changes, Safety Alerts and Charting Notices; Chart User's Guide for symbols.","**NOTAM (D)**: all navaids and public-use aerodromes; aerodrome, airspace, navaids, comms, pointer NOTAMs; includes (U) unverified and (O) other.","**FDC**: regulatory - chart/IAP changes, lasers, TFRs, WAAS/GPS, ADS-B, special notices.","**Center area** FDC NOTAMs filed under the ARTCC (e.g. ZAN for Alaska); **security** NOTAMs under ZZZ.","International NOTAMs not in a briefing unless requested; NTAP discontinued June 2020 - Domestic and International Notices websites now."],"ce":""},{"h":"ICAO IFR flight plan","obj":"Fill out the key ICAO flight plan boxes.","pts":["FSS went ICAO-only in Aug 2019; AIM Appendix 4 details it. File and get clearance before entering controlled airspace (91.173).","**Flight rules**: I, V, Y (starts IFR, changes), Z (starts VFR, changes) - note the change point in the route.","Type of flight optional within US domestic airspace (G = general aviation).","Wake category: **L** = 15,500 lb or less; **M** = above 15,500 to below 300,000; **H** = 300,000 or more.","Equipment: **S** = VHF, VOR, ILS; N = none. Example: S + G (GPS) + R (PBN) with Mode C = **SGR/C**; remarks **PBN/B2C2D2** (RNAV 5, 2, 1)."],"ce":""},{"h":"Filing & cancelling","obj":"Explain how to file, pick up and cancel an IFR plan.","pts":["File at least **30 min** before departure; clearance available from 30 min prior to **2 hr** after proposed departure.","Ground: nearest FSS or ATCT (phone 1-800-WX-BRIEF, radio, app). Air: FSS or ARTCC - don't enter IMC until cleared.","Cancel any time in VFR conditions outside Class A (AIM 5-1-15); VFR flight following can be requested.","Towered airport: closed automatically on landing. Non-towered: pilot cancels by radio or FSS - do it expeditiously."],"ce":""},{"h":"Control sequence","obj":"Walk the facilities used on a towered IFR cross-country.","pts":["FSS (brief, file) - ATIS - Clearance Delivery - Ground (state IFR).","Tower (takeoff) - Departure (radar contact) - ARTCC en route.","Destination ATIS - Approach Control - Tower (landing; closes the plan)."],"ce":""},{"h":"PBN: RNAV vs. RNP","obj":"Explain performance-based navigation and nav specs.","pts":["**PBN** defines required performance (accuracy) for airspace, not equipment; any approved system that meets it may be used.","Nav spec number = lateral accuracy in NM, 95% of the time (RNAV 1 = within 1 NM).","**RNAV**: point-to-point; RNAV 1 for SIDs/STARs, RNAV 2 for Q/T routes; domestic uses RNAV mostly because radar does the monitoring.","**RNP** = RNAV + onboard performance monitoring and alerting.","US levels: RNP 2 en route, RNP 1 terminal, **RNP APCH** 1 NM scaling to 0.3 on final, RNP AR down to 0.1 (OpsSpecs/LOA)."],"ce":"Title trap: 'RNAV' in 'RNAV (GPS) RWY 27' names the method, not the spec - its spec is RNP APCH.","figs":[{"f":"6a86c67599c0b907.webp","w":473,"h":355,"cap":"RNP (Required Navigation Performance)"},{"f":"26a2f0766ab96f0d.webp","w":458,"h":197,"cap":"Standard U.S. RNP Levels"},{"f":"83653277fd027064.webp","w":571,"h":112,"cap":"What it Means for You"}]},{"h":"RAIM","obj":"Explain GPS integrity monitoring and preflight checks.","pts":["**RAIM** = receiver verifies satellite signal integrity; it makes un-augmented GPS RNP-capable.","Needs **5 satellites**, or **4 + baro-aiding**; fault exclusion needs 6 (or 5 + baro).","Alerts: not enough satellites, or error exceeds the limit for the current phase of flight.","Un-augmented GPS: preflight RAIM prediction required (SAPT, receiver utility, FSS); predicted loss **> 5 min** - delay, cancel or re-route.","Alternate means of nav required; it need not be monitored while RAIM is available, but must be once RAIM is lost."],"ce":""},{"h":"WAAS & GPS preflight","obj":"State what WAAS changes and the GPS preflight check.","pts":["**WAAS**: ground stations compute corrections, broadcast via geostationary satellites - better accuracy and integrity.","TSO-C145/C146: no RAIM prediction when WAAS is available; no alternate means of nav required (GPS-only filing).","WAAS out (check NOTAMs): receiver reverts to un-augmented - RAIM check required; equipment carriage rule still doesn't apply.","Alternate planning: use **LNAV or circling** minimums, not LPV; vertically guided minima may be flown on arrival if WAAS is available.","Check: Am I approved (chart PBN box vs. AFM)? Will it load (current database, procedure by name)? Will integrity be there?"],"ce":""},{"h":"VOR Minimum Operating Network","obj":"Explain the VOR MON backup.","pts":["NAS moving to PBN; VORs reduced from 896 to 590 by 2030.","Lets aircraft that lost GPS (or have none) navigate VOR to VOR to a **MON airport** and fly a conventional approach (ILS, LOC, VOR).","A MON airport is assured within **100 NM**.","New low service volume: **70 NM from 5,000 to 18,000 ft ATH**; near-continuous coverage above 5,000 ft AGL.","New DME service volume: up to 12,900 ft ATH to line-of-sight distance."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"7333df893c70e656.webp","w":653,"h":219,"cap":"VOR Minimum Operating Network (VOR MON)"},{"f":"c8ecb8ead58281f8.webp","w":653,"h":219,"cap":"VOR Minimum Operating Network (VOR MON)"}]},{"h":"Airframe icing","obj":"Recognize icing and respond.","pts":["Be alert in visible moisture and temps near freezing (-10 to +2 C); watch the windscreen and wings (flashlight at night).","Effects cumulative: less thrust and lift, more drag and weight, higher stall speed.","**1/2 inch** of ice can cut lift by 50% and increase frictional drag by 50%.","Use deice/anti-ice per manufacturer; leave the precipitation or go to above-freezing altitude (not necessarily lower); else land at nearest suitable airport."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"199f577848e8c761.webp","w":319,"h":183,"cap":"Airframe Icing"}]}],"recall":[["When is an alternate NOT required (91.169)?","From 1 hr before to 1 hr after ETA, forecast ceiling at least 2,000 ft AND visibility at least 3 SM"],["Standard alternate minimums?","Precision: 600 ft and 2 SM\nNon-precision: 800 ft and 2 SM\nNo approach: descent from MEA, approach and landing in VFR"],["No published minimum IFR altitude - how low can you go?","Mountainous: 2,000 ft above highest obstacle within 4 NM of course\nNon-mountainous: 1,000 ft within 4 NM"],["When may you fly below the MEA?","Down to the MOCA, when within 22 NM of the VOR concerned"],["What must be reported under 91.183?","Time and altitude over reporting points (only those requested while in radar contact)\nUnforecast weather\nAny other safety-of-flight information"],["Why does the ETA on the nav log matter beyond fuel?","With radio failure and no EFC, it is when you begin the approach (91.185)\nIt also sets the window for the alternate weather requirement"],["What is a (U) NOTAM vs. an (O) NOTAM?","(U): unverified, from a source other than airport management\n(O): other info not meeting NOTAM criteria but useful to operations"],["Decode SGR/C and PBN/B2C2D2.","S = VHF radio, VOR, ILS; G = IFR GPS; R = PBN capable; /C = Mode C\nB2 = RNAV 5, C2 = RNAV 2, D2 = RNAV 1"],["Who closes an IFR flight plan at a non-towered airport?","The pilot - by radio with ATC or by calling FSS, expeditiously"],["RNAV (GPS) vs. RNAV (RNP) approach title?","RNAV (GPS): fly it - spec is RNP APCH\nRNAV (RNP): RNP AR, special authorization required"],["How many satellites does RAIM need?","5, or 4 plus baro-aiding\nFault exclusion: 6, or 5 plus baro-aiding"],["Planning a WAAS-GPS alternate - which minimums?","LNAV or circling minimums (not LPV)\nVertically guided minima may still be flown on arrival if WAAS is available"],["What does the VOR MON guarantee?","A MON airport with a conventional approach within 100 NM\nNear-continuous VOR coverage above 5,000 ft AGL"],["Picking up ice - what do you do?","Use deice/anti-ice equipment\nExit the precipitation or climb/descend to above-freezing air\nIf unable, land at the nearest suitable airport"]]},"cfii-iii-c-instrument-check":{"req":false,"ofigs":{},"pts":{"task":"III.C INSTRUMENT FLIGHT DECK CHECK","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one TASK.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of an instrument flight deck check by describing the reasons for the check and the detection of defects that could affect safe instrument flight. The check shall include:","items":[{"t":"Communications equipment.","sub":[]},{"t":"Navigation equipment.","sub":[]},{"t":"Magnetic compass.","sub":[]},{"t":"Heading indicator/horizontal situation indicator/remote magnetic indicator.","sub":[]},{"t":"Attitude indicator.","sub":[]},{"t":"Altimeter.","sub":[]},{"t":"Turn-and-slip indicator/turn coordinator.","sub":[]},{"t":"Vertical-speed indicator.","sub":[]},{"t":"Airspeed indicator.","sub":[]},{"t":"Outside air temperature.","sub":[]},{"t":"Clock.","sub":[]},{"t":"Pitot heat.","sub":[]},{"t":"Electronic flight instrument display.","sub":[]},{"t":"Traffic awareness/warning/avoidance system.","sub":[]},{"t":"Terrain awareness/warning/alert system.","sub":[]},{"t":"FMS.","sub":[]},{"t":"Automatic pilot.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"The student develops knowledge of preflight instrument checks and has an effective preflight check for the instruments.","key":["Develop a Pattern","Stick to the Pattern"],"cards":[{"h":"Comm & nav equipment","obj":"Check radios and nav receivers before IFR flight.","pts":["Lost comm under IFR may be an emergency (91.185); check antennas, use all radios, request a radio check if needed.","Transponder standby, reply light on during warm-up (or per manufacturer).","**VOR**: 91.171 check within **30 days** - record date, place, bearing error, sign.","**DME**: verify distance; **ILS**: if a LOC is on the field, tune, identify, note indication.","**GPS**: IFR approved, certified, current database; run start-up/self-test; check RAIM."],"ce":""},{"h":"Compass, heading & attitude","obj":"Check the heading and attitude instruments during taxi.","pts":["**Compass**: full of fluid, moves freely, correct on known headings (runways, taxiways).","**HI / HSI / RMI**: correct on known headings during taxi.","**Attitude indicator**: allow 5 min gyro spin-up (or as applicable); set horizon bar.","AI is unreliable if it shows more than **5 degrees** of pitch or bank during taxi."],"ce":""},{"h":"Pitot-static instruments","obj":"Check the altimeter, VSI and airspeed.","pts":["Logbook: altimeter and static system check within **24 months** (91.411); static ports clear.","**Altimeter**: set current setting; error vs field elevation **> 75 ft** requires maintenance.","Conservative practice: add any altimeter error to the MDA or DH.","**VSI**: note level indication. **Airspeed**: alive and increasing early in the takeoff roll."],"ce":""},{"h":"Turn coordinator, OAT, clock, pitot heat","obj":"Finish the steam-gauge check.","pts":["**Turn coordinator / turn-and-slip**: in taxi turns the ball moves freely to the outside of the turn.","**OAT**: correct indication. **Clock**: working, correct time.","**Pitot heat**: turn on and verify per manufacturer."],"ce":""},{"h":"Electronic displays & automation","obj":"Check glass, traffic/terrain, FMS and autopilot.","pts":["**EFIS**: on power-up, instruments displayed properly, cautions/warnings normal; failures are obvious (red X).","**Traffic & terrain**: verify per manufacturer on power-up.","**FMS**: operation per manufacturer; adequate satellite coverage, check RAIM if needed.","**Autopilot**: check per manufacturer, including that it can be disconnected."],"ce":""}],"recall":[["VOR check requirements for IFR?","Within the preceding 30 days (91.171)\nRecord date, place, bearing error, and sign"],["Altimeter reads 90 ft off field elevation with current setting. Go?","No - more than 75 ft error requires maintenance\nUnder 75 ft: record it and add it to MDA/DH"],["Which instruments require the 91.411 logbook check?","Altimeter and static system (affects altimeter, VSI, airspeed) - within 24 months"]]},"cfii-iv-a-maneuver-lesson":{"req":true,"ofigs":{},"pts":{"task":"IV.A MANEUVER LESSON","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one maneuver from AREAS OF OPERATION VI through IX and ask the applicant to present a preflight lesson on the selected maneuver as the lesson would be taught to a student. Previously developed lesson plans from the applicant’s library may be used.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the selected maneuver by:","items":[{"t":"Using a lesson plan that includes all essential items to make an effective and organized presentation.","sub":[]},{"t":"Stating the objective.","sub":[]},{"t":"Giving an accurate, comprehensive oral description of the maneuver, including the elements and associated common errors.","sub":[]},{"t":"Using instructional aids, as appropriate.","sub":[]},{"t":"Describing the recognition, analysis, and correction of common errors.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"The examiner selects at least one maneuver from Areas VI-IX and has the applicant present a preflight lesson on it as it would be taught to a student; previously developed lesson plans may be used.","key":["Lessons for this task are in sections VI through IX"],"cards":[{"h":"Preflight maneuver lesson","obj":"Present a preflight lesson on an examiner-selected maneuver from Areas VI-IX.","pts":["Use a lesson plan with all essential items for an effective, organized presentation.","**State the objective.**","Give an accurate, comprehensive description including the **elements** and **common errors**.","Use **instructional aids** as appropriate.","Describe **recognition, analysis and correction** of common errors."],"ce":"Bring your own lesson plans - the PTS allows previously developed plans from your library."}],"recall":[["What must a preflight maneuver lesson include (PTS)?","Lesson plan with all essential items\nObjective stated\nDescription with elements and common errors\nInstructional aids\nRecognition, analysis, correction of errors"]]},"cfii-v-a-atc-clearances":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-2-1":[{"f":"8f987fee135f5720.webp","w":578,"h":133,"cap":"File a Flight Plan"}]},"pts":{"task":"V.A AIR TRAFFIC CONTROL CLEARANCES","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one TASK.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of air traffic control clearances by describing:","items":[{"t":"Pilot and controller responsibilities to include tower, en route control, and clearance void times.","sub":[]},{"t":"Correct and timely copying of an ATC clearance.","sub":[]},{"t":"Ability to comply with the clearance.","sub":[]},{"t":"Correct and timely read-back of an ATC clearance, using standard phraseology.","sub":[]},{"t":"Correct interpretation of an ATC clearance and, when necessary, request for clarification, verification, or change.","sub":[]},{"t":"Setting of communication and navigation frequencies in compliance with an ATC clearance.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Student understands the ATC communication system, the IFR clearance format, and how to obtain, copy, and read back a clearance.","key":["Pilot / Controller Responsibilities","C-R-A-F-T","Always Clarify, if Unsure"],"cards":[{"h":"What a clearance is","obj":"Define an ATC clearance and when one is required.","pts":["**Clearance**: ATC authorization, to prevent collision between known aircraft, to proceed under specified traffic conditions in controlled airspace","**FAR 91.173**: no IFR in controlled airspace without a filed flight plan AND an ATC clearance","File (FSS, ForeFlight, etc.) - plan goes to the ARTCC computer","Computer sends flight strips to tower, departure radar facility, and the first Center sector","Pick up: \"(Facility), (Callsign), ready to copy IFR to (destination)\""],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"8f987fee135f5720.webp","w":578,"h":133,"cap":"File a Flight Plan"}]},{"h":"CRAFT - the standard clearance","obj":"Copy and read back a clearance in CRAFT order.","pts":["**C**learance limit: usually destination; sometimes a short-range fix plus frequency for the long-range clearance","**R**oute: DP, en route fixes, arrival - often \"as filed\"; notify ATC if unable (e.g., no RNAV)","**A**ltitude: initial altitude to maintain","**F**requency: departure control; **T**ransponder: squawk code","Pre-fill CRAFT, line out/update; read back promptly in CRAFT order, note \"read back correct\""],"ce":"Scenario: you missed the route - tell ATC what you missed, then read back those items again."},{"h":"\"Cruise\" clearance","obj":"Explain the block of airspace a cruise clearance assigns.","pts":["Rare; assigns a block from the minimum IFR altitude up to the cruise altitude","Climb, descent, and level-off within the block at pilot's discretion","Once you descend and verbally report leaving an altitude, you may not climb back without further clearance"],"ce":""},{"h":"Interpretation, clarification & change","obj":"Know what to expect and when to question a clearance.","pts":["Interpret using CRAFT, the Pilot/Controller Glossary, en route fix locations, and DPs","Once accepted, you must comply with the clearance","You may request a different clearance","Always request clarification/amendment if not fully understood or unsafe"],"ce":""},{"h":"Obtaining a clearance - towered vs non-towered","obj":"Describe where to get a clearance at each airport type.","pts":["**Towered**: Clearance Delivery if available, otherwise Ground","**Non-towered**: check the Chart Supplement for the method","Options: FSS by phone, ATC by radio/phone (if published), RCO to FSS, UNICOM may contact ATC","Or depart VFR and pick up the IFR clearance airborne"],"ce":""},{"h":"Clearance void time","obj":"Explain void time and what happens if you miss it.","pts":["Issued at uncontrolled fields: time by which you must depart; reserves your space in the system","Clearance auto-cancels if not airborne by void time; contact ATC with intentions (new clearance or cancel)","No contact within **30 min** after void time = search and rescue; other IFR traffic suspended until then","Departing at/after void time: no IFR separation, possible 91.173 violation","Clearances available 30 min before to 2 hr after filed departure time; can ask ATC to hold longer"],"ce":""},{"h":"Pilot responsibilities","obj":"State the pilot's duties regarding clearances.","pts":["File, obtain clearance, acknowledge receipt and understanding","Request clarification/amendment if not understood or unsafe","Contact ATC if unable to meet climb gradients; if unable on a DP climb gradient, depart VMC","Promptly comply except in emergency; advise ATC ASAP and get an amended clearance if deviating","See and avoid in VMC; PIC is final authority (FAR 91.3) and may deviate in emergency"],"ce":""},{"h":"Controller responsibilities","obj":"State ATC's duties regarding clearances.","pts":["Issue appropriate clearances for the operation","Assign altitudes above minimum IFR altitudes in controlled airspace","Ensure acknowledgement/read back; correct incorrect, distorted, or incomplete read backs","Include DPs in the clearance when necessary; specify takeoff direction and obtain pilot concurrence on terrain/obstruction clearance"],"ce":""},{"h":"Tower Enroute Control (TEC)","obj":"Explain TEC and its advantages.","pts":["Control of IFR en route traffic in delegated airspace between two or more adjacent approach facilities","Flight beneath the en route structure, routed by towers and departure/approach controllers","Usually below 10,000' and flights under 2 hours","Advantages: abbreviated filing, fewer delays, reduced separation and communications","TEC routes are in the Chart Supplement"],"ce":""},{"h":"Setting com & nav frequencies (technique)","obj":"Set up the cockpit per the clearance before departure.","pts":["Com 1: Tower active, Departure standby; Com 2: arrival frequencies","Bug initial heading and altitude; enter/check route in GPS and activate","Tune/check first station; set OBS to initial course","Secondary NAV: approach facility (e.g., ILS) for an emergency return in IMC","Transponder on assigned code, standby until takeoff, then ALT"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["What does CRAFT stand for?","Clearance limit\nRoute\nAltitude\nFrequency (departure)\nTransponder code"],["What two things does FAR 91.173 require before operating IFR in controlled airspace?","A filed IFR flight plan and an ATC clearance."],["You miss your void time at a non-towered field. What must you do, and what happens if you don't?","Contact ATC and advise intentions (new clearance or cancel IFR).\nNo contact within 30 min after void time results in search and rescue."],["How early and how late is a filed clearance normally available?","30 minutes prior to the filed departure time and 2 hours after it (ATC can be asked to hold it longer)."],["List ways to get a clearance at a non-towered airport.","FSS by phone\nATC by radio/phone if published in the Chart Supplement\nRCO to FSS / UNICOM relay\nDepart VFR and pick it up airborne"],["If you cannot meet a departure procedure's climb gradient, what are your options?","Contact ATC; if unable to maintain the DP climb gradient, you must depart under VMC."],["What is TEC and where do you find the routes?","Tower Enroute Control - IFR flight beneath the en route structure via adjacent approach facilities, usually below 10,000' and under 2 hours.\nRoutes are in the Chart Supplement."]]},"cfii-v-b-procedures-clearances":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-2":[{"f":"96c19d735d578275.webp","w":432,"h":341,"cap":"SIDs (Standard Instrument Departure)"},{"f":"19d2dc440528095a.webp","w":462,"h":711,"cap":"Graphic ODP Example:"}],"1-3-2":[{"f":"85241c77297be655.webp","w":444,"h":679,"cap":"Transition Routes"}],"1-3-3":[{"f":"262f0acf7c5a8536.webp","w":721,"h":294,"cap":"Understanding the Charts"},{"f":"f7951704a1ff24a9.webp","w":721,"h":294,"cap":"Understanding the Charts"}],"3-2":[{"f":"97936c78768c83d3.webp","w":672,"h":457,"cap":"Pilot Responsibilities when Choosing / Planning a STAR"},{"f":"a6bf408505adeba9.webp","w":393,"h":371,"cap":"Pilot Responsibilities when Choosing / Planning a STAR"},{"f":"29463b9815cc03a5.webp","w":393,"h":371,"cap":"Pilot Responsibilities when Choosing / Planning a STAR"}],"5-2":[{"f":"073a8fd30cba006f.webp","w":18,"h":12,"cap":"Pilot Reports"},{"f":"26b53b658f8230a1.webp","w":19,"h":12,"cap":"Pilot Reports"}]},"pts":{"task":"V.B COMPLIANCE WITH DEPARTURE, EN ROUTE, AND ARRIVAL PROCEDURES AND","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one TASK.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements related to compliance with departure, en route, and arrival procedures and clearances by describing:","items":[{"t":"Selection and use of current and appropriate navigation publications.","sub":[]},{"t":"Pilot and controller responsibilities with regard to DPs, En Route Low and High Altitude Charts, and STARs.","sub":[]},{"t":"Selection and use of appropriate communications frequencies.","sub":[]},{"t":"Selection and identification of the navigation aids.","sub":[]},{"t":"Accomplishment of the appropriate checklist items.","sub":[]},{"t":"Pilot’s responsibility for compliance with vectors and also altitude, airspeed, climb, descent, and airspace restrictions.","sub":[]},{"t":"Pilot’s responsibility for the interception of courses, radials, and bearings appropriate to the procedure, route, or clearance.","sub":[]},{"t":"Procedures to be used in the event of two-way communications failure.","sub":[]},{"t":"The uses of the multifunction display and other graphical navigational displays, if installed, to monitor position track, wind drift, and other parameters to maintain situational awareness and desired flightpath.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Student understands how to operate in departure (DP), en route, and arrival (STAR) areas and can apply it in the airplane.","key":["Write down ATC Instructions","Pre-Approach Brief","Current Charts"],"cards":[{"h":"Departure procedures: ODP vs SID","obj":"Distinguish the two types of DPs.","pts":["**DP**: preplanned IFR transition from terminal area to en route structure","**ODP**: obstacle clearance only; only at airports with instrument approaches; one per runway; no ATC climb requirements","**SID**: increases terminal capacity, reduces communication and environmental impact, also provides obstacle clearance","SIDs are used at large, busy airports and nearby high-traffic airports"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"96c19d735d578275.webp","w":432,"h":341,"cap":"SIDs (Standard Instrument Departure)"},{"f":"19d2dc440528095a.webp","w":462,"h":711,"cap":"Graphic ODP Example:"},{"f":"85241c77297be655.webp","w":444,"h":679,"cap":"Transition Routes"},{"f":"262f0acf7c5a8536.webp","w":721,"h":294,"cap":"Understanding the Charts"},{"f":"f7951704a1ff24a9.webp","w":721,"h":294,"cap":"Understanding the Charts"}]},{"h":"Planning a departure (AIM 5-2-9h)","obj":"List pilot planning steps for an IFR departure.","pts":["Consider terrain/obstructions; is an ODP or suitable SID available, and any restrictions?","Can obstacle clearance be maintained visually, or fly the ODP? Consider engine loss/degraded climb","**DVA**: ATC random vectors until above MVA/MIA in lieu of ODP/SID; noted in the TPP Takeoff Mins/ODP section","Check Takeoff Obstacle Notes for the runway","Default: 35' above departure end, 400' before turning, at least 200 ft/NM to minimum IFR altitude"],"ce":""},{"h":"ODPs - finding and flying them","obj":"Locate, file, and fly an ODP.","pts":["In TPP **Section L** (IFR Takeoff Minimums and (Obstacle) Departure Procedures); usually text","Graphic ODPs have \"(OBSTACLE)\" in the title, e.g., GEYSR THREE DEPARTURE (OBSTACLE)","Assigned by ATC only if needed for separation; otherwise flying it is the pilot's choice - almost always safer","Graphic: file by computer code; textual: remarks \"will depart (airport) (runway) via textual ODP\"","If not in your clearance, tell ATC you intend to fly the ODP"],"ce":""},{"h":"SIDs","obj":"Explain SID types, location, and pilot responsibilities.","pts":["In TPP Terminal Charts section (after approaches/airport diagram); text and graphic; legend TPP pg G2 / Chart User's Guide pg 125","Types: Non-RNAV (ground navaids), RNAV (GPS, VOR/DME, DME/DME), Radar (vectors to route/fix)","Transition routes: file the transition; clearance must include departure and transition","Only flown with ATC clearance; if assigned and accepted, it must be flown","Accept only if you meet performance and nav accuracy, have the chart, and understand it; RNAV SID must be in a current database"],"ce":"Can't or won't fly SIDs? Put \"NO SIDs\" in remarks."},{"h":"\"Maintain\" vs \"Climb Via\"","obj":"Interpret SID altitude clearances.","pts":["**Maintain (altitude)**: used when no published crossing restrictions and/or a radar vector SID - climb to and maintain","**Climb Via**: comply with the SID lateral path and published speed/altitude restrictions","Climb via authorizes joining the procedure after departure, or climbing from an assigned altitude to the next waypoint's altitude","\"Climb via\" then \"maintain X\": all intermediate altitude restrictions removed - climb directly","Unsure? Query the controller"],"ce":""},{"h":"Departure communications & charts","obj":"Sequence frequencies and prepare current charts.","pts":["Clearance Delivery (or Ground) -> Tower -> Departure/Center (the F in CRAFT)","On each handoff: copy, acknowledge, contact with call sign and altitude (\"Cessna 514GS, 5,000 feet\")","Radar SID lost-comm procedures take precedence over the standard ones","Verify current pubs and FDC NOTAMs; carry procedures for route, alternates, every intended airport","What you file may not be what you're cleared - mark likely procedures, keep charts reachable"],"ce":""},{"h":"Departure safety & efficiency","obj":"Apply safety and efficiency habits to departures.","pts":["Set and keep personal minimums - legal is not necessarily safe","Verify climb gradients, altitudes, airspeeds, obstacles and terrain","Stay ahead: review procedures, know what's next, ask for help","SOPs; file ODPs/SIDs or NO SIDs; know what you'll say before keying the mic"],"ce":""},{"h":"En route charts","obj":"Describe low and high en route charts.","pts":["Roadmaps for IFR: navaids, courses, altitudes; connect end of DP to start of STAR","**Low**: below 18,000' MSL - airways, RNAV routes, controlled airspace limits, VHF navaids, OROCA, SUA, MTRs","Low chart airports: those with an instrument approach or a 3,000' minimum hard-surface runway","**High**: above 18,000' MSL - jet routes, RNAV Q-routes, NRS waypoints, selected airports","Legend: Aeronautical Chart User's Guide pgs 59-89"],"ce":""},{"h":"En route pilot rules: vectors, altitude, airspeed","obj":"State pilot requirements en route.","pts":["Maintain MEAs, MCAs, MAAs, reporting points on airways; use current charts","Vectors (AIM 5-5-6): turn promptly, maintain heading; question incorrect headings/altitudes","Controlled airspace: maintain ATC altitude; VFR-on-top: FAR 91.159","Uncontrolled below 18,000: 0-179 odd thousands, 180-359 even thousands","Assigned speed: hold within +/-10 kt; advise if unable; report TAS change of 5% or 10 kt (greater)"],"ce":""},{"h":"Climbs & descents (AIM 4-4-10)","obj":"Explain expected rates and ATC phraseology.","pts":["Execute upon receipt; optimum rate until 1,000' from target, then 500-1,500 fpm (helps prevent RAs)","Can't climb/descend at 500 fpm? Advise ATC","**Immediately**: urgency to avoid an imminent situation","**Pilot's discretion**: start when you wish, any rate, may level temporarily - but can't return to a vacated altitude","**Expedite**: best rate without exceptional handling change; if unable, advise"],"ce":""},{"h":"Airspace restrictions & clearance compliance","obj":"Stay in protected airspace and comply with clearances.","pts":["**FAR 91.181**: fly the airway centerline; other routes, the direct course between fixes","Federal airways are generally 4 NM each side of centerline (8 NM total); lead turns if needed","**FAR 91.123**: no deviation without amended clearance, emergency, or TCAS RA response","Question clearances believed incorrect; advise if one would violate a CFR; don't accept RNAV routes without RNAV","**FAR 91.3**: emergency deviation allowed; report and get a new clearance ASAP"],"ce":""},{"h":"ATC en route responsibilities","obj":"Know what ATC provides en route.","pts":["Issue clearances; assign altitudes at/above minimum IFR altitudes","May vector below MEA using Minimum Vectoring Altitudes (pilots don't have these)","Vectors for separation, noise abatement, operational advantage, or on pilot request","VFR aircraft not at an assigned altitude may be vectored at any altitude - terrain is the pilot's responsibility","Verify readbacks; en route separation and traffic advisories"],"ce":""},{"h":"En route comms, charts, safety","obj":"Manage handoffs, charts, fuel and diversions.","pts":["Climb/descent call: \"(Center), (Callsign), (current alt) climbing/descending (assigned alt)\"","Select current charts for the whole route incl. alternates; check Chart Supplement and NOTAMs","Monitor time and fuel: TOC, waypoints, TOD; compare actual vs planned; balance tanks","Know diversion airports and en route weather; don't fly into icing if not certified; avoid storms; keep personal minimums"],"ce":""},{"h":"STARs","obj":"Explain what a STAR is and how to plan one.","pts":["IFR routing from en route structure to the terminal area; starts en route but doesn't reach the pavement","Ends at a fix/navaid connecting to (or vectors to) an approach; one STAR can serve multiple airports","TPP **Section Z**; legend TPP pg G1 / Chart User's Guide pg 124; RNAV or Non-RNAV","Transitions at start (feeders) and/or end (to airports/runways)","Check direction, speeds, altitudes, equipment; file STAR + transition, or \"NO STAR\" in remarks"],"ce":""},{"h":"STAR pilot responsibilities & clearances","obj":"Interpret STAR clearances.","pts":["Pilot accepts or refuses an issued STAR; must possess at least the approved chart (AIM 5-4-1)","RNAV STARs must be retrievable by name from the database","\"Cleared HADLY ONE arrival\" = lateral routing only; altitudes/speeds come later","**Descend Via**: follow STAR routing, altitudes, and speeds; descend at pilot's discretion to depicted waypoint altitude","\"Expect\" altitudes are not part of the clearance until ATC says them"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"97936c78768c83d3.webp","w":672,"h":457,"cap":"Pilot Responsibilities when Choosing / Planning a STAR"},{"f":"a6bf408505adeba9.webp","w":393,"h":371,"cap":"Pilot Responsibilities when Choosing / Planning a STAR"},{"f":"29463b9815cc03a5.webp","w":393,"h":371,"cap":"Pilot Responsibilities when Choosing / Planning a STAR"}]},{"h":"Arrival comms & briefs","obj":"Handle arrival handoffs and brief the approach.","pts":["Center often starts the STAR, then hands off to Approach, then Tower","No approach control: Center clears you, frequency change; call on CTAF, cancel IFR by radio or phone","Pre-Approach Brief **FFIIMMMMS**: Fixes, Frequencies, Inbound course, Identify, Marker beacons, Minutes, Minimums, Missed, Security","**5 T's** at each fix/heading change and GS intercept: Turn, Time, Twist, Throttle, Talk"],"ce":""},{"h":"NAVAIDs (DP, en route, STAR)","obj":"Tune, identify, and change over navaids correctly.","pts":["Record frequencies/waypoints on navlog; tune and identify first navaid before takeoff if in range","Identify on G1000 by the identifier, or listen to Morse via NAV button","Backup: second en route navaid, or departure airport ILS for immediate return","Change from station behind to station ahead at the **COP**; none depicted = midpoint or turn point"],"ce":""},{"h":"Position reports (FAR 91.183, AIM 5-3-2)","obj":"Know when and how to make position reports.","pts":["Non-radar: time and altitude at each designated (compulsory) reporting point; radar: only points requested","Discontinue on \"radar contact\"; resume on \"radar contact lost\" / \"radar service terminated\"","Oceanic: report over every point, even \"on request\"","Items: ID, position, time, altitude, flight plan type, ETA + next point, following point, remarks","Also report unforecast weather and anything affecting safety of flight"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"073a8fd30cba006f.webp","w":18,"h":12,"cap":"Pilot Reports"},{"f":"26b53b658f8230a1.webp","w":19,"h":12,"cap":"Pilot Reports"}]},{"h":"Additional reports (AIM 5-3-3)","obj":"List reports required at all times and when not in radar contact.","pts":["**Always**: vacating an assigned altitude; VFR-on-top altitude change; unable 500 fpm; missed approach","**Always**: TAS change 5%/10 kt; reaching/leaving a holding fix; loss of nav equipment (ID, equipment, impairment, help needed)","**Always**: any safety-of-flight info","**Non-radar**: leaving FAF/OM inbound; estimate error more than 2 minutes; unforecast/hazardous weather"],"ce":""},{"h":"Minimum vs emergency fuel","obj":"Distinguish minimum fuel from a fuel emergency.","pts":["**Minimum fuel** (AIM 5-5-15): can't accept undue delay; advisory only, NOT an emergency, no priority","Say it on initial contact after call sign: \"XYZ Approach, Diamond 4DS, minimum fuel\"","Need priority? Declare an emergency and report fuel remaining in minutes","**Emergency fuel** (Feb 2012 Air Traffic Bulletin): not defined in AIM/FARs; PIC judges must proceed directly due to low fuel - priority required"],"ce":""},{"h":"MFD situational awareness","obj":"Explain how an MFD improves SA.","pts":["Moving map shows position relative to desired course","GPS CDI sensitivity doesn't vary with distance like a VOR (1 dot at 100 NM is far larger than at 10 NM)","Visualize wind drift (heading vs course), often actual wind; ground speed for time/fuel","Also weather, traffic, terrain, time to waypoints, airports, navaids"],"ce":""},{"h":"ADS-B basics","obj":"Explain ADS-B Out/In and services.","pts":["NextGen foundation; broadcasts every second vs radar sweep every 5-12 s; not limited by line-of-sight sites","Automatic, Dependent (GPS/FMS position), Surveillance (3D position/ID), Broadcast","**Out**: GPS position, altitude, ground speed to ground stations/aircraft","**In** (voluntary): **FIS-B** on 978 MHz UAT (weather, SIGMETs, PIREPs, TFRs); **TIS-B** on 1090ES and UAT (traffic)","**ADAPT** deviation: working transponder + encoder; request 24 hr to 1 hr before flight"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Unless specified otherwise, what does departure obstacle clearance assume?","Cross the departure end of runway at least 35' AGL\nClimb to 400' before the initial turn\nClimb at least 200 ft/NM to the minimum IFR altitude"],["What is a DVA and where is it noted?","Diverse Vector Area - ATC may give random radar vectors until above the MVA/MIA, providing obstacle/terrain avoidance in lieu of an ODP or SID.\nNoted in the TPP Takeoff Minimums & (Obstacle) Departure Procedures section."],["Is an ODP mandatory?","Only if assigned by ATC (done only when necessary for separation). Otherwise it is the pilot's choice, though complying is almost always safer."],["You were cleared to \"climb via\" the SID; airborne, ATC says \"maintain 7,000.\" What about the SID's intermediate altitude restrictions?","They are removed - climb directly to 7,000."],["How accurately must you hold an ATC-assigned airspeed, and when must you report a TAS change?","Within +/-10 knots.\nReport when average TAS at cruise varies 5% or 10 knots (whichever is greater) from the flight plan."],["Cleared to climb \"at pilot's discretion\" - what are you allowed to do?","Start when you wish, climb at any rate, level off temporarily at intermediate altitudes.\nOnce you vacate an altitude, you may not return to it."],["What climb/descent rate does the AIM expect approaching an assigned altitude?","Optimum rate until 1,000' from the altitude, then 500-1,500 fpm. Advise ATC if unable to maintain 500 fpm."],["Under FAR 91.123, when may you deviate from an ATC clearance?","When an amended clearance is obtained, an emergency exists, or in response to a TCAS resolution advisory."],["Cleared for a STAR by name with no altitude - what are you cleared for?","Only the lateral routing. Altitude and speed instructions will come later (e.g., a specific altitude or \"descend via\")."],["Are \"expect\" altitudes on a STAR part of your clearance?","No - they let you anticipate, but are not part of the clearance until ATC issues them verbally."],["Recite the 5 T's.","Turn (which way?)\nTime (start timer)\nTwist (rotate OBS)\nThrottle (set as required)\nTalk (report to ATC)"],["What is FFIIMMMMS?","Pre-approach brief: Fixes, Frequencies, Inbound course, Identify, Marker beacons, Minutes, Minimums, Missed, Security."],["What reports are required only when NOT in radar contact?","Leaving the FAF or outer marker inbound\nPrevious time estimate in error by more than 2 minutes\nUnforecast and/or hazardous weather\n(plus compulsory position reports)"],["What must a loss-of-navigation-equipment report include?","Aircraft ID, equipment affected, impairment to IFR operation, and nature/extent of assistance desired from ATC."],["Does \"minimum fuel\" give you priority?","No. It is an advisory that an emergency is possible with delay. For priority, declare an emergency and report fuel remaining in minutes."],["Where is ADS-B Out required (FAR 91.225)?","Class A, B, C; above the ceiling of B/C up to 10,000' MSL\nWithin 30 NM of Appendix D airports up to 10,000' MSL\nClass E at/above 10,000' MSL excluding at/below 2,500' AGL\nAt/above 3,000' MSL over the Gulf of Mexico within 12 NM of the coast"],["FIS-B vs TIS-B?","FIS-B: weather/flight info, 978 MHz UAT only.\nTIS-B: traffic on transponder aircraft, available to 1090ES and UAT users."]]},"cfii-v-x-atc-system":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-4-2":[{"f":"f242439a1dfca0eb.webp","w":664,"h":414,"cap":"Where do they Operate?"}]},"pts":null,"intro":"Big-picture understanding of the ATC system components; foundation for V.A and V.B.","key":["FSS","Tower","TRACON","ARTCC"],"cards":[{"h":"FSS and tower","obj":"Describe FSS services and the tower positions in an IFR flight.","pts":["**FSS**: briefings, flight plans, relay clearances, originate NOTAMs, broadcast weather","Contact: 1-800-WX BRIEF, radio, RCO, GCO, through certain navaids (see Chart Supplement)","Clearance Delivery at busier airports; else Ground does clearances and taxi","Tower won't issue takeoff clearance until departure issues a release","IFR towers have vectoring authority; VFR towers accept inbound IFR but can't vector"],"ce":""},{"h":"TRACON and ARTCC","obj":"Describe terminal and en route radar facilities.","pts":["**TRACON** (\"Departure\"/\"Approach\"): link between airport and en route; normally 30 NM, up to 10,000'","Releases departures, controls terminal traffic, can assign MVAs below procedural altitudes","Radar: ASR (primary) + ARTS (transponder) combined; data block = ID, type, altitude, airspeed","**ARTCC**: separation in en route structure, above 10,000', covers several states; ARSR radar; high/low sectors"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"f242439a1dfca0eb.webp","w":664,"h":414,"cap":"Where do they Operate?"}]},{"h":"Control sequences","obj":"Walk through the facilities used on a typical IFR flight.","pts":["**Towered**: FSS -> ATIS -> Clearance Delivery -> Ground -> Tower -> Departure -> Center -> ATIS -> Approach -> Tower","Tower hands you to departure once the transponder \"tags up\"; after landing, tower closes the IFR plan","**Non-towered**: FSS -> clearance + void time -> Center after takeoff -> Approach","Non-towered arrival: cancel IFR airborne if VMC; if IMC, cancel on the ground"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Why might you be \"holding for release\" at a towered airport?","Tower can't clear you for takeoff until the departure controller (TRACON) can fit you into the departure flow and issues a release."],["Who closes your IFR flight plan at a towered vs non-towered destination?","Towered: tower closes it after landing.\nNon-towered: you cancel - airborne if VMC, or on the ground (FSS, Approach, ARTCC) if IMC."]]},"cfii-v-xx-low-visibility-taxi":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"2-2":[{"f":"532ba6f0991a506f.webp","w":464,"h":495,"cap":"Geographic Position Markings"}],"3-2":[{"f":"97ff356edc0de7e3.webp","w":720,"h":193,"cap":"Low Visibility Taxi Charts"}]},"pts":null,"intro":"Student understands taxi procedures, lighting, markings, signs, and taxi charts for low visibility (LVO/SMGCS, below 1,200' RVR).","key":["Taxi Slow","Focus Outside","Low Visibility Taxi Chart"],"cards":[{"h":"Taxiing in low visibility (AIM 4-3-19)","obj":"Apply safe low-visibility taxi habits.","pts":["Full attention on the moving aircraft; taxi slowly, eyes outside","Sterile cockpit: withhold checklists and nonessential talk until stopped, brakes set","Tell ATC at the first sign of disorientation - tower may not see you"],"ce":""},{"h":"LVO/SMGCS lighting & markings","obj":"Identify low-visibility lights and markings.","pts":["AC 120-57: plan for ops below 1,200' RVR; two levels: 1,200-600' and below 600' RVR","Taxiway edge: steady blue; centerline: steady green; clearance bar: 3 steady yellow in-pavement","Runway guard lights: flashing yellow (elevated pair or in-pavement row) at runway hold position","**Stop bar**: steady red row; turned off with lead-on lights on after ATC clearance","Geographic position marking: pink circle with number/letter, white inner and black outer ring"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"532ba6f0991a506f.webp","w":464,"h":495,"cap":"Geographic Position Markings"}]},{"h":"Airport requirements by RVR","obj":"Contrast below-1,200' and below-600' RVR requirements.","pts":["<1,200': taxiway edge or centerline lights; runway guard lights; painted hold markings; guidance signs","<600': centerline lights (edge on curves); stop bars + green lead-on lights; clearance bar lights at holds","<600' non-movement area: centerline lights or a \"follow me\" vehicle plan","Elevated guard lights: no centerline lights and width <=150'; in-pavement: centerline lights, >150', or ILS stop bar"],"ce":""},{"h":"Low visibility taxi charts","obj":"Know who makes them and when they're required.","pts":["Developed by airport operator with the LVO/SMGCS Working Group and ATC","Routes for below 1,200 down to 500 RVR, and for below 500 RVR; some airports per runway (ex. ORD)","Required to have one if operating with SMGCS","FAA does not produce them - private sources (e.g., Jeppesen)"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"97ff356edc0de7e3.webp","w":720,"h":193,"cap":"Low Visibility Taxi Charts"}]}],"recall":[["How is a controlled stop bar used?","Red steady lights across the taxiway at the runway hold. After ATC clearance, the stop bar turns off and green lead-on lights turn on, confirming clearance to enter/cross."],["Who publishes SMGCS low visibility taxi charts?","Private sources such as Jeppesen - the FAA does not produce SMGCS charts. You must have one when operating in conjunction with SMGCS."]]},"cfii-vi-a-bai-flight-a-e":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"4-2":[{"f":"3bdc5a984e21e450.webp","w":712,"h":205,"cap":"Crosscheck"},{"f":"b83240ac7a47d0d8.webp","w":712,"h":205,"cap":"Crosscheck"}],"4-2-2":[{"f":"a173b9f19375bcab.webp","w":719,"h":204,"cap":"Crosscheck"},{"f":"6b470c71cfa3c786.webp","w":719,"h":204,"cap":"Crosscheck"}]},"pts":null,"intro":"Student smoothly controls the airplane by instruments only, maintaining an effective crosscheck and correcting attitude to PTS/ACS standards.","key":["Pitch + Power = Performance","Trim","Crosscheck","Adjust"],"cards":[{"h":"Control & performance method","obj":"Explain the three instrument categories.","pts":["Attitude instrument flying: controlling spatial position by instruments, not outside references","**Pitch + Power = Performance**","**Control**: attitude indicator and power indicators - immediate indications, precise adjustment","**Performance**: altimeter, airspeed, VSI, heading indicator, turn coordinator","**Navigation**: course, range, glide-slope indicators and bearing pointers"],"ce":""},{"h":"Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust","obj":"Teach the four procedural steps.","pts":["**Establish** attitude and power on control instruments; known settings reduce workload","**Trim** until control pressures are neutralized - frees attention","**Crosscheck** performance instruments; determine needed correction","**Adjust** attitude/power and repeat"],"ce":""},{"h":"Establish - pitch, bank, power","obj":"Use the attitude indicator and power indicators correctly.","pts":["AI gives immediate, direct indication of pitch/bank; set approximate then fine tune","Pitch changed by precise amounts vs horizon; bank vs bank scale","Power: set approximate throttle, check indicator, fine tune - don't fixate","Trim for hands-off flight; trim relieves pressures, it doesn't fly the airplane"],"ce":"CE: control inputs without reference to the AI; faulty trim (none, or over/under controlling with trim)."},{"h":"Crosschecks","obj":"Describe the three crosscheck patterns.","pts":["Continuous, logical observation of instruments - performance never stays constant on its own","**Selected radial**: AI -> instrument -> AI -> next; most common; only one in IFH for glass","**Inverted V**: AI -> turn coordinator -> AI -> VSI -> AI","**Rectangular**: top three, then bottom three; equal weight, slower return to critical instrument","Bank: check HI and TC; pitch: altimeter, VSI, airspeed"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"3bdc5a984e21e450.webp","w":712,"h":205,"cap":"Crosscheck"},{"f":"b83240ac7a47d0d8.webp","w":712,"h":205,"cap":"Crosscheck"},{"f":"a173b9f19375bcab.webp","w":719,"h":204,"cap":"Crosscheck"},{"f":"6b470c71cfa3c786.webp","w":719,"h":204,"cap":"Crosscheck"}]},{"h":"Crosscheck errors & interpretation","obj":"Recognize fixation, omission, emphasis.","pts":["**Fixation**: staring at one instrument, eliminating the crosscheck","**Omission**: leaving an instrument out of the scan","**Emphasis**: relying on one instrument (often the one best understood) instead of the combination","Interpretation: pitch = ASI, altimeter, VSI, AI; bank = HI, TC, AI"],"ce":""},{"h":"Adjust","obj":"Make small, precise corrections.","pts":["Limit AI pitch changes to 1/2 - 1 bar width","Bank about equal to degrees to turn, not to exceed 30 degrees","Standard rate bank estimate: TAS/10 + 5","Fine tune power for desired performance"],"ce":"CE: misinterpreting instruments and improper corrections, e.g., using rudder to fix heading."},{"h":"Straight-and-level","obj":"Set and verify straight-and-level.","pts":["Nose on horizon, wings level + cruise power","Performance: altimeter constant, VSI 0, airspeed constant, heading constant, TC level/coordinated","Trim, crosscheck, correct, retrim"],"ce":""},{"h":"Constant airspeed climbs & descents","obj":"Enter, maintain, and level off.","pts":["**Climb**: ~10 degrees nose up; as speed nears climb speed, full power; hold speed with pitch (1/2-1 bar)","**Descent**: set descent power, hold level as speed decays, then ~3 degrees nose down at desired speed","Level off: lead by 10% of vertical speed (500 fpm = 50')","Climb level-off: reduce power, smooth elevator to level; descent: add power, level","Crosscheck VSI, altimeter, AI for level, then trim"],"ce":""},{"h":"Constant rate climbs & descents","obj":"Hold a target VSI.","pts":["**Climb**: 5-10 degrees nose up (estimate) with climb power simultaneously; VSI = desired fpm","**Descent**: reduce power, 1-3 degrees nose down (estimate), vary as required","Airspeed varies with the rate","Same 10% lead and level-off procedure"],"ce":""},{"h":"Turns to headings","obj":"Turn precisely and roll out on heading.","pts":["Bank = degrees to turn, max 30; standard rate bank ~ TAS/10 + 5, check against TC","Coordinated aileron and rudder on the AI; small pitch increase to hold altitude","Roll out ~10 degrees before heading, or lead by 1/2 bank or less for small turns","Reduce pitch during rollout; increase scan when coordinating roll and pitch"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Name the control instruments and the performance instruments.","Control: attitude indicator, power indicators.\nPerformance: altimeter, airspeed indicator, VSI, heading indicator, turn coordinator."],["What are the four steps of the control & performance method?","Establish\nTrim\nCrosscheck\nAdjust"],["Name and define the three crosscheck errors.","Fixation - staring at one instrument\nOmission - leaving an instrument out\nEmphasis - relying on one instead of the combination"],["Which crosscheck is the only one the IFH discusses for glass cockpits?","Selected radial crosscheck (based off the attitude indicator)."],["How far ahead do you lead a level-off?","10% of the vertical speed - e.g., 500 fpm is led by 50'."],["How do you estimate the bank angle for a standard rate turn, and how do you verify it?","TAS/10 + 5, then double check against the turn coordinator."],["List the eBook's common errors for basic attitude instrument flight.","Fixation, omission, emphasis\nImproper instrument interpretation\nImproper control applications\nFailure to establish proper pitch/bank/power during corrections\nFaulty trim procedure"]]},"cfii-vi-a-straight-and-level":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"2-4-3":[{"f":"715d0c0f5718ec38.webp","w":712,"h":219,"cap":"Power"},{"f":"6435811e765f1bd2.webp","w":712,"h":219,"cap":"Power"}]},"pts":{"task":"VI.A STRAIGHT-AND-LEVEL FLIGHT","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK H and at least one other TASK. The applicant shall select either the primary and supporting or the control and performance method for teaching this AREA OF","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of teaching straight-and-level flight by describing—","sub":["the relationship of pitch, bank, and power in straight-and-level flight.","procedure using full panel and partial panel","coordination of controls and trim."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to straight-and-level flight by describing—","sub":["slow or improper cross-check during straight-and-level flight.","improper power control.","failure to make smooth, precise corrections, as required.","uncoordinated use of controls.","improper trim control."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains straight-and-level flight from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to straight-and-level flight.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Know the elements of flying straight-and-level solely by reference to instruments and apply them to hold straight-and-level flight.","key":["Primary Pitch: Altimeter","Primary Bank: Heading Indicator","Primary Power: Airspeed Indicator","Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust"],"cards":[{"h":"Primary & Supporting Method","obj":"Explain how primary and supporting instruments are assigned for each maneuver.","pts":["Direct extension of **Control & Performance**: Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust.","Attitude is set with the attitude indicator and power instruments, but each maneuver has specific primary and supporting instruments.","**Primary**: gives the most pertinent information for a specific parameter.","**Supporting**: shows a trend away from the desired parameter (no direct indication); forewarns of an impending deviation.","An efficient crosscheck catches and corrects these deviations early."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"715d0c0f5718ec38.webp","w":712,"h":219,"cap":"Power"},{"f":"6435811e765f1bd2.webp","w":712,"h":219,"cap":"Power"}]},{"h":"Pitch Control in Level Flight","obj":"Hold altitude with small pitch changes using the altimeter as primary.","pts":["**Pitch attitude**: angle between the longitudinal axis and the actual horizon; at a constant airspeed only one pitch attitude gives level flight.","Attitude indicator = direct pitch; limit changes to ½ to 1 bar width.","**Altimeter is primary**: at constant power/airspeed any altitude deviation is a pitch change. Error <100 ft: ½ bar; >100 ft: initial full bar.","**VSI**: trend and rate instrument; correct at double the deviation (100 ft off = ~200 fpm), max 500-1,000 fpm. VSI primary when correcting back to altitude.","Airspeed indicator: indirect pitch; a rapid airspeed change means a large pitch change."],"ce":"CE: chasing the VSI needle in turbulence; abrupt pitch changes cause lag and needle reversal."},{"h":"Bank and Power","obj":"Keep wings level using the heading indicator as primary and manage power vs. pitch.","pts":["**Bank angle**: angle between the lateral axis and the horizon; adjust with coordinated aileron and rudder.","**Heading indicator primary** in straight flight; correct with bank no greater than degrees to turn, or standard rate (TAS/10 + 5).","Attitude indicator is primary for bank only when flying a specific bank angle. Ball off center = slip/skid; center with rudder.","At a given airspeed, power determines climb, descent, or level; altitude held constant, power determines airspeed.","Altitude high + airspeed low (or vice versa): pitch fixes both. Both high or both low: pitch and power needed."],"ce":""},{"h":"Trim and Partial Panel","obj":"Trim correctly and hold straight-and-level with gyro/AHRS instruments failed.","pts":["Establish the attitude, then trim to relieve pressures; do not fly the plane with trim. Attitude, power, or configuration changes usually need trim.","**Partial panel**: attitude and heading indicators lost; remaining: altimeter, airspeed, turn coordinator, VSI, magnetic compass.","Pitch: altimeter still primary; use it with airspeed and VSI, correct by rate of change, small gentle pressures.","Bank: keep turn coordinator mini airplane level; average a swinging compass.","Return to heading with timed standard rate turns (10° off = 3 seconds)."],"ce":"CEs: slow/improper crosscheck, improper power control, rough corrections, uncoordinated controls, improper trim."}],"recall":[["Straight-and-level: primary instrument for pitch, bank, and power?","Pitch: Altimeter\nBank: Heading Indicator\nPower: Airspeed Indicator"],["Supporting instruments for pitch, bank, and power in straight-and-level?","Pitch: attitude indicator, VSI, airspeed indicator\nBank: attitude indicator, turn coordinator\nPower: RPM, manifold pressure, etc."],["You are 100 ft low. What VSI and what bar-width correction does the eBook teach?","Use a vertical speed about double the deviation: ~200 fpm (max 500-1,000 fpm).\nUnder 100 ft: ½ bar width; over 100 ft: initial full bar width."],["Altitude high and airspeed low. Pitch or power?","Pitch alone: lowering the nose trades altitude for airspeed and fixes both.\nIf both are high or both low, change pitch and power."]]},"cfii-vi-b-turns":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"3-3":[{"f":"776a06225e5eda64.webp","w":701,"h":247,"cap":"Once established, trim the airplane, continue the crosscheck, adjust as necessary, repeat"},{"f":"895b15859ebe78fc.webp","w":701,"h":247,"cap":"Once established, trim the airplane, continue the crosscheck, adjust as necessary, repeat"}]},"pts":{"task":"VI.B TURNS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK H and at least one other TASK. The applicant shall select either the primary and supporting or the control and performance method for teaching this AREA OF","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of teaching turns by describing—","sub":["the relationship of true airspeed and angle of bank to a standard rate turn.","technique and procedure using full panel and partial panel for entry and recovery of a constant rate turn, including the performance of a half-standard rate turn.","coordination of controls and trim."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to turns by describing—","sub":["improper cross-check procedures.","improper bank control during roll-in and roll-out.","failure to make smooth, precise corrections, as required.","uncoordinated use of controls.","improper trim technique."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains turns from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to turns.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Know what a standard rate turn is, how to approximate its bank angle, and how to enter, hold, and recover from it by instruments.","key":["Standard Rate = 3° per second","Standard Rate Bank = TAS/10 + 5","Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust"],"cards":[{"h":"True Airspeed & Angle of Bank","obj":"Explain how bank and TAS drive rate and radius of turn.","pts":["**Standard rate** = 3° per second; basis for all IFR turns.","Rate of turn depends on the **horizontal component of lift (HCL)**, which varies directly with bank in a coordinated turn.","Same airspeed: more bank = faster rate, smaller radius.","Higher airspeed = slower rate, larger radius.","Standard rate bank estimate: **TAS ÷ 10 + 5** (110 kt = 16°). Other formulas: TAS/10 + 7; 15% of TAS."],"ce":""},{"h":"Constant Rate Turn: Entry, Hold, Recovery","obj":"Fly a standard rate turn with the correct primary instruments in each phase.","pts":["Roll-in: **attitude indicator primary for bank**; established: **turn coordinator primary**; note and hold that bank.","Pitch: altimeter primary; loss of vertical lift pitches the nose down, so add back pressure.","Power: airspeed drops (higher AOA, more induced drag); add power.","Roll-out: lead by about ½ the bank angle; attitude indicator primary during recovery, heading indicator once about level.","Half standard rate: same technique with half the bank, held on the turn coordinator."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"776a06225e5eda64.webp","w":701,"h":247,"cap":"Once established, trim the airplane, continue the crosscheck, adjust as necessary, repeat"},{"f":"895b15859ebe78fc.webp","w":701,"h":247,"cap":"Once established, trim the airplane, continue the crosscheck, adjust as necessary, repeat"}]},{"h":"Partial Panel Turns & Coordination","obj":"Turn accurately with attitude and heading indicators failed.","pts":["Establish standard rate on the turn coordinator.","Small turns/corrections: timed standard rate turns (10° = 3 seconds).","Larger turns: magnetic compass, adjusting for errors (ANDS, UNOS).","Crosscheck must accelerate; do not fixate on turn coordinator or compass; watch altitude and airspeed.","Keep the ball centered throughout; trim once control pressures are set."],"ce":"CEs: improper crosscheck, poor bank control on roll-in/roll-out, rough corrections, uncoordinated controls, improper trim."}],"recall":[["Estimate the standard rate bank angle at 110 KTAS.","TAS ÷ 10 + 5 = 11 + 5 = 16°"],["In a standard rate turn, which instrument is primary for bank during roll-in, while established, and on roll-out?","Roll-in: attitude indicator\nEstablished: turn coordinator\nRoll-out: attitude indicator, then heading indicator once about level"],["Why does a level turn need back pressure and more power?","Loss of vertical lift pitches the nose down (back pressure).\nHigher AOA increases induced drag, so airspeed decays (add power)."],["How far before the desired heading do you start the roll-out?","About ½ the angle of bank."]]},"cfii-vi-c-change-of-airspeed":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"2-1-1":[{"f":"268eb5974aacf7a9.webp","w":719,"h":223,"cap":"Decreased Speed"},{"f":"5e5dc1584a1b7c0c.webp","w":719,"h":223,"cap":"Decreased Speed"}],"3-2":[{"f":"d0f9d430f048b56d.webp","w":472,"h":287,"cap":"Establish the Turn, then Change Airspeed"}]},"pts":{"task":"VI.C CHANGE OF AIRPSEED IN STRAIGHT-AND-LEVEL AND TURNING FLIGHT","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK H and at least one other TASK. The applicant shall select either the primary and supporting or the control and performance method for teaching this AREA OF","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of teaching change of airspeed in straight-and-level flight and turns by describing—","sub":["procedure using full panel and partial panel for maintaining altitude and changing airspeed in straight-and-level and turning flight.","coordination of controls and trim technique."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to changes of airspeed in straight-and- level and turning flight by describing—","sub":["slow or improper cross-check during straight-and-level flight and turns.","improper power control.","failure to make smooth, precise corrections, as required.","uncoordinated use of controls.","improper trim technique."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains changes of airspeed in straight-and-level and turning flight from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to changes of airspeed in straight-and- level and turning flight.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Change airspeed with no other effect on level or turning flight, to ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust","Higher Speed = Lower Pitch","Lower Speed = Higher Pitch","Scan, Scan, Scan"],"cards":[{"h":"Changing Airspeed Straight-and-Level","obj":"Slow down or speed up while holding altitude and heading.","pts":["Lift changes with airspeed; without matching pitch changes, altitude suffers.","**Slowing**: reduce power (usually below what will be needed); add back pressure as speed decays; trim; adjust pitch for gear/flaps.","Approaching target speed, add power to capture it.","**Speeding up**: add power, lower the nose as it accelerates, trim; reduce power to capture.","Increase crosscheck rate throughout."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"268eb5974aacf7a9.webp","w":719,"h":223,"cap":"Decreased Speed"},{"f":"5e5dc1584a1b7c0c.webp","w":719,"h":223,"cap":"Decreased Speed"}]},{"h":"Changing Airspeed Partial Panel","obj":"Change airspeed without attitude or heading indicators.","pts":["Same process, different scan; altimeter primary for altitude.","If climbing/descending, first freeze the altimeter, then gently return to altitude; avoid large pitch changes.","Heading: turn coordinator level plus magnetic compass; let the compass stabilize, average if swinging.","Correct heading with standard rate turns (10° off = 3 second turn)."],"ce":""},{"h":"Changing Airspeed in Turning Flight","obj":"Hold standard rate and altitude while speed changes in a turn.","pts":["Pitch: altimeter primary; attitude and VSI secondary.","Bank: attitude indicator primary on roll-in, turn coordinator primary once established; heading secondary.","Power: MP/RPM primary while setting power, airspeed indicator primary while reaching speed.","Bank for a given rate is proportional to airspeed: slower = less bank, and slower needs more pitch.","Two methods: establish the turn then change speed, or change speed during turn entry (harder). Partial panel: roll out on the compass (ANDS, UNOS)."],"ce":"CEs: slow/improper crosscheck, improper power control, rough corrections, uncoordinated controls, improper trim.","figs":[{"f":"d0f9d430f048b56d.webp","w":472,"h":287,"cap":"Establish the Turn, then Change Airspeed"}]}],"recall":[["You slow down while holding a standard rate turn. What must happen to bank and pitch?","Bank decreases (bank for a given rate is proportional to airspeed).\nPitch increases to hold altitude."],["Two methods for changing airspeed in a turn?","1. Establish the turn, then change airspeed.\n2. Change airspeed simultaneously with turn entry (more challenging)."],["When slowing in level flight, why reduce power below the final setting?","To decelerate; as the target speed approaches, add power back to capture and hold it, with back pressure and trim to hold altitude."]]},"cfii-vi-d-speed-climbs-descents":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"2-1-1":[{"f":"d147641f0cfeeb67.webp","w":467,"h":310,"cap":"Constant Airspeed Straight Climbs"}],"2-3":[{"f":"30c4dbf62d6af036.webp","w":675,"h":215,"cap":"Constant Airspeed Straight Climbs"},{"f":"fb5a66829f8d0cf7.webp","w":675,"h":215,"cap":"Constant Airspeed Straight Climbs"}]},"pts":{"task":"VI.D CONSTANT AIRSPEED CLIMBS AND DESCENTS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK H and at least one other TASK. The applicant shall select either the primary and supporting or the control and performance method for teaching this AREA OF","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of constant airspeed climbs and descents by describing—","sub":["procedure using full panel and partial panel for an entry into a straight climb or climbing turn, from either cruising or climbing airspeed.","a stabilized straight climb or climbing turn.","a level-off from a straight climb or climbing turn, at either cruising or climbing airspeed.","procedure using full panel and partial panel for an entry into a straight descent or descending turn from either cruising or descending airspeed.","a stabilized straight descent or descending turn.","a level-off from a straight descent or descending turn, at either cruising or descending airspeed."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to constant airspeed climbs and descents by describing—","sub":["failure to use a proper power setting and pitch attitude.","improper correction of vertical rate, airspeed, heading, or rate-of-turn errors.","uncoordinated use of controls.","improper trim control."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains a constant airspeed climb and a constant airspeed descent from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to constant airspeed climbs and descents.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Fly constant airspeed climbs and descents, straight and turning, to ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust","10% Rule","Scan, Scan, Scan"],"cards":[{"h":"Constant Airspeed Straight Climb","obj":"Hold a specific airspeed at a set power and accept the resulting climb rate.","pts":["Raise the nose to the approximate climb attitude; power at the same time or as speed nears climb speed.","Transition: **attitude indicator primary for pitch**; established: **airspeed indicator primary for pitch**, heading indicator for bank.","Too slow: lower pitch; too fast: raise pitch; ½ to 1 bar movements.","Level off: lead by **10% of the vertical speed**; lower the nose, let it accelerate, reduce to cruise power, trim.","Partial panel: entries easier if made at climb speed; altimeter, VSI, airspeed for pitch; small inputs, allow time."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"d147641f0cfeeb67.webp","w":467,"h":310,"cap":"Constant Airspeed Straight Climbs"},{"f":"30c4dbf62d6af036.webp","w":675,"h":215,"cap":"Constant Airspeed Straight Climbs"},{"f":"fb5a66829f8d0cf7.webp","w":675,"h":215,"cap":"Constant Airspeed Straight Climbs"}]},{"h":"Constant Airspeed Descents & Turning","obj":"Enter, hold, and level off from a constant airspeed descent.","pts":["Entry: reduce power, hold altitude to slow; near descent speed set descent pitch; trim.","Stabilized: airspeed indicator primary for pitch, heading indicator for bank; power stays constant, correct speed with pitch.","Level off: lead 10% of vertical speed; adjust pitch and power together. To level at cruise, set power 100-150 ft early (varies).","Partial panel: increase power with pitch, or before it to level off at a higher airspeed.","Turning climbs/descents: combine with VI.B turn technique; crosscheck faster."],"ce":"CEs: wrong power/pitch, improper correction of vertical rate, airspeed, heading or rate-of-turn errors, uncoordinated controls, improper trim."}],"recall":[["Constant airspeed climb: primary pitch instrument during the transition vs. once established?","Transition: attitude indicator\nEstablished: airspeed indicator (bank: heading indicator)"],["In a constant airspeed climb, airspeed is low. What do you change?","Pitch: lower the nose (power stays at the climb setting)."]]},"cfii-vi-e-rate-climbs-descents":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"2-3":[{"f":"866eeec8c6c1906c.webp","w":467,"h":295,"cap":"Constant Rate Straight Climbs"}],"2-4":[{"f":"79bc8b6d71690507.webp","w":720,"h":241,"cap":"Constant Rate Straight Climbs"},{"f":"9edada72609c2e01.webp","w":720,"h":241,"cap":"Constant Rate Straight Climbs"}]},"pts":{"task":"VI.E CONSTANT RATE CLIMBS AND DESCENTS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK H and at least one other TASK. The applicant shall select either the primary and supporting or the control and performance method for teaching this AREA OF","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of constant rate climbs and descents by describing—","sub":["procedure using full panel and partial panel for an entry into a constant rate climb or descent.","a stabilized constant rate straight climb or climbing turn, using the vertical speed indicator.","a level-off from a constant rate straight climb or climbing turn.","an entry into a constant rate straight descent or descending turn.","a stabilized constant rate straight descent or descending turn using the vertical speed indicator.","level-off from a constant rate straight descent or descending turn."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to constant rate climbs and descents by describing—","sub":["failure to use a proper power setting and pitch attitude.","improper correction of vertical rate, airspeed, heading, or rate-of-turn errors.","uncoordinated use of controls.","improper trim control."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains a constant rate climb and a constant rate descent from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to constant rate climbs and descents.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Hold a straight or turning constant rate of climb or descent, to ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust","10% Rule","Scan, Scan, Scan"],"cards":[{"h":"Constant Rate Climb","obj":"Hold a specific VSI rate while controlling airspeed.","pts":["Used mostly for short climbs within 1,000 ft; constant airspeed climbs are much more common.","**Pitch controls rate, power controls airspeed.**","Transition: airspeed indicator primary for pitch (VSI may lag 6+ seconds; not on glass); established: **VSI primary for pitch**.","Stabilized: airspeed indicator primary for power; heading indicator for bank.","If no more power is available, pitch must change and the rate can't be held (altitude, weight, power)."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"866eeec8c6c1906c.webp","w":467,"h":295,"cap":"Constant Rate Straight Climbs"},{"f":"79bc8b6d71690507.webp","w":720,"h":241,"cap":"Constant Rate Straight Climbs"},{"f":"9edada72609c2e01.webp","w":720,"h":241,"cap":"Constant Rate Straight Climbs"}]},{"h":"Constant Rate Descent & Level Offs","obj":"Fly a constant rate descent and level off on altitude.","pts":["Entry: reduce power, hold altitude to slow; near descent speed lower the nose; fine-tune VSI; trim.","VSI primary pitch; airspeed primary power (slow = add power); heading indicator primary bank.","Level off: lead by 10% of vertical speed; to level at cruise set cruise power 100-150 ft early (varies).","Attitude indicator is primary pitch during the descent level off.","Partial panel: VSI and altimeter set the attitude; small inputs (VSI lags, sensitive); turning versions combine with VI.B."],"ce":"CEs: wrong power/pitch, improper correction of vertical rate, airspeed, heading or rate-of-turn errors, uncoordinated controls, improper trim."}],"recall":[["Constant rate climb: what controls rate and what controls airspeed?","Pitch controls rate of climb (VSI).\nPower controls airspeed."],["Why use the airspeed indicator for pitch during the transition to a constant rate climb?","The VSI may lag 6 seconds or more; use airspeed until the VSI stabilizes (not applicable to glass VSIs)."],["Stabilized constant rate descent: primary pitch, bank, and power?","Pitch: VSI\nBank: heading indicator\nPower: airspeed indicator"]]},"cfii-vi-f-compass-turns":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-1":[{"f":"4bc093260269ff2c.webp","w":132,"h":137,"cap":"Magnetic Compass"}],"1-2-1":[{"f":"4641209f493e13e5.webp","w":591,"h":314,"cap":"Variation"}],"1-2-2":[{"f":"aa43d624cd3e3734.webp","w":314,"h":175,"cap":"What’s Going On"},{"f":"da724e7c88749e42.webp","w":315,"h":165,"cap":"What’s Going On"}],"2-1-1":[{"f":"59b9c43d91830362.webp","w":308,"h":247,"cap":"What"}]},"pts":{"task":"VI.F TIMED TURNS TO MAGNETIC COMPASS HEADINGS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK H and at least one other TASK. The applicant shall select either the primary and supporting or the control and performance method for teaching this AREA OF","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of timed turns to magnetic compass headings by describing—","sub":["operating characteristics and errors of the magnetic compass.","calibration of the miniature aircraft of the turn coordinator 1, both right and left, using full panel and the clock.","procedures using full panel and partial panel performing compass turns to a specified heading."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to timed turns to magnetic compass headings by describing—","sub":["incorrect calibration procedures.","improper timing.","uncoordinated use of controls.","improper trim control."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains timed turns to magnetic compass headings from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to timed turns to magnetic compass headings.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Understand magnetic compass errors and how to compensate, and turn to headings with the compass and with timed turns.","key":["North Lags, South Leads","UNOS: Undershoot N, Overshoot S","ANDS: Accelerate N, Decelerate S"],"cards":[{"h":"Magnetic Compass Operation","obj":"Describe how the compass works and why it reads backward.","pts":["Two small magnets on a float in clear fluid; card with a lubber line.","Steel pivot in a spring-loaded jewel cup lets the float rotate and tilt up to about 18°.","Pilot sees the card from its backside, so headings appear reversed (flying N, E shows left of the lubber line).","Technique: don't turn toward the displayed heading; move the desired heading to the lubber line."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"4bc093260269ff2c.webp","w":132,"h":137,"cap":"Magnetic Compass"}]},{"h":"Variation & Deviation","obj":"Explain variation and deviation and compute compass course.","pts":["**Variation**: magnetic and geographic north poles not collocated (about 1,300 miles apart).","**Isogonic lines** join equal variation; **agonic line**: no variation.","**Deviation**: local magnetic fields in the aircraft; shown on the compass correction card; minimized by swinging the compass and compensator magnets.","TC ± Var = MC; MC ± Dev = Compass Course. **East is least, West is best**."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"4641209f493e13e5.webp","w":591,"h":314,"cap":"Variation"}]},{"h":"Dip, Acceleration & Oscillation Errors","obj":"Explain turning and acceleration errors and how to compensate.","pts":["Flux lines are vertical at the poles, parallel over the equator; near the poles they dip the card.","Turning from a southerly heading: compass **leads**. From a northerly heading: compass **lags**.","**UNOS**: undershoot N by 30°, 030/330 by 20°, 060/300 by 10°; overshoot S by 30°, 150/210 by 20°, 120/240 by 10°. None for E/W.","**Acceleration error** (E/W headings): **ANDS**, accelerate north, decelerate south.","**Oscillation**: card swings around the heading; use the average."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"aa43d624cd3e3734.webp","w":314,"h":175,"cap":"What’s Going On"},{"f":"da724e7c88749e42.webp","w":315,"h":165,"cap":"What’s Going On"}]},{"h":"Timed Turns","obj":"Turn a set number of degrees using the clock and turn coordinator.","pts":["Simpler than compass turns: no compass errors to account for.","**Time = degrees ÷ 3** (standard rate: 45° in 15 s; half standard: 45° in 30 s).","Primary: bank = turn coordinator, pitch = altimeter, power = airspeed; clock replaces the heading indicator.","Calibrate first: start standard rate as the second hand passes 12/3/6/9; 10 s should give 30°; adjust bank, check both directions.","Start on a cardinal point; same roll-in and roll-out rate cancels entry/recovery time. Partial panel: check the compass after, allowing for errors."],"ce":"CEs: incorrect calibration, improper timing, uncoordinated controls, improper trim.","figs":[{"f":"59b9c43d91830362.webp","w":308,"h":247,"cap":"What"}]}],"recall":[["Heading east, you accelerate. What does the compass show?","A turn toward north (ANDS: Accelerate North, Decelerate South)."],["How do you calibrate the turn coordinator?","Start a standard rate turn as the second hand hits a cardinal point; after 10 s you should have turned 30°.\nAdjust bank if not; check both directions and use that bank for timed turns."]]},"cfii-vi-g-steep-turns":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"2-1":[{"f":"4e47ce57860f12db.webp","w":254,"h":266,"cap":"Full Panel Steep Turn"}]},"pts":{"task":"VI.G STEEP TURNS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK H and at least one other TASK. The applicant shall select either the primary and supporting or the control and performance method for teaching this AREA OF","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of steep turns by describing—","sub":["procedure using full panel and partial panel for entry and recovery of a steep turn.","the need for a proper instrument cross-check.","roll-in/roll-out procedure.","coordination of control and trim."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to steep turns by describing—","sub":["failure to recognize and make proper corrections for pitch, bank, or power errors.","failure to compensate for precession of the horizon bar of the attitude indicator.","uncoordinated use of controls.","improper trim technique."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains steep turns from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to steep turns. If the aircraft used for the practical test has a turn needle, substitute turn needle for miniature aircraft of turn coordinator.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Fly a steep turn in IMC holding altitude and airspeed, recovering on the entry heading.","key":["Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust","Set Approximate Pitch & Power","Scan, Scan, Scan"],"cards":[{"h":"Full Panel Steep Turn","obj":"Enter, hold, and recover from a 45° steep turn by instruments.","pts":["In instrument flight, any turn greater than standard rate may be considered steep; errors grow fast and are harder to correct.","Know and set approximate pitch (much more needed, often the hardest part) and power (more drag).","Bank: **attitude indicator primary** (45°). Pitch: **altimeter primary**. Power: **airspeed indicator primary**.","Bank can correct altitude: high, steepen slightly; low, shallow slightly (no pitch change).","Roll out about ½ the bank angle early; reduce pitch and power as bank comes off."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"4e47ce57860f12db.webp","w":254,"h":266,"cap":"Full Panel Steep Turn"}]},{"h":"Overbanking and Partial Panel Steep Turns","obj":"Recognize a diving spiral and fly steep turns without gyros.","pts":["Overbank: high bank, altimeter and VSI dropping, airspeed rising despite back pressure (diving spiral); back pressure just tightens the turn.","Recover: shallow bank with coordinated aileron/rudder, hold or slightly relax elevator, reduce power if airspeed rises rapidly.","Partial panel: exact bank unknown; turn coordinator primary bank, altimeter primary pitch, airspeed primary power.","Roll out on the magnetic compass, accounting for ANDS/UNOS; plan the roll-out indication ahead."],"ce":"CEs: missing pitch/bank/power errors, not compensating for attitude indicator horizon bar precession, uncoordinated controls, improper trim."}],"recall":[["Steep turn: primary instrument for bank, pitch, and power (full panel)?","Bank: attitude indicator\nPitch: altimeter\nPower: airspeed indicator"],["How do you recognize and recover from an overbank in a steep turn?","High bank, altimeter/VSI falling, airspeed rising despite back pressure.\nShallow the bank (coordinated), hold or slightly relax elevator, reduce power if airspeed climbs rapidly."],["Without changing pitch, how can bank fix altitude in a steep turn?","High: increase bank to reduce vertical lift.\nLow: decrease bank to increase vertical lift."]]},"cfii-vi-h-unusual-attitudes":{"req":true,"ofigs":{"4-2":[{"f":"9c0074319a080019.webp","w":719,"h":267,"cap":"Unusual Attitude Recovery"},{"f":"ea061bed6290df78.webp","w":719,"h":267,"cap":"Unusual Attitude Recovery"}],"4-3-1":[{"f":"25e651778b60dddd.webp","w":714,"h":265,"cap":"Bank"},{"f":"b9682d8fc3eec926.webp","w":714,"h":265,"cap":"Bank"}]},"pts":{"task":"VI.H RECOVERY FROM UNUSUAL FLIGHT ATTITUDES","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK H and at least one other TASK. The applicant shall select either the primary and supporting or the control and performance method for teaching this AREA OF","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of recovery from unusual flight attitudes by describing—","sub":["conditions or situations which contribute to the development of unusual flight attitudes.","procedure using full panel and partial panel for recovery from nose-high and nose-low unusual flight attitudes."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to recovery from unusual flight attitudes by describing—","sub":["incorrect interpretation of the flight instruments.","inappropriate application of controls."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains recovery from unusual flight attitudes, solely by reference to instruments, from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to recovery from unusual flight attitudes.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Recognize and recover from nose high and nose low unusual attitudes, full or partial panel.","key":["Nose High: Power, Pitch, Bank","Nose Low: Power, Bank, Pitch","Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust"],"cards":[{"h":"Causes and Recognition","obj":"List causes of unusual attitudes and how each type looks on the panel.","pts":["**Unusual attitude**: an attitude not normally required for instrument flight; reactions tend to be instinctive and abrupt.","Causes: turbulence, cockpit preoccupation, disorientation, careless crosscheck, instrument failure, misinterpretation, confusion, lack of proficiency.","Rule: an unexpected rate or indication = assume a UA; speed up the crosscheck to confirm attitude or instrument failure.","**Nose high (climbing turn)**: airspeed down, altitude up, positive VSI, bank on TC, lots of blue.","**Nose low (diving spiral)**: airspeed up, altitude down, negative VSI, bank on TC, lots of brown."],"ce":""},{"h":"Why Not Recover on the Attitude Indicator","obj":"Explain which instruments start the recovery and why.","pts":["A spillable attitude indicator may have exceeded its limits; it may have failed and caused the UA.","Even working, errors up to 5° pitch and bank; hard to read in extreme attitudes.","Exception: electronic flight displays: not spillable, red X if failed, useful in extreme attitudes.","Recovery starts from airspeed, altimeter, VSI, and turn coordinator. Follow the POH if it differs.","Intent: nose high = **prevent a stall**; nose low = **prevent overstressing** the airplane."],"ce":""},{"h":"Nose High Recovery: Power, Pitch, Bank","obj":"Recover from a climbing turn with decreasing airspeed.","pts":["**Power**: increase in proportion to the deceleration.","**Pitch**: forward elevator to lower the nose and reduce AOA (prevent a stall).","**Bank**: level with coordinated aileron/rudder by the turn coordinator.","Steps in sequence but nearly simultaneous; then fast crosscheck for overcontrol.","Level flight: altimeter and airspeed stop, reverse, and stabilize; straight: TC level, ball centered."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"9c0074319a080019.webp","w":719,"h":267,"cap":"Unusual Attitude Recovery"},{"f":"ea061bed6290df78.webp","w":719,"h":267,"cap":"Unusual Attitude Recovery"}]},{"h":"Nose Low Recovery: Power, Bank, Pitch","obj":"Recover from a diving spiral without overstressing.","pts":["**Power**: reduce to prevent excess airspeed and altitude loss.","**Bank**: level the wings first, coordinated, by the turn coordinator; reduces load factor.","**Pitch**: smooth back pressure to level attitude; avoid a secondary stall; if ground contact is a concern, use whatever back pressure needed.","Partial panel: same method; recover, then return to altitude and use the compass for heading.","Keep the ball centered; on an EFD with partial panel the slip/skid indicator is lost with no backup TC."],"ce":"CEs: not recognizing the UA, recovering by feel, inappropriate control inputs, missing passage through level flight.","figs":[{"f":"25e651778b60dddd.webp","w":714,"h":265,"cap":"Bank"},{"f":"b9682d8fc3eec926.webp","w":714,"h":265,"cap":"Bank"}]}],"recall":[["Recovery sequence for nose high vs. nose low?","Nose high: Power (increase), Pitch (lower), Bank (level)\nNose low: Power (reduce), Bank (level), Pitch (raise smoothly)"],["Why level the wings before raising the nose in a nose low recovery?","It reduces the load factor in the recovery, avoiding overstressing the airplane."],["Without an attitude indicator, how do you know you are passing through level flight?","The altimeter and airspeed needles stop and reverse direction, then stabilize."],["Why not reorient with a conventional attitude indicator in a UA?","It may have tumbled (spillable) or failed and caused the UA; even working it can be off up to 5° and is hard to read in extreme attitudes."]]},"cfii-vii-a-intercepting-tracking":{"req":true,"ofigs":{"1-1":[{"f":"0a573b19e7983731.webp","w":187,"h":132,"cap":"VOR Components: Ground Transmitter & Receiver"}],"1-2-1":[{"f":"ef24b564263a55db.webp","w":345,"h":223,"cap":"VOR Components: Ground Transmitter & Receiver"}],"2-1-1":[{"f":"ec23caddcd212f57.webp","w":371,"h":446,"cap":"TIM: Tune, Identify, Monitor"}],"2-3":[{"f":"4779683a77af5c23.webp","w":339,"h":407,"cap":"Reverse Sensing"}],"3-2":[{"f":"98810d1d24d08338.webp","w":338,"h":384,"cap":"Intercepting & Maintaining a DME Arc"}]},"pts":{"task":"VII.A INTERCEPTING AND TRACKING NAVIGATIONAL SYSTEMS AND DME ARCS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK A and B. If aircraft is not DME equipped, performance of DME arcs shall be tested orally.","note":"NOTE: While the applicant is expected to be able to fly DME arcs, they may be selected for testing only if they are charted and available (including use of RNAV substitution techniques, if appropriate.)","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of intercepting and tracking navigational systems and DME arcs by describing—","sub":["tuning and identification of a navigational facility.","setting of a selected course on the navigation selector or the correct identification of a selected bearing on the RMI.","method for determining aircraft position relative to a facility.","procedure for intercepting and maintaining a selected course.","procedure for intercepting and maintaining a DME arc.","procedure for intercepting a course or localizer from a DME arc.","recognition of navigation facility or waypoint passage.","recognition of navigation receiver or facility failure."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to intercepting and tracking navigational systems and DME arcs by describing—","sub":["incorrect tuning and identification procedures.","failure to properly set the navigation selector on the course to be intercepted.","failure to use proper procedures for course or DME arc interception and tracking.","improper procedures for intercepting a course or localizer from a DME arc."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains intercepting and tracking navigational systems and DME arcs from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to intercepting and tracking navigational systems and DME arcs.","sub":[]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge on the uses of the MFD and other graphical navigational displays, if installed, to monitor position in relation to the desired flightpath during holding.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Understand VOR concepts and use VORs for navigation and approaches, including intercepting radials and flying DME arcs.","key":["TIM: Tune, Identify, Monitor","Line-of-Sight","Never fly the CDI tail"],"cards":[{"h":"VOR Components","obj":"Describe the VOR ground transmitter and airborne receiver.","pts":["Foundation of the federal airway system, a backup to GPS, and a common approach.","Ground transmitter: assigned frequency, oriented to magnetic north, 360 courses TO/FROM, line-of-sight, various ranges.","Receiver: antenna, tuning device, VOR instrument.","**OBS** selects a radial; **CDI** shows the course relative to the airplane.","**TO/FROM** shows whether the selected course, if flown, goes to or from the station, not which way the airplane is heading."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"0a573b19e7983731.webp","w":187,"h":132,"cap":"VOR Components: Ground Transmitter & Receiver"},{"f":"ef24b564263a55db.webp","w":345,"h":223,"cap":"VOR Components: Ground Transmitter & Receiver"}]},{"h":"Tracking with the VOR","obj":"Tune, identify, and track to and from a VOR with wind correction.","pts":["**TIM**: tune, identify (Morse code, voice, or 3-letter ID on an EFD), monitor. No ID = out of service, don't use.","Alarm flag = signal inadequate (too far or too low, out of line-of-sight).","Center the CDI with TO; turn to that heading. Centered with TO, the needle tail shows the radial you're on.","Crosswind from the right: drift left, CDI moves right; correct right, then crab into the wind. Don't home.","Station passage: TO flips to FROM. **Reverse sensing**: flying TO with a FROM indication (or vice versa) the CDI reads opposite."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"ec23caddcd212f57.webp","w":371,"h":446,"cap":"TIM: Tune, Identify, Monitor"},{"f":"4779683a77af5c23.webp","w":339,"h":407,"cap":"Reverse Sensing"}]},{"h":"Intercepting a Course","obj":"Pick an intercept heading with the eBook's double-the-difference method.","pts":["Where are we? Where do we want to go? How do we get there? Does it make sense?","Intercept angle: **double the difference** between the radial you're on and the one to intercept, applied toward the direction of flight.","Not less than 20° nor more than 90°.","Ex: on the 160 radial, intercept the 205 radial: difference 45°, double = 90°, heading 295 (205 + 90)."],"ce":""},{"h":"Intercepting & Maintaining a DME Arc","obj":"Fly a DME arc with the 10-degree OBS method.","pts":["**DME arc**: constant radius track from a VOR; used in some approaches.","Fly the lead-in radial; about ½ mile before the arc distance turn 90° to join it (lead varies with groundspeed).","Turn the OBS 10° in the direction of flight; when centered, turn the OBS 10° more and make a 3 second standard rate turn.","Repeat until about 10° before the inbound course, then turn to intercept final.","Too close: reduce or skip the turn; too far: turn more (e.g., 15°). Or load it in the GPS."],"ce":"CEs: incorrect tune/ID, nav selector not set to the intercept course, improper arc procedures, improper intercept from the arc.","figs":[{"f":"98810d1d24d08338.webp","w":338,"h":384,"cap":"Intercepting & Maintaining a DME Arc"}]}],"recall":[["What does TIM stand for?","Tune, Identify, Monitor"],["What does the TO/FROM indicator tell you, and what doesn't it?","Whether the selected course, if flown, takes you TO or FROM the station.\nIt does not show whether the airplane is heading to or from it."],["On the 160 radial, cleared to intercept the 205 radial. Intercept heading?","Difference 45°, doubled = 90°; 205 + 90 = 295.\n(Intercept angle 20° to 90°.)"],["What is reverse sensing?","Flying TO a VOR with a FROM indication (or vice versa): the CDI reads opposite, so following the needle leads away from course."],["On a DME arc the distance is decreasing. What do you do?","Reduce or eliminate the turn (e.g., rotate the OBS 10° but don't turn). If increasing, turn more (e.g., 15°)."]]},"cfii-vii-b-holding":{"req":true,"ofigs":{"1-1":[{"f":"ecb8ea1b561cd96b.webp","w":396,"h":221,"cap":"Basics"}],"2-3":[{"f":"db2eb4fac932fa94.webp","w":278,"h":487,"cap":"Holding Instructions"}],"3-1":[{"f":"2efcd14ad21d59fb.webp","w":295,"h":224,"cap":"Entry Procedures"}],"6-2-1":[{"f":"90209e3f6bb6f474.webp","w":715,"h":190,"cap":"Timing (pictured, below)"}],"8-1":[{"f":"b44ee53bc9c38c76.webp","w":149,"h":217,"cap":"Wind Drift Correction (pictured, right)"}],"9-2":[{"f":"920243123d0e0ce7.webp","w":720,"h":193,"cap":"Common Errors"}]},"pts":{"task":"VII.B HOLDING PROCEDURES","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASK A and B. If aircraft is not DME equipped, performance of DME arcs shall be tested orally.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of holding procedures by describing—","sub":["setting of aircraft navigation equipment.","requirement for establishing the appropriate holding airspeed for the aircraft and altitude.","recognition of arrival at the holding fix and the prompt initiation of entry into the holding pattern.","timing procedure.","correction for wind drift.","use of DME in a holding pattern.","compliance with ATC reporting requirements."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to holding procedures by describing—","sub":["incorrect setting of aircraft navigation equipment.","inappropriate altitude, airspeed, and bank control.","improper timing.","improper wind drift correction.","failure to recognize holding fix passage.","failure to comply with ATC instructions."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains holding procedures from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to holding procedures.","sub":[]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge on the use of the MFD and other graphical navigational displays, if installed, to monitor position in relation to the desired flightpath during holding.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Know why holds exist, determine position relative to the fix, pick the entry, and correct timing and wind to ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Standard Hold: Right Turns","Use the Entry that Makes Sense","Outbound Leg: 3x Wind Correction"],"cards":[{"h":"Holding Basics & Speeds","obj":"Describe the standard holding pattern and maximum holding speeds.","pts":["Predetermined maneuver keeping aircraft in specified airspace awaiting further clearance.","Racetrack: inbound, 180° turn, outbound, 180° turn. **Standard = right turns**; left only when ATC specifies.","Max speeds: **up to 6,000 MSL 200 KIAS; 6,001-14,000 230 KIAS; 14,001+ 265 KIAS**. Notify ATC if unable.","6,001-14,000 may be restricted to 210 kt; any hold may be restricted to 175 kt (icon; generally Cat A/B IAPs).","Turns: standard rate or 30° bank, whichever is less."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"ecb8ea1b561cd96b.webp","w":396,"h":221,"cap":"Basics"}]},{"h":"Holding Instructions","obj":"List what ATC issues and what to do with no clearance beyond the fix.","pts":["Direction from the fix (8 compass points), fix, radial/course/bearing/airway/route, leg length if DME/RNAV, left turns if used, EFC time.","Ex: 'Hold east of SLI VOR on the 090 radial, EFC 1530Z': no length = 1-minute legs, no direction = right turns.","Reaching the clearance limit without further clearance: maintain last altitude, hold as depicted (else standard on the inbound course), request further clearance.","Draw it: fix, inbound leg TO the fix on the holding side, turns, outbound leg, your position, then the entry."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"db2eb4fac932fa94.webp","w":278,"h":487,"cap":"Holding Instructions"},{"f":"2efcd14ad21d59fb.webp","w":295,"h":224,"cap":"Entry Procedures"}]},{"h":"Entries","obj":"Fly the parallel, teardrop, and direct entries.","pts":["Slow to holding speed within 3 minutes of the fix.","**Parallel**: at the fix, parallel the holding course outbound about 1 minute; turn in the holding direction through more than 180°; back to the fix or intercept inbound.","**Teardrop**: at the fix, turn 30° into the holding side for about 1 minute, then turn in the holding direction to intercept inbound.","**Direct**: fly to the fix and turn to follow the pattern.","Use the entry that makes sense. Begin the entry promptly on reaching the fix."],"ce":""},{"h":"Nav Setup, Fix Arrival & Timing","obj":"Set up the equipment, recognize the fix, and time the legs.","pts":["VOR hold: tune/ID, set inbound course. DME fix: follow the radial to the distance. RNAV: set up in GPS; always build the hold in the GPS.","Fix arrival: VOR = TO/FROM flips; DME = readout at the distance; RNAV = on the EFD.","Initial outbound leg **1 min at or below 14,000 MSL, 1½ min above**; adjust later legs for 1-minute inbound legs.","Start outbound timing over or abeam the fix, whichever is later (VOR: flag reverses; intersection: after the outbound turn; locator: RB 90° minus drift).","DME hold: distances replace time; turn at the specified DME (5 NM legs = turn at 5 DME)."],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"90209e3f6bb6f474.webp","w":715,"h":190,"cap":"Timing (pictured, below)"}]},{"h":"Wind Correction & Lost Comms","obj":"Explain triple drift correction and leaving the clearance limit under 91.185.","pts":["Crab to hold the inbound course; **triple it outbound** (4° in = 12° out).","Why: groundspeed differs in each turn, so turn radii differ; without overcorrecting you are blown off the inbound leg.","Lost comms, limit is an approach fix: leave as close as possible to the EFC; no EFC, as close as possible to the ETA.","Limit not an approach fix: leave at EFC; no EFC, proceed to an approach fix and begin descent/approach near the ETA."],"ce":"CEs: incorrect nav setup, poor altitude/airspeed/bank control, improper timing or wind correction, missing fix passage, not complying with ATC.","figs":[{"f":"b44ee53bc9c38c76.webp","w":149,"h":217,"cap":"Wind Drift Correction (pictured, right)"},{"f":"920243123d0e0ce7.webp","w":720,"h":193,"cap":"Common Errors"}]}],"recall":[["Maximum holding airspeeds by altitude?","Up to 6,000 MSL: 200 KIAS\n6,001-14,000: 230 KIAS (may be 210)\n14,001 and above: 265 KIAS\nAny hold may be restricted to 175 kt"],["What does ATC include in holding instructions?","Direction from the fix, holding fix, radial/course/bearing/airway/route, leg length (DME/RNAV), left turns if any, EFC time."],["Arriving at the clearance limit with no further clearance. What do you do?","Maintain last altitude, hold as depicted (or standard on the inbound course if not depicted), and immediately request further clearance."],["Initial outbound leg time, and when does timing start?","1 min at or below 14,000 MSL; 1½ min above.\nStart over or abeam the fix, whichever is later."],["Inbound needs 4° of wind correction. Outbound correction?","Triple it: 12°."],["Lost comms, clearance limit is not an approach fix, no EFC. When do you leave?","Proceed to a fix where an approach begins and start descent and approach as close as possible to the ETA (filed or amended). (91.185)"]]},"cfii-viii-a-non-precision-approach":{"req":true,"ofigs":{"1-3-2":[{"f":"cb646dc6efdb6415.webp","w":652,"h":417,"cap":"Decision Altitude / Decision Height"},{"f":"ee45ca8f2c17c644.webp","w":652,"h":417,"cap":"Decision Altitude / Decision Height"}],"2-2":[{"f":"239227b3038517b9.webp","w":206,"h":341,"cap":"Bottom Bar"}],"5-1-3":[{"f":"5748dbcbf320eaeb.webp","w":282,"h":208,"cap":"Visual Descent Point (VDP)"},{"f":"bd0c5f7d20345607.webp","w":289,"h":196,"cap":"Timed Approach"}],"6-2":[{"f":"705f94c08329c44f.webp","w":37,"h":31,"cap":"Cold Weather Corrections"}],"6-3-1":[{"f":"9b79681cc844dc1a.webp","w":460,"h":155,"cap":"ICAO Cold Temperature Error Table"}]},"pts":{"task":"VIII.A NONPRECISION INSTRUMENT APPROACH (NPA)","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASKS A and B, to be combined with TASK C, D, or E. At least one nonprecision approach procedure shall be accomplished without the use of the gyroscopic heading and attitude indicators under simulated instrument conditions. Circling approaches are not applicable to helicopters.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of a nonprecision instrument approach by describing—","sub":["selection of the appropriate instrument approach procedure chart.","pertinent information on the selected instrument approach chart.","radio communications with ATC and compliance with ATC clearances, instructions, and procedures.","appropriate aircraft configuration, airspeed, and checklist items.","selection, tuning, identification, and determination of operational status of ground and aircraft navigation equipment.","adjustments applied to the published MDA and visibility criteria for the aircraft approach category.","maintenance of altitude, airspeed, and track, where applicable.","establishment and maintenance of an appropriate rate of descent during the final approach segment.","factors that should be considered in determining whether: (1) the approach should be continued straight-in to a landing; (2) a circling approach to a landing should be made; or (3) a missed approach should be performed."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to a nonprecision instrument approach by describing—","sub":["failure to have essential knowledge of the information on the instrument approach chart.","incorrect communications procedures or noncompliance with ATC clearances or instructions.","failure to accomplish checklist items.","faulty basic instrument flying technique.","inappropriate descent below the MDA."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains a nonprecision instrument approach from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to a nonprecision instrument approach.","sub":[]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge on the uses of the MFD and other graphical navigational displays, if installed, to monitor position, track, wind drift, and other parameters to maintain desired flightpath.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Student knows the elements of non-precision approaches and can set up, brief, and fly them without instructor help to ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Verify the Chart","Brief the Approach","5Ts: Turn, Time, Twist, Throttle, Talk"],"cards":[{"h":"What is a non-precision approach?","obj":"Distinguish non-precision, precision, and APV approaches.","pts":["**Non-precision**: course deviation info but no glidepath deviation info (AIM: no electronic glideslope). Ex: VOR, TACAN, LNAV, NDB, LOC, ASR","**Precision**: course and glidepath guidance meeting ICAO Annex 10 standards. Ex: PAR, ILS, GLS","**APV**: like precision but doesn't meet Annex 10. Ex: LNAV/VNAV, LPV","IFH calls APV non-precision, but it is flown like a precision approach","Non-precision generally doesn't get you as low as precision, but still gets you in in marginal weather"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"cb646dc6efdb6415.webp","w":652,"h":417,"cap":"Decision Altitude / Decision Height"},{"f":"ee45ca8f2c17c644.webp","w":652,"h":417,"cap":"Decision Altitude / Decision Height"}]},{"h":"MDA vs DA/DH","obj":"Explain how the minimum altitude works on each approach type.","pts":["**MDA**: used when no electronic glideslope; lowest altitude descent is authorized on final or during circle-to-land","At MDA: level off at or above it if visual references aren't in sight; may continue at MDA to the MAP","**DA**: used on precision and APV; missed approach must be initiated there if visual references aren't established","**DH**: referenced to height above threshold","DA/DH is the equivalent of a missed approach point"],"ce":""},{"h":"Approach charts","obj":"Walk through the sections of NACO and Jeppesen approach charts.","pts":["**NACO** (Chart User's Guide): Top bar, Briefing info (freqs, course, runway distances, notes, missed approach, lighting)","NACO cont.: Plan view (IAF through missed, MSA diagram), Profile view, Minimums/Airport diagram (time to MAP), Bottom bar (amendments, lat/long)","**Jeppesen** (Chart Legend): Top bar, Briefing strip (freqs, course, FAF alt, MDA, elevations, missed, notes, MSA)","Jepp cont.: Plan view, Profile view, Minimums (time to MAP, approach lighting, visual missed approach steps)"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"239227b3038517b9.webp","w":206,"h":341,"cap":"Bottom Bar"}]},{"h":"Approach categories","obj":"Explain how approach category sets the minimums.","pts":["Grouped by landing speed; if not specified, **1.3 VSO at max landing weight**","Category dictates landing minimums; higher-speed categories generally have higher minimums","Keeps the aircraft in a safe area: terrain, turn radius/containment, altitude loss at DA, missed approach climb","**A** <91 kt, **B** 91-120, **C** 121-140, **D** 141-165, **E** 166+","Certified in one category; never use a lower one. Faster approach (no flaps, icing, heavy) = use higher category minimums"],"ce":""},{"h":"Choosing the approach and chart","obj":"Select an appropriate, safe approach and verify the chart.","pts":["Consider ATIS (runway/approaches in use), airplane capabilities, and weather (especially ceilings)","**Straight-in**: lower minimums, simpler, safer, more efficient. **Circling**: closed runway, wind, terrain, or equipment; higher minimums, more complex","Apply the correct minimums (straight-in vs circling)","Personal preference: fly what you're competent with; don't learn on the fly close to the ground in IMC. Vertical guidance is safer","Always verify the chart matches the runway (e.g., LOC 25L vs 25R), especially after an approach change"],"ce":""},{"h":"Flying the approach","obj":"Maintain course, altitude, and airspeed while staying ahead of the airplane.","pts":["Always ask \"What am I doing next?\" Verify altitudes, airspeeds, track against the chart","Bug a heading that holds course with wind; adjust the bug L/R to correct deviations","**5 Ts** at every waypoint: Turn, Time, Twist, Throttle, Talk. Establish, Trim, Crosscheck, Adjust; include the chart in the scan","Descent rate **greater than 1,000 FPM** is unacceptable in the final stages (human perceptual limits, any airplane)"],"ce":"CE: Faulty basic instrument flying technique; failure to accomplish checklist items."},{"h":"Visual Descent Point (VDP)","obj":"Explain and calculate a VDP.","pts":["Point from which a normal (3°) descent from MDA to the runway can begin, provided the required visual references are in sight","VDP can be treated as a MAP; beyond it you are no longer using the normal rate and maneuvers required by FAR 91.175. May continue at MDA to MAP","Mnemonic: **GuS wears a HAT**. HAT = MDA minus threshold elevation; GuS = 300 ft/NM for 3°, 400 ft for 4°","HAT ÷ 300 = VDP distance (NM) for 3°. Ex: MDA 1,350 MSL, TDZE 810 → HAT 540 → 1.8 NM"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"5748dbcbf320eaeb.webp","w":282,"h":208,"cap":"Visual Descent Point (VDP)"},{"f":"bd0c5f7d20345607.webp","w":289,"h":196,"cap":"Timed Approach"}]},{"h":"Descent rate and glidepath checks","obj":"Compute required descent rate and verify a 3° path.","pts":["**Timed approach**: (FAF altitude minus TDZE) ÷ time inbound","Ex: FAF 2,000 MSL, TDZE 400 MSL, 2 min inbound = 800 FPM","**Distance based**: feet per NM × NM per minute","**300 ft to 1 NM**: at 10 NM be 3,000 ft above TDZE, at 5 NM 1,500 ft"],"ce":""},{"h":"Non-precision missed approach","obj":"Identify the MAP and when to go missed.","pts":["MAP shown by **time from FAF** (from groundspeed and distance) or a **specific fix** (DME, VOR cross radial, RNAV waypoint)","Go missed: required visibility or visual references absent; normal descent rate/maneuvers not possible; people, equipment, animals on runway; unstable","At the MAP, go around and fly the published procedure unless instructed otherwise; memorize the first 2-3 steps","Obstacles may require steeper climb gradients, published in the chart Notes"],"ce":"CE: Inappropriate descent below MDA."},{"h":"Setup: FFIIMMMMS","obj":"Set up every approach the same way.","pts":["**F**ixes (identify, altitudes/speeds), **F**requencies (nav and comm)","**I**nbound course (set, watch for changes), **I**dentify (tune, identify, monitor)","**M**arker beacons, **M**inimums (category, straight-in vs circling), **M**inutes (FAF to MAP), **M**issed approach (first 2-3 steps)","**S**ecurity: passengers, seat belts, equipment","Identify: G1000 shows the navaid ID by the frequency, or listen to Morse code. No ID = out of service, don't use. Bad equipment shows a flag"],"ce":""},{"h":"Briefing, ATC, checklists, configuration","obj":"Brief concisely and fly the school's configuration profile.","pts":["Brief = briefing strip plus minimums, VDP, MAP, landing distance, runway exit, abnormals; keep it under 1 min","ATC: comply, read back key items, say \"unable\" if you can't comply, \"say again\" if unclear","Every fix: 5 Ts. Pre-landing: **LBBGUMPFS**. Inside FAF: **Reds, Blues, Greens, Whites, Power**","School profile: PT/intercept 100 KIAS, 18-20 in Hg, 2300 RPM; 1 mi outside FAF decel to 90, takeoff flaps","At FAF: 90 KIAS, 11-13 in Hg, 2300 RPM, landing flaps, 6° nose down"],"ce":"CE: Incorrect comm procedures or noncompliance with ATC clearances."},{"h":"Cold weather corrections","obj":"Apply cold temperature corrections correctly.","pts":["Colder than standard: true altitude is **less** than indicated; colder = bigger error, obstacle clearance risk","**CTA** (AIM 7-3-4): high-risk airports, icon and temp limit on the IAP. Correct At, At-or-Above, At-or-Below altitudes when reported temp is below published (AIM 7-3-5)","Advise ATC of corrections (not required if final segment only). Don't correct SID/ODP/STAR or ATC-assigned altitudes","**ICAO Cold Temperature Error Table** (TPP B1): enter at temp, column = chart altitude minus airport elevation; add the value, interpolate; use 5,000 ft column above 5,000","**All Segments** (snowflake/CTA limit): IAF to MA holding altitude, no CTA list. **Individual Segments**: only those marked in the CTA list"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"705f94c08329c44f.webp","w":37,"h":31,"cap":"Cold Weather Corrections"},{"f":"9b79681cc844dc1a.webp","w":460,"h":155,"cap":"ICAO Cold Temperature Error Table"}]},{"h":"Safety and efficiency","obj":"Fly within limits and consistently.","pts":["Personal minimums (wind, ceiling, vis, weather, airspace); don't bend them. Legal is not always safe","Aircraft must have required NAVAIDs and performance (altitudes, missed climb gradients), correct speed/category; check approach Notes","Lost SA: consider a missed approach or request vectors to set up again; ask for help","Current charts and NOTAMs. Same SOPs and briefing every time; know what you'll say before keying the mic"],"ce":"CE: Failure to have essential knowledge of the information on the approach chart."}],"recall":[["List the approach category speed ranges.","A: less than 91 kt\nB: 91-120 kt\nC: 121-140 kt\nD: 141-165 kt\nE: 166 kt or more"],["MDA is 1,350 MSL and TDZE is 810. Where is the VDP for a 3° path?","HAT = 540 ft\n540 ÷ 300 ft/NM = 1.8 NM from the runway"],["What descent rate is unacceptable in the final stages of an approach, and why?","Greater than 1,000 FPM, due to human perceptual limitations, regardless of airplane type."],["What does FFIIMMMMS stand for?","Fixes, Frequencies, Inbound course, Identify, Marker beacons, Minimums, Minutes, Missed approach, Security"],["Which altitudes do you NOT correct for cold temperature?","SID, ODP, and STAR altitudes, and altitudes specifically assigned by ATC (e.g., \"maintain 5,000\")."]]},"cfii-viii-b-precision-approach":{"req":true,"ofigs":{"1-3-1":[{"f":"cb646dc6efdb6415.webp","w":652,"h":418,"cap":"Decision Height"},{"f":"ee45ca8f2c17c644.webp","w":652,"h":418,"cap":"Decision Height"}],"2-2":[{"f":"239227b3038517b9.webp","w":206,"h":341,"cap":"Bottom Bar"}],"6-2":[{"f":"705f94c08329c44f.webp","w":36,"h":30,"cap":"Cold Weather Corrections (AIM 7-3)"}],"6-4":[{"f":"9b79681cc844dc1a.webp","w":403,"h":135,"cap":"Cold Weather Corrections (AIM 7-3)"}]},"pts":{"task":"VIII.B PRECISION INSTRUMENT APPROACH (PA)","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASKS A and B, to be combined with TASK C, D, or E. At least one nonprecision approach procedure shall be accomplished without the use of the gyroscopic heading and attitude indicators under simulated instrument conditions. Circling approaches are not applicable to helicopters.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of a precision instrument approach by describing—","sub":["selection of the appropriate instrument approach chart.","pertinent information on the selected instrument approach chart.","selection, tuning, identification, and determination of operational status of ground and aircraft navigation equipment.","radio communications with ATC and compliance with ATC clearances, instructions, and procedures.","appropriate aircraft configuration, airspeed, and checklist items.","adjustments applied to the published DH/DA and visibility criteria for the aircraft approach category.","maintenance of altitude, airspeed, and track, where applicable.","establishment and maintenance of an appropriate rate of descent during the final approach segment.","factors that should be considered in determining whether: (1) the approach should be continued straight-in to a landing; (2) a circling approach to a landing should be made; or (3) a missed approach should be performed."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to a precision instrument approach by describing—","sub":["failure to have essential knowledge of the information on the instrument approach procedure chart.","incorrect communications procedures or noncompliance with ATC clearances.","failure to accomplish checklist items.","faulty basic instrument flying technique.","inappropriate application of DH/DA."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains a precision instrument approach from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to a precision instrument approach.","sub":[]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge on the uses of the MFD and other parameters to maintain desired flightpath.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Student knows the elements of precision approaches and can set up, brief, and fly them without instructor help to ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Verify the Chart","Brief the Approach","5Ts: Turn, Time, Twist, Throttle, Talk"],"cards":[{"h":"What is a precision approach?","obj":"Define a precision approach and why the ILS matters.","pts":["Course and glidepath info meeting **ICAO Annex 10**; AIM: electronic glideslope or other glidepath provided. Ex: PAR, ILS, GLS","Vertical guidance gets you closer to the ground; minimums usually down to **200 ft AGL**","ILS is expensive, so mostly at large, busy airports; still often ATC's default approach even with PBN","APV (LNAV/VNAV, LPV) doesn't meet Annex 10; IFH calls it non-precision but it's flown like precision"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"cb646dc6efdb6415.webp","w":652,"h":418,"cap":"Decision Height"},{"f":"ee45ca8f2c17c644.webp","w":652,"h":418,"cap":"Decision Height"}]},{"h":"DA and DH","obj":"Explain decision altitude and decision height.","pts":["**DA**: used on precision and APV; initiate the missed if visual references aren't established. Equivalent of the MAP","**DH**: height above threshold to decide continue or go missed","DH is used by aircraft with radar altimeters on **Category II and III ILS**","MDA (non-precision): level at or above it; may continue to the MAP"],"ce":""},{"h":"Flying the ILS","obj":"Track the localizer and glideslope proactively.","pts":["Same basics as non-precision: \"What am I doing next?\", 5 Ts, Establish-Trim-Crosscheck-Adjust, chart in the scan","When the glide slope centers, pitch down about **5°**; correct any ball movement immediately with small inputs","Higher groundspeed requires a higher descent rate, and vice versa","Course and glidepath sensitivity **increase** approaching the runway","Configured and on speed, the glideslope gives a proper descent rate; >1,000 FPM late in the approach is unacceptable"],"ce":"CE: Faulty basic instrument flying technique."},{"h":"Precision missed approach","obj":"Decide correctly at DA.","pts":["At DA without required visual references: go around and fly the published missed unless instructed otherwise","Momentarily descending below DA during the go-around is expected and OK","If visual references appear **after** you are below DA, it is NOT OK to try to land","Memorize the first 2-3 missed steps; note any steeper climb gradient in the chart Notes"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"239227b3038517b9.webp","w":206,"h":341,"cap":"Bottom Bar"}]},{"h":"Setup, brief, and configuration (ILS)","obj":"Use the same setup and school profile for the ILS.","pts":["Choosing, FFIIMMMMS setup, tune-identify-monitor, brief under 1 min, ATC readbacks: same as non-precision","Verify the chart for the right runway (e.g., ILS 25L vs 25R)","School profile: PT/LOC intercept 100 KIAS, 18-20 in Hg, 2300 RPM","**½ dot above GS**: decelerate to 90, takeoff flaps","**GS intercept (FAF)**: 90 KIAS, 13-14 in MP, 2300 RPM, 5° nose down"],"ce":""},{"h":"Cold weather, safety, efficiency","obj":"Apply the same cold-temp and safety rules to precision approaches.","pts":["Cold temp corrections per AIM 7-3: same CTA, table, and segment-method rules as VIII.A","Don't correct ATC-assigned altitudes; advise ATC unless final segment only","Personal minimums, aircraft capability, approach notes, SA, current charts/NOTAMs","SOPs and consistent briefings = efficiency"],"ce":"CE: Failure to have essential knowledge of the approach chart; noncompliance with ATC clearances.","figs":[{"f":"705f94c08329c44f.webp","w":36,"h":30,"cap":"Cold Weather Corrections (AIM 7-3)"},{"f":"9b79681cc844dc1a.webp","w":403,"h":135,"cap":"Cold Weather Corrections (AIM 7-3)"}]}],"recall":[["Where is the MAP on a precision approach?","At the Decision Altitude (DA/DH) while on the glideslope."],["You start the missed at DA and sink slightly below it, then see the runway. Can you land?","No. Momentary descent below DA is expected, but visual references acquired below DA don't permit continuing to land."],["In the school profile, when do you slow to 90 KIAS and set takeoff flaps on an ILS?","At ½ dot above the glide slope. At glideslope intercept (FAF): 90 KIAS, 13-14 in MP, 2300 RPM, 5° nose down."]]},"cfii-viii-c-missed-approach":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-3-1":[{"f":"91a29e10ac643f21.webp","w":275,"h":405,"cap":"Missed Approach Procedure"}]},"pts":{"task":"VIII.C MISSED APPROACH","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASKS A and B, to be combined with TASK C, D, or E. At least one nonprecision approach procedure shall be accomplished without the use of the gyroscopic heading and attitude indicators under simulated instrument conditions. Circling approaches are not applicable to helicopters.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of a missed approach procedure by describing—","sub":["pertinent information on the selected instrument approach chart.","conditions requiring a missed approach.","initiation of the missed approach, including the prompt application of power, establishment of a climb attitude, and reduction of drag.","required report to ATC.","compliance with the published or alternate missed approach procedure.","notification of ATC if the aircraft is unable to comply with a clearance, instruction, restriction, or climb gradient.","performance of recommended checklist items appropriate to the go-around procedure.","importance of positive aircraft control."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to a missed approach by describing—","sub":["failure to have essential knowledge of the information on the instrument approach chart.","failure to recognize conditions requiring a missed approach.","failure to promptly initiate a missed approach.","failure to make the required report to ATC.","failure to comply with the missed approach procedure.","faulty basic instrument flying technique.","descent below the MDA prior to initiating a missed approach."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains a missed approach from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to a missed approach.","sub":[]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge on the uses of the MFD and other graphical navigational displays, if installed, to monitor position and track to help navigate the missed approach.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Student understands missed approach procedures and where to find them, and can fly them safely without instructor help to ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Brief the Missed Approach","Know the first 2-3 Steps","5Ts: Turn, Time, Twist, Throttle, Talk"],"cards":[{"h":"What is a missed approach?","obj":"Explain the purpose and design of the missed approach.","pts":["Published route and altitudes flown if unable to land, returning to the airway structure clear of obstacles","Begins at the MAP and continues to a fix and/or holding pattern","Climb may start before the MAP, but **don't start the route (turns) until the MAP**: it's designed from the MAP","Assumes **200 ft/NM** climb unless published higher","Approach clearance = clearance to fly the published missed, unless otherwise instructed"],"ce":""},{"h":"Missed approach on the chart","obj":"Find the procedure and the MAP on the chart.","pts":["Text in the Missed Approach box of the briefing strip, plus graphics","NACO: dashed line, usually to a hold. Jepp: dashed line on plan view plus step-by-step icons below the profile","MAP: end of the solid course, start of the dashed; drawn for the lowest published minima","Precision: reaching DA/DH on the glideslope. Non-precision: crossing an on-airport navaid, a fix (radial/DME, RNAV waypoint), or time/distance from FAF table (use groundspeed, interpolate)"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"91a29e10ac643f21.webp","w":275,"h":405,"cap":"Missed Approach Procedure"}]},{"h":"When is a missed approach required?","obj":"State the AIM 5-5-5 and FAR 91.175 triggers.","pts":["**AIM 5-5-5**: safe approach/landing not possible; instructed by ATC; at the MAP/DA without sufficient visual reference","Also: below minimums and visibility or runway environment lost; rejected landing for any reason","**91.175**: no descent below MDA/DA unless normal descent rate with normal maneuvers, flight visibility not less than required, and a runway reference visible"],"ce":""},{"h":"91.175 visual references","obj":"List the references that permit descent below DA/MDA.","pts":["**Approach light system**: can't go below **100 ft above TDZE** unless red terminating bars or side row bars are visible","Threshold, threshold markings, threshold lights","Touchdown zone, TDZ markings, TDZ lights","Runway, runway markings, runway lights","REIL or VASI"],"ce":""},{"h":"Flying the missed","obj":"Execute the missed with positive control.","pts":["**Power** full; **Attitude** climb, Vx or Vy as required (school: 7° nose up); **Configure** like a takeoff: flaps to takeoff, retract at safe altitude and speed","Then continue like a normal takeoff (After Takeoff/Climb checklist)","Dangers: low, IMC, disorienting pitch/power changes; departure stall / elevator trim stall","Aviate, Navigate, Communicate. Memorize the first 2-3 steps; fly, don't read","Fly the published missed unless ATC says otherwise; watch for alternate missed (ATC, chart, NOTAM)"],"ce":"CE: Failure to promptly initiate a missed approach; failure to comply with the procedure."},{"h":"Missed approach communication","obj":"Make required reports and handle unable.","pts":["Tell ATC you're on the missed (they may not see you); give the reason when time permits unless ATC-initiated (AIM 5-5-5)","Report time and altitude entering the hold; report cold temp altitude corrections","Once established, request your intentions: another approach, hold, alternate. You may not fly the whole procedure","If unable to comply with a clearance, gradient, or restriction, say \"unable\" and request an alternate missed, ahead of time","Don't attempt the approach if you can't comply with any part of the procedure"],"ce":"CE: Failure to make the required report to ATC."}],"recall":[["What climb gradient does a missed approach assume?","200 ft per NM, unless a higher gradient is published."],["You go missed early, before the MAP. What do you do?","You may climb, but continue on the approach course to the MAP before turning. The route is designed from the MAP; don't turn early."],["What 3 conditions does 91.175 require to go below DA/MDA?","1. Descent at a normal rate using normal maneuvers\n2. Flight visibility not less than required\n3. At least one required visual reference for the runway visible and identifiable"],["You only see the approach lights at DA. How low can you go?","To 100 ft above TDZE, unless the red terminating bars or side row bars are visible and identifiable."],["What reports are required on a missed approach?","That you're flying the missed (with reason when time permits, unless ATC-initiated)\nTime and altitude entering the hold\nCold temperature altitude corrections\nInability to comply with a clearance, gradient, or restriction"]]},"cfii-viii-d-circling-approach":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"7":[{"f":"49f34db2d1fdb2af.webp","w":492,"h":303,"cap":"ATC Compliance"},{"f":"ad1aa868b753b8f2.webp","w":492,"h":303,"cap":"ATC Compliance"}],"1-2":[{"f":"7a151f569a2760ae.webp","w":289,"h":177,"cap":"What is a Circling Approach?"}],"3-1":[{"f":"6ac0823018202758.webp","w":280,"h":423,"cap":"Approach Charts"}],"4-2":[{"f":"e3bf96884c17c691.webp","w":16,"h":16,"cap":"Protected Circling Area"}],"4-3":[{"f":"58ed962cc21aa02f.webp","w":221,"h":220,"cap":"The obstacle evaluation area (OEA) must be assessed for each category on the procedure"}],"5-2":[{"f":"9d32ef32b9512772.webp","w":589,"h":229,"cap":"Fly no Lower than the Circling Mins until in a Position to make a Final Descent for a Landing"}]},"pts":{"task":"VIII.D CIRCLING APPROACH (AIRPLANE)","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASKS A and B, to be combined with TASK C, D, or E. At least one nonprecision approach procedure shall be accomplished without the use of the gyroscopic heading and attitude indicators under simulated instrument conditions. Circling approaches are not applicable to helicopters.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements of a circling approach by describing—","sub":["selection of the appropriate circling approach maneuver considering the maneuvering capabilities of the aircraft.","circling approach minimums on the selected instrument approach chart.","compliance with advisories, clearance instructions, and/or restrictions.","importance of flying a circling approach pattern that does not exceed the published visibility criteria.","maintenance of an altitude no lower than the circling MDA until in a position from which a descent to a normal landing can be made."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to a circling approach by describing—","sub":["failure to have essential knowledge of the circling approach information on the instrument approach chart.","failure to adhere to the published MDA and visibility criteria during the circling approach maneuver.","inappropriate pilot technique during transition from the circling maneuver to the landing approach."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains a circling approach from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to a circling approach.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Student understands circling concepts and procedures and can fly a circling approach to a landing or a missed approach to ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Use the Circling Minimums","Lose Visual, Go Missed","MAP for the Approach Flown"],"cards":[{"h":"What is a circling approach?","obj":"Distinguish the two types and when circling-only is designed.","pts":["**Circling-only approach**: no straight-in minimums. **Straight-in with circling minimums**: used to land on a different runway","Circling-only when (IPH 4-11): final course alignment exceeds **30°**, descent gradient > **400 ft/NM** FAF to TCH, or runway not clearly defined","Named by type plus a letter: first is 'A', then alphabetical (e.g., VOR-A)","Tower usually gives the direction, e.g., circle east for left downwind runway 18","Low altitude, low speed, marginal weather: know your aircraft performance"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"7a151f569a2760ae.webp","w":289,"h":177,"cap":"What is a Circling Approach?"},{"f":"6ac0823018202758.webp","w":280,"h":423,"cap":"Approach Charts"}]},{"h":"When to circle and chart info","obj":"Recognize circling situations and read circling minimums.","pts":["Circle when: required by procedure, terrain, weather (approach doesn't serve the wind runway), construction (approach to closed runway)","NACO: circling minimums in Landing Minimums, below straight-in, can differ by category","Jepp: in Minimums, to the right of straight-in","Check Notes, e.g., circling to certain runways NA at night, or circling south NA","Cleared for approach to one runway and circle to another: use the chart you were cleared on, with **circling** minimums"],"ce":""},{"h":"Protected circling area","obj":"Explain circling radius and obstacle clearance.","pts":["Stay within your category's protected area; circling minimums give at least **300 ft** obstacle clearance there","Radius table on **TPP page B2**: by category and circling MDA altitude (accounts for higher TAS at altitude)","Ex: MDA 1,000 ft or less: A 1.3, B 1.7, C 2.7, D 3.6, E 4.5 NM","OEA: lines equal to the radius drawn from each authorized runway threshold, arcs joined; MDA based on OEA and final segment (FAA Order 8260.3)"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"e3bf96884c17c691.webp","w":16,"h":16,"cap":"CAT E"},{"f":"58ed962cc21aa02f.webp","w":221,"h":220,"cap":"The obstacle evaluation area (OEA) must be assessed for each category on the procedure"}]},{"h":"How to circle","obj":"Fly the circle from clearance to landing.","pts":["ATC clears the circling approach; tower gives instructions once visual. Configure per POH","Passing the FAF, descend to circling minimums: higher rate than normal, but never over **1,000 fpm**. Visual early? Consider leveling higher","Once visual, tell tower and circle as instructed; no tower: broadcast intentions","Stay in the circling area, keep the runway in sight; no lower than circling MDA until normal descent and maneuvers allow landing","AIM 5-4-20: shortest path to base or downwind, weather permitting; passing over the airport or other runways is OK; consider traffic and standard left turns"],"ce":"CE: Failure to adhere to the published MDA and visibility during the circle."},{"h":"Missed from a circle","obj":"Go missed correctly when visual is lost.","pts":["Lose visual contact at any point: fly the missed approach","Fly the missed for the **approach you were cleared to fly**, not the landing runway","Begin a **climbing turn toward the landing runway**; continue until established on the missed course, then fly the procedure","Inform tower/ATC and coordinate next steps","ATC compliance: follow circling instructions; question unsafe/unclear clearances; say unable if needed"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"9d32ef32b9512772.webp","w":589,"h":229,"cap":"Fly no Lower than the Circling Mins until in a Position to make a Final Descent for a Landing"},{"f":"49f34db2d1fdb2af.webp","w":492,"h":303,"cap":"ATC Compliance"},{"f":"ad1aa868b753b8f2.webp","w":492,"h":303,"cap":"ATC Compliance"}]}],"recall":[["When is an approach published as circling-only?","Final course alignment exceeds 30° from the runway\nDescent gradient greater than 400 ft/NM from FAF to TCH\nRunway not clearly defined"],["You're cleared for a straight-in approach to one runway, then circle to land on another. Which chart and minimums?","The ILS 17 chart (the approach you were cleared to fly), using its circling minimums, not straight-in."],["How much obstacle clearance do circling minimums provide, and where?","At least 300 ft within the protected circling area (OEA) for your category."],["You lose sight of the runway on the downwind of a circle. What do you do?","Begin a climbing turn toward the landing runway, continue until established on the missed course for the approach you flew, fly the published missed, and inform ATC."]]},"cfii-viii-e-landing-from-an-approach":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-1-1":[{"f":"c349bcbdb9f436c2.webp","w":311,"h":445,"cap":"ATC will provide current visibility reports"}]},"pts":{"task":"VIII.E LANDING FROM A STRAIGHT-IN APPROACH","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select TASKS A and B, to be combined with TASK C, D, or E. At least one nonprecision approach procedure shall be accomplished without the use of the gyroscopic heading and attitude indicators under simulated instrument conditions. Circling approaches are not applicable to helicopters.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements related to landing from a straight-in approach by describing—","sub":["effect of specific environmental, operational, and meteorological factors.","transition to, and maintenance of, a visual flight condition.","adherence to ATC advisories, such as NOTAMs, wind shear, wake turbulence, runway surface, and braking conditions.","completion of appropriate checklist items.","maintenance of positive aircraft control."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to landing from a straight-in approach by describing—","sub":["inappropriate division of attention during the transition from instrument to visual flight conditions.","failure to complete required checklist items.","failure to properly plan and perform the turn to final approach.","improper technique for wind shear, wake turbulence, and crosswind.","failure to maintain positive aircraft control throughout the complete landing maneuver."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains a landing from a straight-in approach from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to landing from a straight-in approach.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"Student knows the elements of landing after a straight-in instrument approach and can transition safely from instruments to a visual landing to ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Stabilized Approach","Keep GS/LOC in Crosscheck","Transition with VASI/PAPI"],"cards":[{"h":"Required visibility","obj":"Explain visibility reports and the pilot's landing decision.","pts":["**91.175**: may not land if flight visibility is less than the approach requires","**Prevailing**: greatest visibility equaled or exceeded around at least half the horizon; miles","**RVV**: transmissometer visibility for a runway; miles; used in lieu of prevailing for that runway","**RVR**: distance seen down the runway from a moving aircraft; hundreds of feet; touchdown/mid/rollout/far end; mostly Part 121/135","Only the pilot determines flight visibility; below minimums = go missed regardless of report. Landing with RVR below required is hard to defend"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"c349bcbdb9f436c2.webp","w":311,"h":445,"cap":"ATC will provide current visibility reports"}]},{"h":"Inoperative components","obj":"Adjust minimums for inoperative equipment.","pts":["Published minimums assume all components and visual aids work","Inoperative equipment = higher minimums","More than one inoperative: use the highest minimum required by any single inoperative component","See the Inoperative Components or Visual Aids Table, inside front cover of the TPP"],"ce":""},{"h":"Transitioning to visual flight","obj":"Split attention to acquire the runway without losing the glidepath.","pts":["**100-200 ft above MDA/DA**: split attention inside and outside to find a 91.175 reference","Approach lights alone: may continue to 100 ft above TDZE","Once visual, keep using vertical guidance; transition with **VASI/PAPI**; don't disregard the glideslope","Watch for sloped runway, night, lighting, haze, heavy rain"],"ce":"CE: Inappropriate division of attention from instrument to visual."},{"h":"Stable approach and gates","obj":"Keep the approach stable and use go-around gates.","pts":["Constant speed, descent rate, flight path, configuration; minimize changes; helps spot wind shear","Use the **VDP** as a makeshift MAP; beyond it descent exceeds 3° and can go unstable","**Gates**: points where unmet criteria = go-around (e.g., checklists done, configured, on speed by set altitudes)","Airline 737 example: gear down, 15° flaps, 180 kt by 1,500 AGL; fully configured on speed by 1,000 AGL","Go missed if you can't positively control the airplane (weather, workload); tell ATC your intentions"],"ce":""},{"h":"Checklists and ATC advisories","obj":"Complete landing items and act on advisories.","pts":["Once visual, verify before landing checklist: Reds, Blues, Greens, Whites, Power (fixed gear); gear down and locked if retractable","Maintain normal approach speed; don't assume checklists are done; go around if distracted or behind","Heed advisories: NOTAMs, wind shear, wake turbulence, runway surface, braking","Need more time to assess an advisory? Go around and fly the missed"],"ce":"CE: Failure to plan the turn to final; improper wind shear, wake, crosswind technique; loss of positive control."}],"recall":[["Two components are inoperative. How do you determine minimums?","Raise each minimum to the highest required by any single inoperative component (Inop Components Table, inside front cover of the TPP)."],["What is an approach gate?","A point on the approach where, if set criteria (checklists, configuration, speed) aren't met, you go around."]]},"cfii-viii-x-procedure-turns":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"2-2-2":[{"f":"5bbb161cd4e48551.webp","w":679,"h":462,"cap":"Types of Procedure Turns"},{"f":"70051f07555bfaaf.webp","w":679,"h":462,"cap":"Types of Procedure Turns"},{"f":"3e92aeb96daf8387.webp","w":292,"h":424,"cap":"Types of Procedure Turns"},{"f":"71951cb11969ab10.webp","w":715,"h":210,"cap":"Flying the Racetrack"},{"f":"5867db090d4e3aaa.webp","w":715,"h":210,"cap":"Flying the Racetrack"},{"f":"8b3849526d97f055.webp","w":715,"h":210,"cap":"Flying the Racetrack"}]},"pts":null,"intro":"Student understands what procedure turns are, when they are required, and how to fly them safely.","key":["PT, Hold-in-Lieu, Teardrop","When not to use a PT: SNoRT"],"cards":[{"h":"What is a procedure turn?","obj":"Explain the purpose of a PT.","pts":["Maneuver to **reverse direction** and establish inbound on the approach course when straight-in isn't possible","Allows descent from an IAF or assigned altitude to the PT altitude","Intercepts the inbound course far enough out to align with final","3 basic types: **holding in lieu of PT**, **teardrop**, **45°/180°**; depicted in plan and profile views","Lesson assumes standard rate turns"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"5bbb161cd4e48551.webp","w":679,"h":462,"cap":"Types of Procedure Turns"},{"f":"70051f07555bfaaf.webp","w":679,"h":462,"cap":"Types of Procedure Turns"}]},{"h":"Hold-in-lieu and teardrop","obj":"Fly the hold-in-lieu and teardrop correctly.","pts":["**Hold-in-lieu**: hold over an IF or FAF, normal entries; once inbound and cleared, start the approach","Extra circuits aren't expected; tell ATC if you need them. Max holding speeds and profile leg time/distance apply","Depicted hold must be followed except **radar vectors** or **NoPT**","**Teardrop**: depart IAF on published outbound, turn back, intercept inbound before the IF; lose a lot of altitude in limited airspace","If a teardrop is depicted and course reversal is required, it must be flown"],"ce":""},{"h":"Barbed arrow (45°/180°)","obj":"Fly the pilot's-choice course reversal.","pts":["Most flexible: where to turn and type/rate of turn are pilot's choice; stay within the Remain Within distance","Options: 45°/180°, 80°/260°, racetrack, teardrop","**45/180** (example times): cross fix outbound, 2 min; turn 45° left, 1 min; turn right 180°; intercept and turn the last 45°","**80/260**: 2 min outbound, turn 80° left, immediately turn right 260° to intercept","**Teardrop option**: turn left 30° for 1 min, 20° for 2 min, or 10° for 3 min; then right 210°, 200°, or 190°. **Racetrack**: like a hold, enter on the maneuvering side"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"3e92aeb96daf8387.webp","w":292,"h":424,"cap":"Types of Procedure Turns"},{"f":"71951cb11969ab10.webp","w":715,"h":210,"cap":"Flying the Racetrack"},{"f":"5867db090d4e3aaa.webp","w":715,"h":210,"cap":"Flying the Racetrack"},{"f":"8b3849526d97f055.webp","w":715,"h":210,"cap":"Flying the Racetrack"}]},{"h":"When NOT to fly a PT: SNoRT","obj":"Know the rules in 91.175(j) and AIM 5-4-9.","pts":["**91.175(j)**: radar vector to final course/fix, timed approach from a holding fix, or NoPT: no PT unless cleared by ATC","**AIM 5-4-9(a)**: a depicted PT or hold-in-lieu is required unless cleared straight-in","**SNoRT**: Straight-in, NoPT, Radar vectors, Timed approach, plus absence of the PT barb (PT not authorized)","To fly a PT when not required, get an amended clearance first. Unsure? Ask!"],"ce":""},{"h":"PT descents, distance, speed","obj":"Fly the PT within altitude, distance, and speed limits.","pts":["PT altitude is a **minimum until established inbound**","Don't descend from PT fix altitude until crossing the fix or abeam it outbound, when noted ('Maintain ... until established outbound') or an at-or-above altitude is shown","No such note/altitude: descend to PT altitude immediately on crossing the fix, any direction","Complete within the profile's Remain Within distance, usually **10 NM**","**AIM 5-4-9(a)(3)**: max **200 kt** on a PT approach; max holding speeds for hold-in-lieu"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Teardrop option on a barbed PT: what timing goes with 30°, 20°, and 10°?","30° for 1 min, then right 210°\n20° for 2 min, then right 200°\n10° for 3 min, then right 190°"],["What does SNoRT stand for?","Straight-in approach\nNoPT\nRadar vectors\nTimed approach\n(also: absence of the PT barb)"],["You're on a NoPT segment but want to fly the PT to lose altitude. Can you?","Only after advising ATC and receiving a clearance to execute the procedure turn."],["Max speed on an approach involving a procedure turn, and why?","200 knots (AIM 5-4-9(a)(3)), to stay within the obstruction clearance area. Hold-in-lieu uses max holding speeds."]]},"cfii-ix-a-lost-comms":{"req":false,"ofigs":{},"pts":{"task":"IX.A LOSS OF COMMUNICATIONS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one TASK. The evaluator shall omit TASKS C and D unless the applicant furnishes a multiengine airplane for the practical test, then TASK C or D is mandatory.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements related to loss of communications by describing:","items":[{"t":"Recognition of loss of communications.","sub":[]},{"t":"When to continue with flight plan as filed or when to deviate.","sub":[]},{"t":"How to determine the time to begin an approach at destination.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"The learner knows the elements of lost communications on an IFR flight plan in IFR and VFR conditions and can safely react to a lost comm situation.","key":["Squawk: 7600","Route: AVE F","Altitude: MEA"],"cards":[{"h":"Lost comm: general","obj":"Know at all times what route and altitude to fly and when to leave a clearance limit if the radios quit.","pts":["**Lost communication** = inability to communicate with ATC on an IFR flight plan: total (2-way) loss, or only unable to receive or transmit","Procedures are in **FAR 91.185** - IFR operations: two-way radio communications failure","ATC will clear the airspace to keep you and others safe - so you must fly exactly per the regulation","Squawk **7600** to alert ATC to a radio failure"],"ce":""},{"h":"Recognizing lost comm","obj":"Troubleshoot before assuming a loss of communications.","pts":["Clue: abnormally quiet radio - query ATC to see if it's a problem or just quiet","You may hear other aircraft but get no response to your transmissions","Check volume, power, switches; verify the frequency; check the other comm; run POH troubleshooting checklists","You may be out of range - relay through another aircraft, or with none around broadcast on guard (**121.5**) to request a frequency"],"ce":""},{"h":"Identify the problem","obj":"Match the response to what has actually failed.","pts":["**Transmit but not hear**: hard to identify - transmit intentions in case they are going out","**Hear but not transmit**: you still receive instructions; acknowledge/respond with the **Ident** button","**Total failure**: always keep transmitting intentions in case they are going out"],"ce":""},{"h":"Continue as filed or deviate?","obj":"Know when 91.185 IFR procedures apply.","pts":["Primary objective: prevent extended IFR operation without radio communication","Radio fails on an IFR clearance in **VFR conditions**: continue VFR if possible and **land as soon as practicable**","No requirement to land as soon as *possible*","If **IFR conditions** prevail, comply with 91.185 - this ensures aircraft separation"],"ce":""},{"h":"91.185 route: AVE F","obj":"Fly the route in this order of priority.","pts":["**A**ssigned: route in the last ATC clearance received","**V**ectored: direct from the point of failure to the fix, route, or airway in the vector clearance","**E**xpected: route ATC advised may be expected in a further clearance","**F**iled: route in the flight plan"],"ce":""},{"h":"91.185 altitude: MEA","obj":"Fly the highest of three altitudes for the route segment being flown.","pts":["**M**inimum altitude for IFR operations","**E**xpected: altitude ATC advised may be expected","**A**ssigned: altitude in the last ATC clearance received","Re-evaluate on each segment - fly the highest of the three"],"ce":"Scenario: if the MEA on the next segment is above your assigned altitude, which do you fly?"},{"h":"Leaving the clearance limit","obj":"Know when to start the approach.","pts":["Limit **is** a fix where an approach begins: commence descent as close as possible to the **EFC** time; if none, as close as possible to the **ETA** filed","Limit **is not** an approach fix: leave the limit at the **EFC** time; if none, leave upon arrival over the limit","Then proceed to a fix where an approach begins and descend as close as possible to the ETA from the filed or amended ETE"],"ce":""},{"h":"Re-establishing communications","obj":"Use all possible means while flying 91.185.","pts":["Squawk 7600 - informs ATC of the problem","Monitor navaids, try other aircraft, run applicable checklists","Return to the previously assigned frequency; try an FSS, New York Radio, or San Francisco Radio","Give position, altitude, last assigned frequency; transmit on guard","No priority among frequencies - do all at once if possible; keep transmitting intentions"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Your radio goes quiet in IMC. What do you do before declaring lost comm?","Query ATC; check volume, power, switches, frequency\nTry the other comm; run POH checklists\nRelay through another aircraft or call on 121.5 for a frequency"],["You can hear ATC but can't transmit. How do you acknowledge instructions?","Use the transponder Ident button"],["Lost comm in VFR conditions on an IFR clearance - what does 91.185 require?","Continue VFR if possible and land as soon as practicable\n(not 'as soon as possible')"],["Lost comm route priority (AVE F)?","Assigned\nVectored (direct to the fix/route/airway in the vector clearance)\nExpected\nFiled"],["Lost comm altitude (MEA)?","Highest of, for the segment being flown:\nMinimum IFR altitude\nExpected altitude\nAssigned altitude"],["Lost comm: clearance limit is not an approach fix and you have no EFC. When do you leave it?","Upon arrival over the clearance limit; proceed to an approach fix and begin descent as close as possible to the ETA calculated from the filed or amended ETE"],["Besides squawking 7600, how do you try to re-establish contact?","Return to previous frequency; try FSS / New York or San Francisco Radio; transmit on guard\nGive position, altitude, last frequency\nKeep transmitting intentions"]]},"cfii-ix-b-loss-of-gyros":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-2":[{"f":"d39a03dafec6e4d6.webp","w":271,"h":176,"cap":"General"}],"1-2-1":[{"f":"32e8dff2fc2f0e64.webp","w":269,"h":199,"cap":"Analog Instrument Failure"}]},"pts":{"task":"IX.B APPROACH WITH LOSS OF PRIMARY FLIGHT INSTRUMENT INDICATORS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one TASK. The evaluator shall omit TASKS C and D unless the applicant furnishes a multiengine airplane for the practical test, then TASK C or D is mandatory.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements related to loss of primary flight instrument indicators by describing—","sub":["recognition of inaccurate or inoperative primary instrument indicators and advising ATC and the evaluator.","notification of ATC or evaluator anytime that the aircraft is unable to comply with an ATC clearance or whether able to continue the flight.","importance of utilizing navigation equipment in an emergency situation and demonstrating a nonprecision approach without the use of primary flight instruments."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to loss of primary flight instrument indicators by describing—","sub":["recognition of failed system components that relate to primary flight instrument indication(s).","failure to notify ATC of situation.","failure to transition to emergency mode/standby instrumentation."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains loss of primary flight instrument indicators by conducting a non-precision approach without the use of these indicators.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects common errors related to loss of primary flight instrument indicators.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"The learner can safely pilot the airplane without gyro instruments in all phases of an IFR flight, meeting ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Ignore Failed Instruments","Get to VMC, if Possible","Notify ATC"],"cards":[{"h":"What's lost and failure types","obj":"Understand which instruments fail together.","pts":["A gyro failure can take out the **heading indicator, attitude indicator and/or turn coordinator**","Turn coordinators traditionally use a different power source (ex. vacuum for AI/HI, electric for TC) - if AI/HI fail the TC still works, and vice versa","**AHRS failure**: lose AI, HI and TC; generally a red X; not standardized between aircraft; go to backup AI, compass, moving map","**Analog**: warning flag or disagreement between instruments","**Pneumatic (vacuum)**: gyros slow and wander; many small aircraft have no warning - monitor the vacuum gauge"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"d39a03dafec6e4d6.webp","w":271,"h":176,"cap":"General"},{"f":"32e8dff2fc2f0e64.webp","w":269,"h":199,"cap":"Analog Instrument Failure"}]},{"h":"Recognizing failed instruments","obj":"Find the failure fast while keeping control.","pts":["Recognized by a warning indicator or inconsistency between instruments","Immediately compare **AI with turn coordinator and VSI** - checks pitch/bank and compares the static, suction, and electric systems","G1000: faulty instruments show a red X; use standby instruments and MFD moving map","Maintain control while identifying; expedite the crosscheck - aviate, navigate, communicate"],"ce":""},{"h":"Transition to partial panel","obj":"Make a timely transition to a partial panel scan.","pts":["**Ignore** (cover if possible) the failed AI and HI - they can trick you into wrong inputs and disorientation","Pitch: **altimeter (primary)**, VSI, airspeed - plus backup AI if available","Bank: **turn coordinator** primary; glass cockpit - backup AI","Heading: magnetic compass - timed (standard rate) and compass turns (ANDS, UNOS)","Moving map 'track up' eases orientation; small pressures - stop the needle, then return to altitude"],"ce":""},{"h":"Notify ATC and restore instruments","obj":"Fly first, then communicate and troubleshoot.","pts":["When conditions permit, tell ATC the problem and your ability to continue in IMC","Continue in VFR conditions if possible - request vectors, use weather reports","No VMC: choose the best available approach, with ATC vectors if available","Troubleshoot only if time permits: run checklists (power source, reset, alternate system)"],"ce":""},{"h":"Flying the partial-panel approach","obj":"Make the approach as simple as possible.","pts":["Stay ahead; use the **5 T's** (Turn, Time, Twist, Throttle, Talk) at each waypoint","PTS requires a **non-precision** approach; real life - request the simplest/safest (PAR, vectors to ILS/LOC)","Tune, identify, monitor; set measurable go-around criteria (ex. >1 dot, +100/-0 ft)","Glass: load approach, track up, zoom in; else compass heading and small turns for wind","Accelerated crosscheck is necessary"],"ce":"CE: slow to recognize inaccurate indications; failure to notify ATC; failure to transition from full to partial panel"}],"recall":[["Fastest technique to identify a failed gyro instrument?","Compare the attitude indicator with the turn coordinator and VSI\n(cross-compares static, suction/pressure, and electric systems)"],["Why can a vacuum failure be hard to catch in a small airplane?","Gyros slow down and wander, showing incorrect info\nMany small aircraft have no vacuum warning - monitor the vacuum gauge"],["Partial panel: what is primary for pitch and for bank?","Pitch: altimeter (with VSI and airspeed)\nBank: turn coordinator (glass: backup AI)"],["Two steps to fix an altitude deviation on partial panel?","1. Smooth input to stop the needle movement\n2. Smooth input to return to the desired altitude"],["What type of approach does the PTS require partial panel, and what would you request in real life?","PTS: non-precision approach\nReal life: simplest/safest, e.g. PAR or vectors to final for an ILS or localizer"],["Common errors in loss of primary flight instruments?","Slow to recognize inaccurate indications\nFailure to notify ATC\nFailure to adequately transition to partial panel"]]},"cfii-ix-c-engine-failure":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"2-1":[{"f":"db2383c3b73c60ae.webp","w":224,"h":207,"cap":"Maneuvering with One Engine Inoperative"}]},"pts":{"task":"IX.C ENGINE FAILURE             DURING      STRAIGHT-AND-LEVEL          FLIGHT    AND TURNS","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one TASK. The evaluator shall omit TASKS C and D unless the applicant furnishes a multiengine airplane for the practical test, then TASK C or D is mandatory.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements related to engine failure during straight-and- level flight and turns, solely by reference to instruments, by describing—","sub":["appropriate methods to be used for identifying and verifying the inoperative engine.","technique for maintaining positive aircraft control by reference to instruments.","importance of accurately assessing the aircraft’s performance capability with regard to action that maintains altitude or minimum sink rate considering existing conditions."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to engine failure during straight- and-level flight and turns, solely by reference to instruments, by describing—","sub":["failure to recognize an inoperative engine.","hazards of improperly identifying and verifying the inoperative engine.","failure to properly adjust engine controls and reduce drag.","failure to establish and maintain the best engine inoperative airspeed.","failure to follow the prescribed checklist.","failure to establish and maintain the recommended flight attitude for best performance.","failure to maintain positive aircraft control while maneuvering.","hazards of exceeding the aircraft’s operating limitations.","faulty basic instrument flying technique."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains straight-and-level flight and turns after engine failure, solely by reference to instruments, from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to straight-and-level flight and turns after engine failure, solely by reference to instruments.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"The learner can safely control and maneuver the airplane with an engine failed in instrument conditions, meeting ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Fly First","Zero Sideslip","Rudder Control"],"cards":[{"h":"Basic attitude instrument flight","obj":"Pitch + Power = Performance, applied single engine.","pts":["In IMC an engine failure is harder to recognize and potentially disorienting - fly first, checklists second","**Establish** known pitch and power on the control instruments","**Trim** until control pressures are neutralized","**Crosscheck** performance instruments; **Adjust** attitude or power; repeat"],"ce":""},{"h":"Maneuvering with one engine inop","obj":"Recognize, control, then manage the failure by instruments.","pts":["Recognize: unexpected **yaw and roll toward the failed engine**; abnormal indications, sounds, feel","Rudder and aileron for directional control; establish **zero sideslip: 1-3° bank and ½ ball toward the operating engine** (varies by aircraft)","Same steps as VMC: **Full Power, Reduce Drag, Identify, Verify, Fix or Feather**; restart if time permits","Between every step check performance: zero sideslip, airspeed, heading, altitude, engine indications"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"db2383c3b73c60ae.webp","w":224,"h":207,"cap":"Maneuvering with One Engine Inoperative"}]},{"h":"SE straight-and-level by instruments","obj":"Nose slightly high + zero sideslip + 80% power = SE straight-and-level.","pts":["Control: pitch **4° nose high**, bank **1-3°**, rudder **½ ball**, power **80%**","Performance: altimeter constant, VSI 0 fpm, airspeed constant, heading constant","Turn coordinator: level + ½ ball","Trim, crosscheck, adjust and retrim"],"ce":""},{"h":"SE level turn by instruments","obj":"Nose slightly high + zero sideslip + 80% power = SE level turn.","pts":["Control: pitch **5-6° nose high**, bank **10°**, rudder **½ ball**, power **80%**","Performance: altimeter constant, VSI 0, airspeed constant, heading turning; TC banked + ½ ball","Add bank and keep zero sideslip; small, smooth inputs - no large banks or aggressive inputs","Increase pitch to hold altitude; trim, crosscheck, adjust"],"ce":"CE: failure to identify/verify the inop engine, reduce drag, hold best engine-inop airspeed, follow the checklist; exceeding limitations"}],"recall":[["How do you establish zero sideslip by instruments?","1-3° bank and ½ ball deflection toward the operating engine\n(varies by aircraft)"],["Engine failure flow?","Full Power, Reduce Drag, Identify, Verify, Fix or Feather\nRestart if time and conditions permit"],["Single-engine straight-and-level vs level turn: pitch, bank, power?","S&L: 4° nose high, 1-3° bank, 80%\nTurn: 5-6° nose high, 10° bank, 80%\nBoth: ½ ball rudder"],["Name some common errors for engine failure by instruments.","Failure to recognize the inop engine; misidentify/verify\nNot adjusting controls or reducing drag\nNot holding best engine-inop airspeed or attitude; skipping the checklist\nExceeding limitations; faulty instrument technique"]]},"cfii-ix-d-single-engine-approach":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"1-1":[{"f":"db2383c3b73c60ae.webp","w":243,"h":224,"cap":"Managing an Engine Failure"}],"6-3":[{"f":"dc0f63a15460c4d8.webp","w":720,"h":193,"cap":"Common Errors:"}]},"pts":{"task":"IX.D INSTRUMENT APPROACH—ONE ENGINE INOPERATIVE (MULTIENGINE)","area":"NOTE: The evaluator shall select at least one TASK. The evaluator shall omit TASKS C and D unless the applicant furnishes a multiengine airplane for the practical test, then TASK C or D is mandatory.","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant:","items":[{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements related to an instrument approach with one engine inoperative by describing—","sub":["maintenance of altitude, airspeed and track appropriate to the phase of flight or approach segment.","procedure if unable to comply with an ATC clearance or instruction.","application of necessary adjustments to the published MDA and visibility criteria for the aircraft approach category.","establishment and maintenance of an appropriate rate of descent during the final approach segment.","factors that should be considered in determining whether: (1) the approach should be continued straight-in to a landing; or (2) a circling approach to a landing should be performed."]},{"t":"Exhibits instructional knowledge of common errors related to an instrument approach with one engine inoperative by describing—","sub":["failure to have essential knowledge of the information that appears on the selected instrument approach chart.","failure to use proper communications procedures.","noncompliance with ATC clearances.","incorrect use of navigation equipment.","failure to identify and verify the inoperative engine and to follow the emergency checklist.","inappropriate procedure in the adjustment of engine controls and the reduction of drag.","inappropriate procedure in the establishment and maintenance of the best engine inoperative airspeed.","failure to establish and maintain the proper flight attitude for best performance.","failure to maintain positive aircraft control.","faulty basic instrument flying technique.","inappropriate descent below the MDA or DH.","faulty technique during roundout and touchdown."]},{"t":"Demonstrates and simultaneously explains an instrument approach with one engine inoperative from an instructional standpoint.","sub":[]},{"t":"Analyzes and corrects simulated common errors related to an instrument approach with one engine inoperative.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"The learner can safely fly a single-engine precision and non-precision approach and landing meeting ACS/PTS standards.","key":["Zero Sideslip","Never < VYSE, Never Ever < VMC","Normal Landing"],"cards":[{"h":"Managing the failure on approach","obj":"Control first, then the approach.","pts":["Instrument privileges on a multi-engine rating require flying an approach with a failed engine","Recognize (yaw/roll toward dead engine), zero sideslip (1-3° bank, ½ ball toward good engine)","Full Power, Reduce Drag, Identify, Verify, Fix or Feather; restart if possible","Check control between every step - aviate, navigate, communicate"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"db2383c3b73c60ae.webp","w":243,"h":224,"cap":"Managing an Engine Failure"}]},{"h":"Maintaining course, altitude, airspeed","obj":"Accelerated scan while holding zero sideslip.","pts":["Establish, trim, crosscheck, adjust; include the approach chart in the scan","Always ask 'What am I doing next?'; 5 T's at every waypoint","Course: bug the heading that holds course for wind, correct either side of the bug","Glideslope: fix ball movement immediately; higher groundspeed = higher descent rate","**>1,000 fpm** in the final stages is unacceptable (human perception limits); be stabilized to reach DA with a TDZ landing"],"ce":""},{"h":"Choosing an approach","obj":"Pick the simplest, safest approach within single-engine capability.","pts":["Consider ATIS, airplane capability (incl. SE performance), weather (ceilings vs minimums)","**Straight-in** highly preferred SE: lower minimums, less maneuvering and power changes","**Circle** SE only if absolutely necessary - level-offs, power changes, complexity; use correct minimums","Personal: fly what you're competent with; lateral + vertical guidance is safer","Verify the chart and runway (ex. LOC 25L vs 25R), especially after a change"],"ce":""},{"h":"Approach setup and briefing","obj":"Normal brief plus single-engine items.","pts":["**TIM**: Tune, Identify, Monitor","**FFIIMMMMS**: Fixes, Freq, Inbound course, Identify, Marker beacons, Minutes, Minimums, Missed, Security","Speeds and configuration change; the approach itself stays the same","Brief single-engine go-around; as power changes, rudder changes with it"],"ce":""},{"h":"SE precision approach configuration","obj":"Example profile - adjust to the POH.","pts":["PT / LOC intercept: **100 KIAS, 80%**","½ dot below GS: decelerate to **90 KIAS, 65%**, flaps as necessary","GS intercept (FAF): SE approach speed (no slower than **VYSE**), ~**65%**, ~**5° nose down**"],"ce":""},{"h":"SE non-precision configuration","obj":"Example profile - adjust to the POH.","pts":["PT / LOC intercept: **100 KIAS, 80%**","1 NM from FAF: decelerate to **90 KIAS, 65%**, flaps as necessary","FAF: no slower than VYSE, ~**55%**, ~**6° nose down** - higher descent rate to reach MDA before the MAP","MDA/step-down: level off without going below; ~**80%** power, not below VYSE; more power = more rudder"],"ce":""},{"h":"Checklists","obj":"Flows used on the SE approach.","pts":["Any fix: **5 T's** - Turn, Time, Twist, Throttle, Talk","Pre-landing **LBBGUMPFS**: Landing gear, Boost pump, Brakes (off), Gas, Undercarriage, Mixture, Prop, Flaps, Safety","Inside OM/FAF: **Reds, Blues, Greens, Whites, Power** - mixture, prop, engine gauges, lights, power set"],"ce":""},{"h":"SE landing","obj":"Basically a normal landing - manage rudder as power comes off.","pts":["Once visual, attention mostly outside, still crosscheck; keep zero sideslip, course, GS","Avoid 'look up, fly up' - hold constant pitch on the glideslope; transition to VASI/PAPI","Roundout/flare: power to idle and **reduce rudder** with it","Otherwise the airplane yaws toward the rudder pressure, since the thrust yaw it countered is gone"],"ce":""},{"h":"ATC instructions and PIC authority","obj":"Say 'unable' when needed.","pts":["In an emergency an ATC clearance or instruction may not be feasible","Pilot must tell ATC **'unable'** - don't put yourself in unnecessary danger; state intentions and needs","**FAR 91.3**: PIC is responsible for the operation of the aircraft; in an in-flight emergency may deviate from any rule as necessary"],"ce":"CE: inappropriate descent below MDA or DH; noncompliance with clearances; faulty roundout and touchdown","figs":[{"f":"dc0f63a15460c4d8.webp","w":720,"h":193,"cap":"Common Errors:"}]}],"recall":[["Maximum acceptable descent rate in the final stages of an approach and why?","1,000 fpm - greater is unacceptable due to human perceptual limitations, regardless of airplane type"],["Straight-in vs circling on one engine?","Straight-in highly preferred: lower minimums, less maneuvering and fewer power changes\nCircle only if absolutely necessary"],["SE precision vs non-precision at the FAF: power and pitch?","Precision: ~65%, ~5° nose down\nNon-precision: ~55%, ~6° nose down (to reach MDA before MAP)\nBoth: no slower than VYSE"],["Power at MDA / step-down level-off single engine?","Approximately 80%, do not slow below VYSE, add rudder to keep zero sideslip"],["Why must rudder be reduced as power comes to idle in a SE landing?","Rudder was countering the operating engine's thrust yaw; when thrust is removed, held rudder yaws the airplane toward the rudder pressure"],["Key speed rule for a single-engine approach?","Never below VYSE, never ever below VMC"]]},"cfii-x-a-postflight":{"req":true,"ofigs":{},"pts":{"task":"X.A CHECKING INSTRUMENTS AND EQUIPMENT","area":"","note":"","obj":"To determine that the applicant exhibits instructional knowledge of the elements related to checking instruments and equipment by describing:","items":[{"t":"Importance of noting instruments and navigation equipment for improper operation.","sub":[]},{"t":"Reasons for making a written record of improper operation or failure and/or calibration of instruments prior to next IFR flight.","sub":[]}]},"intro":"The learner understands how to check instruments and equipment for proper operation and when to make a record of improper operation.","key":[],"cards":[{"h":"Postflight check","obj":"Recognize and record any faulty instruments.","pts":["Extremely important to recognize and record faulty instruments - especially for IMC flight","Check all instruments **and their power sources** for proper operation","Note equipment not functioning properly; record and report the malfunctions","Why: the next pilot is made aware and maintenance can be done before the next flight"],"ce":""},{"h":"Documenting malfunctions","obj":"Use the maintenance log.","pts":["Document all malfunctions in a **maintenance log** - one history anyone can access","Methods vary by school or owner","Alerts other pilots before flight; shows trends and what has or hasn't been fixed","Critical where many pilots share aircraft (flight schools); unreported issues pass to the next pilot"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["Why record instrument malfunctions after the flight?","Alerts the next pilot, shows trends and what's been fixed, gets the issue to maintenance before the next flight - safety"]]},"cfii-xi-a-energy-mgmt":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"3-1":[{"f":"c1efb86d35b36d69.webp","w":490,"h":331,"cap":"Energy Balance & Control"}],"3-2":[{"f":"24aa3f008c4019cf.webp","w":709,"h":204,"cap":"Energy Balance & Control"}],"4-1-2":[{"f":"e08e93780a8175e9.webp","w":313,"h":222,"cap":"Pilot Control of Energy"}],"4-1-4":[{"f":"ba1486200b8c3a99.webp","w":308,"h":248,"cap":"Advanced Elevator Concepts"},{"f":"4ece6bd91e56694f.webp","w":305,"h":247,"cap":"Advanced Elevator Concepts"}],"5-5-1":[{"f":"630525bb72d164b9.webp","w":14,"h":0,"cap":"Unintended Altitude-Airspeed Deviations      Energy Errors"},{"f":"d0729ab91f1768d2.webp","w":406,"h":413,"cap":"Unintended Altitude-Airspeed Deviations      Energy Errors"},{"f":"0ea22791039a415a.webp","w":293,"h":248,"cap":"Example"}],"5-5-2":[{"f":"90ab14b149d9aced.webp","w":326,"h":237,"cap":"Unrecoverable depletion of energy"}]},"pts":null,"intro":"Controlling altitude and airspeed to maintain safe and efficient flight (Airplane Flying Handbook Ch. 4).","key":[],"cards":[{"h":"What and why","obj":"Energy management prevents LOC-I, CFIT, and approach/landing accidents.","pts":["**Energy management**: controlling altitude and airspeed for safe, efficient flight","Prevents loss of control in flight (**LOC-I**), **CFIT**, approach/landing accidents","Needed to hold vertical paths and airspeed profiles and transition between them (final, constant-speed climbs, level-offs)"],"ce":""},{"h":"The airplane as an energy system","obj":"Total energy = potential (altitude) + kinetic (airspeed).","pts":["An airplane in flight is an **open** energy system: gains energy by thrust, loses it to drag","Energy can be stored as airspeed or altitude","T > D: total energy increases; T < D: decreases (descent or deceleration)","T = D: energy constant - any speed/altitude change is a trade","Energy balance equation: thrust-drag (left) always matches altitude/airspeed change (right)"],"ce":""},{"h":"Pilot control of energy","obj":"Throttle sets total energy; elevator distributes it.","pts":["**Throttle** = total energy controller (left side of the equation); changes normally initiated with thrust, not drag","**Elevator** = energy distribution controller between altitude and airspeed (right side)","Reservoir analogy: throttle valve regulates net flow; elevator valve regulates flow between altitude and airspeed reservoirs","Neither control alone sets altitude or speed - **Pitch + Power controls Energy State**"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"c1efb86d35b36d69.webp","w":490,"h":331,"cap":"Energy Balance & Control"},{"f":"24aa3f008c4019cf.webp","w":709,"h":204,"cap":"Energy Balance & Control"},{"f":"e08e93780a8175e9.webp","w":313,"h":222,"cap":"Pilot Control of Energy"}]},{"h":"Advanced elevator concepts","obj":"Elevator effects on each side of the power curve.","pts":["Front side (high speed/low AOA): pull back = brief energy-exchange climb, slower speed, less drag","Back side (low speed/high AOA): push forward = brief energy-exchange descent, faster speed, less drag","With excess power, can climb at constant airspeed or turn level","In a level turn, back pressure raises AOA and load factor - more drag and power required; energy drops faster than throttle alone"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"ba1486200b8c3a99.webp","w":308,"h":248,"cap":"Advanced Elevator Concepts"},{"f":"4ece6bd91e56694f.webp","w":305,"h":247,"cap":"Advanced Elevator Concepts"}]},{"h":"3 rules of energy control","obj":"Coordinate throttle and elevator.","pts":["**Increase total energy**: add throttle; pitch up to climb at constant speed, or pitch down gradually to accelerate level","**Decrease total energy**: reduce throttle; pitch down just enough to descend at constant speed","**Exchange energy**: pitch up to trade speed for altitude, pitch down to trade altitude for speed","At the new state, adjust pitch and throttle to hold the new speed-path profile"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"630525bb72d164b9.webp","w":14,"h":0,"cap":"Unintended Altitude-Airspeed Deviations      Energy Errors"},{"f":"d0729ab91f1768d2.webp","w":406,"h":413,"cap":"Unintended Altitude-Airspeed Deviations      Energy Errors"},{"f":"0ea22791039a415a.webp","w":293,"h":248,"cap":"Example"}]},{"h":"Energy errors","obj":"Diagnose total vs distribution errors.","pts":["**Total energy error**: altitude and airspeed deviate the **same** way (low & slow, high & fast) - fix with **throttle**","**Distribution error**: deviate in **opposite** ways (high & slow, low & fast) - fix with **elevator**","Combined errors: throttle and elevator together","Ex. below glidepath: crosscheck airspeed - low & slow, low & fast, or low & on-speed each need a different fix"],"ce":""},{"h":"Irreversible deceleration / sink rate","obj":"Recognize unrecoverable energy depletion.","pts":["Loss of airspeed/energy + insufficient power available; excess power is required to climb","Below a certain altitude, failure to recover means hitting the ground regardless","Ex. high, hot departure toward terrain: pitching up slows the plane until power available = required, climb stops; slower still, required > available","Recovery: **lower the nose and gain speed (VX)** at the expense of altitude and/or turn around","Also: slow/unstable approach, high-drag go-around, steep pattern turns; at high speed - steep spiral, dives near the ground"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"90ab14b149d9aced.webp","w":326,"h":237,"cap":"Unrecoverable depletion of energy"}]}],"recall":[["What is total mechanical energy, and what happens when T - D = 0?","Potential (altitude) + kinetic (airspeed)\nT = D: total energy constant; changes in speed/altitude come only from trading one for the other"],["Which control manages total energy and which distributes it?","Throttle: total energy controller\nElevator: distribution between altitude and airspeed"],["Three rules of energy control?","Increase total energy (throttle up, pitch to distribute)\nDecrease total energy (throttle down, pitch to distribute)\nExchange energy (pitch only)"],["High and slow on approach - total energy or distribution error, and which control fixes it?","Distribution error (opposite directions)\nCorrect by exchanging altitude and airspeed with the elevator"],["Low and slow - what type of error?","Total energy error (too little energy) - correct with throttle"],["Recovery from an irreversible deceleration on a hot, high departure toward terrain?","Lower the nose and gain speed (VX) at the expense of altitude and/or turn around"]]},"cfii-xi-b-adm-crm-srm":{"req":false,"ofigs":{"5":[{"f":"80cadc895b69a85c.webp","w":519,"h":505,"cap":"Evaluation"}],"2-4":[{"f":"604491bf5052e06d.webp","w":381,"h":203,"cap":"Step 3: Mitigate the Risk"}],"4-3-2":[{"f":"12f80c4974a4c2a8.webp","w":655,"h":750,"cap":"Decision Making Process"}]},"pts":null,"intro":"The learner understands ADM, CRM, and risk management and can apply them to all future flights.","key":["Accept no unnecessary risk","PAVE Checklist","DECIDE Model"],"cards":[{"h":"Aeronautical Decision Making","obj":"A systematic mental process to consistently determine the best course of action.","pts":["Sound decisions are the key to preventing accidents; ~**80%** of accidents are human factors","Steps: identify hazardous attitudes and behavior modification techniques","Recognize and cope with stress; develop risk assessment skills","Use all resources; evaluate the effectiveness of your ADM skills"],"ce":""},{"h":"4 principles of risk management","obj":"Proactively identify hazards and mitigate risks.","pts":["**Accept no unnecessary risk** - don't make it more dangerous than necessary","**Make risk decisions at the appropriate level** - single pilot: the pilot decides; otherwise 'go up the ladder' (chief pilot, experienced CFI)","**Accept risk when benefits outweigh costs**","**Integrate risk management into planning at all levels**"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"604491bf5052e06d.webp","w":381,"h":203,"cap":"Step 3: Mitigate the Risk"}]},{"h":"Risk management process","obj":"Identify, assess, mitigate.","pts":["**1. Identify the hazard**: any real or potential condition that can cause degradation, injury, illness, death, damage or loss","**2. Assess the risk**: likelihood (probability) and severity (consequences)","**3. Mitigate**: reduce or eliminate at least one component; cost/benefit to decide","Mitigation ex.: cancel/delay, bring a CFI or more experienced pilot"],"ce":""},{"h":"Risk assessment matrix","obj":"Tangibly measure risk.","pts":["Differentiate a low-risk from a high-risk flight in advance","Likelihood: **Probable, Occasional, Remote, Improbable**","Severity: **Catastrophic, Critical, Marginal, Negligible**","Ex.: likelihood a pilot flying MVFR meets IFR; severity if not IFR rated"],"ce":""},{"h":"PAVE checklist","obj":"Assess risk by category; if unmanageable, cancel.","pts":["**P**ilot: Am I ready? IMSAFE, proficiency, recency, currency","**A**ircraft: maintenance, landing distance, performance, equipment, fuel, altitude","En**V**ironment: weather, terrain, airports, airspace, day/night","**E**xternal pressures: can make a pilot ignore all other risk factors - follow personal minimums, plan for delays, manage passenger expectations"],"ce":""},{"h":"SRM / CRM and resources","obj":"Use all resources.","pts":["How to gather information, analyze it, decide; team management applied to the flight deck","Includes pilots, dispatchers, cabin crew, maintenance, ATC","**Internal**: equipment, systems, charts, books, ingenuity/knowledge/skill, passengers (even non-pilots)","**External**: ATC and flight service - traffic, vectors, weather, emergency help","**Workload**: plan, prioritize, sequence; prepare before high workload (brief the approach early); when behind, do tasks in order of importance"],"ce":""},{"h":"5 P's check","obj":"Evaluate the situation at key points, or when an emergency arises.","pts":["**Plan, Plane, Pilot, Passengers, Programming**","At least 5 times: preflight, pre-takeoff, hourly or midpoint, pre-descent, FAF or entering the pattern","Pilot = IMSAFE; Passengers - involve them, they may be more risk averse than you","Programming: plan when and where to program automation, based on familiarity and capability"],"ce":""},{"h":"Decision making process","obj":"Analytical vs automatic decision making.","pts":["**DECIDE**: Detect, Estimate, Choose, Identify, Do, Evaluate","Analytical (DECIDE, 5 P's): gather, review, analyze, rate, select, evaluate - not always possible","**Automatic/naturalized**: reflexive, anchored in training and experience; take the first workable option - needed in emergencies","**Operational pitfalls** come with experience: completing as planned, pleasing passengers, meeting schedules - eliminate them"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"12f80c4974a4c2a8.webp","w":655,"h":750,"cap":"Decision Making Process"}]},{"h":"Evaluation","obj":"Review and debrief every flight.","pts":["What went well? What could have gone better? What will I (we) do next time?","Areas: planning & decision making, leadership, situational awareness, communication","Monitor/cross-check, workload management, automation management"],"ce":"","figs":[{"f":"80cadc895b69a85c.webp","w":519,"h":505,"cap":"Evaluation"}]}],"recall":[["Roughly what share of aviation accidents result from human factors?","Approximately 80%"],["Four fundamental principles of risk management?","Accept no unnecessary risk\nMake risk decisions at the appropriate level\nAccept risk when benefits outweigh costs\nIntegrate RM into planning at all levels"],["Hazard vs risk, and the two components of risk?","Hazard: real or potential condition that can cause harm or loss\nRisk components: likelihood (probability) and severity"],["Which PAVE element is most dangerous and why?","External pressures - the one factor that can cause a pilot to ignore all other risk factors"],["5 P's, and when do you run them?","Plan, Plane, Pilot, Passengers, Programming\nPreflight, pre-takeoff, hourly/midpoint, pre-descent, FAF or entering pattern"],["What does DECIDE stand for?","Detect, Estimate, Choose, Identify, Do, Evaluate"]]},"cfii-xi-c-ipc":{"req":false,"ofigs":{},"pts":null,"intro":"How to conduct, structure, and endorse an IPC under 61.57(d).","key":[],"cards":[{"h":"Currency and the IPC","obj":"When an IPC is required.","pts":["**61.57(c)**: within the preceding **6 calendar months**: **6 instrument approaches, holding procedures & tasks, intercepting & tracking courses**","**61.57(d)**: a pilot who failed to meet these for **more than 6 calendar months** reestablishes currency with an IPC","IPC areas: ATC clearances & procedures, flight by reference to instruments, navigation systems, approach procedures, emergency operations, postflight","Instructor must hold an **instrument rating on the flight instructor certificate** appropriate to the category and class"],"ce":""},{"h":"IPC ACS tasks","obj":"Instrument ACS areas required in an IPC.","pts":["Area I none; II none; III **B**; IV **B**; V **A**; VI **All**; VII **B, C, D**; VIII **All**","Minimum flight tasks: holding, unusual attitude recovery, intercept/track nav systems & arcs","Non-precision, precision, missed, circling approaches, landing from an approach","One engine inop flight and approach (AMEL), loss of primary flight instruments, checking instruments & equipment"],"ce":""},{"h":"Structuring the IPC","obj":"Tailor it to the pilot.","pts":["Talk to the pilot: type of flying, amount and recency of experience, weak topics","An out-and-back cross-country is excellent: one leg XC procedures, one leg aircraft control","Agree on the plan and the criteria for satisfactory completion (ex. ACS standards)","Some ground review can be gauged or done in flight"],"ce":""},{"h":"Sample IPC","obj":"Two-leg plan from the eBook.","pts":["Prep: FAA IPC Prep Course; IFR flight plan with an alternate","**Leg 1**: departure procedure, divert enroute, non-precision to a missed and hold, precision to landing","**Leg 2**: aircraft control - steep turns, slow flight/stalls, unusual attitudes, partial panel; partial-panel non-precision, then precision to landing","Include one missed and hold, one circling, one partial-panel approach; adjust to the aircraft's nav capability"],"ce":""},{"h":"Ground review topics (AC 61-98)","obj":"Regs to cover on the ground.","pts":["Pilot: 61.57, 91.3, 91.123, 91.103, fitness (AIM 8-1)","Aircraft: 91.167 fuel, 91.171 VOR check, 91.185 lost comm, 91.187 malfunction reports, 91.205, 91.207 ELT, 91.209, 91.213, 91.411, 91.413","Environment: 91.169, 91.173, 91.175 (incl. 91.175(j) PT), 91.177, 91.179, 91.181, 91.183, PIREPs (AIM 5-3-2, 5-3-3), AIM Ch. 1/4/5, TPP symbols","External pressure: emergency procedures, national security/intercept (AIM Ch. 5, 6)"],"ce":""},{"h":"Post-flight review and endorsement","obj":"Debrief and endorse.","pts":["Satisfactory or not, give a comprehensive analysis including ways to improve","If unsatisfactory, log as **dual instruction given, not a failure** (not a checkride); offer a course of action","Endorsement **AC 61-65 A.71**: name, grade of certificate, certificate number, satisfactorily completed the IPC of **§61.57(d)** in a [make and model] on [date]"],"ce":""}],"recall":[["61.57(c) instrument experience requirements?","Within the preceding 6 calendar months:\n6 instrument approaches\nHolding procedures & tasks\nIntercepting & tracking courses"],["Who may give an IPC?","An instructor with an instrument rating on their flight instructor certificate appropriate to the category and class of aircraft used"],["Which approaches are required minimum tasks in an IPC?","Non-precision, precision, missed, circling, and landing from an approach"],["An IPC is unsatisfactory. How do you log it?","As dual instruction given, not a failure - it's not a checkride\nDebrief and offer a practical course of action"],["Which AC endorsement is used for an IPC, and what does it include?","AC 61-65 A.71: name, grade of pilot certificate, certificate number, completed the IPC of 61.57(d), make and model, date"]]}};
